searchData={"items":[{"type":"module","title":"Anemos","doc":"Anemos — event-driven automation DSL.\n\nProvides `run/2` to parse, compile, and dispatch a rule source string\nagainst a given event in a single call.","ref":"Anemos.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.report/0","doc":"What a single `run/2` actually did, beyond the operations it produced.\n\n`:subscribed` and `:reachable` exist because an empty result list has\nthree unrelated causes, and a caller that cannot tell them apart cannot\nact on any of them: nothing subscribed to the event, something subscribed\nbut the context boundary denied it, or a rule ran and every branch was\nfalse. See `run_report/2`.","ref":"Anemos.html#t:report/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.default_modules/0","doc":"The default module registrations used by `run/2` and `run_report/2`.\n\nEvery one of these is a development stub: it computes in memory and\nperforms no I/O. They exist so the DSL and the shipped examples can be\nexercised, not so a rule file can manage real infrastructure.","ref":"Anemos.html#default_modules/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.example_modules/0","doc":"Fake module registrations that let `priv/examples/` run end to end.\n\nThese are demonstration doubles, not implementations: they compute from\ntheir arguments and the supplied context data and touch nothing outside\nthe VM. Pass them explicitly (`modules: Anemos.example_modules()`, or\n`mix anemos.run --stub-modules`) — they are not registered by default,\nbecause `SECRET` and `INFRA` answering plausibly is exactly the failure a\npolicy author needs to see rather than sail past.","ref":"Anemos.html#example_modules/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.run/2","doc":"Parse, compile, and dispatch an event against rules in `source`.\n\nOptions:\n  - `:event` — the event name to dispatch (required)\n  - `:context` — optional raw runtime context map (default `%{}`)\n  - `:context_path` — the context the caller occupies, for boundary\n    matching against each rule's `context` block\n  - `:context_data` — external key-value data that `?var` resolves against\n  - `:modules` — extra module registrations, merged over `default_modules/0`\n  - `:authorization` — passed through to the configured\n    `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority` as `LoadRequest.authorization`; a token,\n    a peer identity, whatever the authority was written to check\n\n`:context_path` and `:context_data` are merged into `:context`; they are a\nconvenience for callers that would otherwise hand-build the map. They are\ntwo different things: the first is the scope the caller is standing in,\nthe second is the data it brought with it.","ref":"Anemos.html#run/2"},{"type":"function","title":"The load is gated - Anemos.run/2","doc":"`source` goes through the same trust boundary as\n`Anemos.Runtime.load/3`: the unwaivable `:max_policy_bytes` ceiling, then\nthe module configured as `config :anemos, :load_authority` (A-59). This\nis not a developer shortcut around the boundary — a host embedding this\nto execute policy it did not write gets the gate it configured, here as\nwell as there.\n\nA refusal is `{:error, %Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct{}}` with code\n`anemos.runtime/load_denied`. The default authority is\n`LoadAuthority.Permissive`, so an embedder that has configured nothing\nsees no change.\n\nReturns `{:ok, results}` or `{:error, reason}`. Use `run_report/2` when an\nempty result list needs to be distinguished from a boundary denial.","ref":"Anemos.html#run/2-the-load-is-gated"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.run/2","doc":"iex> source = \"rule t { when EV { [DEVICE::set \\\"light\\\" \\\"on\\\"] } }\"\n    iex> {:ok, [%{module: \"DEVICE\", verb: \"set\", args: [\"light\", \"on\"]}]} =\n    ...>   Anemos.run(source, event: \"EV\")\n\n    iex> source = \"rule t { context \\\"garage\\\" { when EV { log \\\"hi\\\" } } }\"\n    iex> {:ok, []} = Anemos.run(source, event: \"EV\")\n    iex> {:ok, [_]} = Anemos.run(source, event: \"EV\", context_path: \"garage\")\n\n    iex> {:error, _} = Anemos.run(\"rule {\", event: \"EV\")","ref":"Anemos.html#run/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.run_report/2","doc":"Like `run/2`, but returns a `t:report/0` instead of a bare result list.","ref":"Anemos.html#run_report/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.run_report/2","doc":"iex> source = \"rule t { context \\\"garage\\\" { when EV { log \\\"hi\\\" } } }\"\n    iex> {:ok, denied} = Anemos.run_report(source, event: \"EV\")\n    iex> {denied.subscribed, denied.reachable, denied.operations}\n    {1, 0, []}\n\n    iex> source = \"rule t { when EV { log \\\"hi\\\" } }\"\n    iex> {:ok, silent} = Anemos.run_report(source, event: \"OTHER\")\n    iex> {silent.subscribed, silent.reachable}\n    {0, 0}","ref":"Anemos.html#run_report/2-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST","doc":"AST node structs for the Anemos DSL.\n\nEach struct represents a node in the abstract syntax tree produced by `Anemos.Parser`.\nAll nodes carry a `:line` field for error reporting.","ref":"Anemos.AST.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.At","doc":"A time-triggered `at` block with a time spec, optional guard, and body.","ref":"Anemos.AST.At.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.At.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.At.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.BinaryOp","doc":"A binary operator expression.","ref":"Anemos.AST.BinaryOp.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.BinaryOp.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.BinaryOp.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.BlockArgs","doc":"A block argument list of key-value pairs.","ref":"Anemos.AST.BlockArgs.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.BlockArgs.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.BlockArgs.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.BuiltinCall","doc":"A built-in function call (filter, map, reduce, emit).","ref":"Anemos.AST.BuiltinCall.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.BuiltinCall.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.BuiltinCall.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Capability","doc":"A named capability declaration with context, body, and source line.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Capability.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Capability.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Capability.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Command","doc":"A module command expression with a verb and arguments.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Command.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Command.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Command.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Condition","doc":"A named condition declaration with a body and source line.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Condition.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Condition.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Condition.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Context","doc":"A context scope block with a dotted path and body.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Context.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Context.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Context.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.DataDecl","doc":"A named data declaration with content, optional source/transclude, scope, and source line.","ref":"Anemos.AST.DataDecl.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.DataDecl.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.DataDecl.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Demand","doc":"A `demand` expression referencing a capability with a PAM-style control flag.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Demand.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Demand.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Demand.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Done","doc":"A `done` statement: exits the current handler with no value.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Done.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Done.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Done.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Emit","doc":"An `emit` expression that produces a typed value to an optional destination.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Emit.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Emit.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Emit.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Flow","doc":"A named flow declaration with a body and source line.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Flow.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Flow.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Flow.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.For","doc":"A `for` loop: iterates `var` over `iterable`, executing body each time. Covers `for`, `each`, `every`, `foreach`.","ref":"Anemos.AST.For.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.For.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.For.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.If","doc":"A conditional `if` block with an optional guard, body, and else branch.","ref":"Anemos.AST.If.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.If.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.If.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Introspect","doc":"An introspection statement — `show`, `list`, `graph`, `trace`,\n`explain`, `exists`, `infer`, `assert` — or `exists` in expression\nposition. The subject is a bare `{:name, \"NAME\"}`, a `{:catalogue,\n:rules | :conditions | :modules | :events | :policies | :schedules}`, or\n`{:expr, expression}`.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Introspect.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Introspect.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Introspect.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.It","doc":"An `it` modal reference block.","ref":"Anemos.AST.It.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.It.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.It.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Let","doc":"A `let` variable binding.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Let.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Let.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Let.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.ListLiteral","doc":"A list literal expression.","ref":"Anemos.AST.ListLiteral.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.ListLiteral.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.ListLiteral.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Literal","doc":"A literal value node (string, integer, float, boolean, atom).","ref":"Anemos.AST.Literal.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Literal.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Literal.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Log","doc":"A `log` expression.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Log.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Log.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Log.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.NextEvent","doc":"A `nextevent` statement: transitions to another `when` block within the\nsame rule, optionally carrying a `with { }` argument block (A-55).","ref":"Anemos.AST.NextEvent.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.NextEvent.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.NextEvent.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.NextRule","doc":"A `nextrule` statement: dispatches to a different rule's pipeline,\noptionally carrying a `with { }` argument block.","ref":"Anemos.AST.NextRule.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.NextRule.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.NextRule.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.On","doc":"An `on` invocation block triggered by a named invocation.","ref":"Anemos.AST.On.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.On.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.On.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Program","doc":"Top-level program node wrapping all parsed declarations.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Program.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Program.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Program.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.QueryDecl","doc":"A named query declaration with expect/engine/body/return, scope, and source line.","ref":"Anemos.AST.QueryDecl.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.QueryDecl.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.QueryDecl.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Return","doc":"A `return` statement: exits the current handler, optionally with a value.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Return.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Return.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Return.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Rule","doc":"A named rule declaration with a body, optional metadata, and source line.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Rule.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Rule.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Rule.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Set","doc":"A `set` variable assignment.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Set.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Set.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Set.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.SignedBy","doc":"A source-level `signed_by` declaration (A-60).\n\nA detached signature covers the whole source, so provenance is a property\nof the file rather than of any one declaration in it. This is the placement\nthat says so, and the only one under which a source carrying no `rule` —\nconditions, capabilities, data blocks — can make a provenance claim at all.","ref":"Anemos.AST.SignedBy.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.SignedBy.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.SignedBy.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.Triple","doc":"An RDF-style triple with subject, predicate, and object.","ref":"Anemos.AST.Triple.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.Triple.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.Triple.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.UnaryOp","doc":"A unary operator expression.","ref":"Anemos.AST.UnaryOp.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.UnaryOp.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.UnaryOp.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.VarRef","doc":"A variable reference with an optional sigil prefix.","ref":"Anemos.AST.VarRef.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.VarRef.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.VarRef.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.When","doc":"A `when` trigger block: fires on an event, with optional duration, guard, and body.","ref":"Anemos.AST.When.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.When.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.When.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.AST.While","doc":"A `while` loop: repeats body while condition is truthy.","ref":"Anemos.AST.While.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.AST.While.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.AST.While.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Application","doc":"OTP application callback for Anemos. Starts no interpreter.\n\nAnemos is a *library* application. Loading it does not bring up an\ninterpreter, because there is nothing to bring up: the interpreter starts\nblank and every rule, module, condition and capability is loaded into it at\nruntime. There is no startup configuration to read and therefore no\nsupervision tree this callback could construct without inventing policy.\n\nThe supervision tree is host-started. A host that wants an interpreter calls\n`Anemos.Runtime.start_link/1` and places the returned supervisor under its\nown tree, where it can choose the restart strategy and the process names.\nMultiple independent runtimes in one VM are a supported consequence of that\nchoice. To read what the system actually runs, read `Anemos.Runtime` — not\nthis module.\n\nWhat this callback does do is one-shot: it forces `Anemos.Runtime.Errors` to\nload and re-runs `Comn` discovery, so the `Comn` registries see the Anemos\nerror definitions and discoverable modules that were not yet loaded when\n`:comn` itself booted. These are idempotent side effects with no process to\nown them, so they are not children.\n\n`Anemos.Supervisor` is started childless to satisfy the `Application.start/2`\ncontract, which must return a supervisor pid. It is a placeholder for\nprocess-less application state, not an empty slot waiting to be filled.","ref":"Anemos.Application.html"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Anemos.Channel","doc":"Behaviour for channel connectors — the bridge between Anemos emit\noperations and the host app's messaging infrastructure.\n\nImplement this behaviour to route emitted messages through your\nsystem's event bus, NATS, AMQP, or any other messaging layer.","ref":"Anemos.Channel.html"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Built-in connectors - Anemos.Channel","doc":"* `Anemos.Channel.ComnBus` — publishes via `Comn.EventBus` (Registry-based pub/sub)\n  * `Anemos.Channel.Logger` — logs emits via `Logger.info/1` (default)","ref":"Anemos.Channel.html#module-built-in-connectors"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Usage - Anemos.Channel","doc":"# Use the built-in Comn.EventBus connector:\n    Anemos.Runtime.connect(:anemos, Anemos.Channel.ComnBus)\n\n    # Or implement your own (see Anemos.Channel.Logger for a minimal example):\n    # @behaviour Anemos.Channel\n    # @impl true\n    # def publish(channel, envelope), do: ...\n    Anemos.Runtime.connect(:anemos, MyApp.NatsChannel)","ref":"Anemos.Channel.html#module-usage"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Default - Anemos.Channel","doc":"If no connector is registered, `Anemos.Channel.Logger` is used —\nit logs emits via `Logger.info/1` so they're never silent.","ref":"Anemos.Channel.html#module-default"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.Channel.publish/2","doc":"Publish a message envelope to a named channel. Called when a rule executes `emit [X] to channel(path)`.","ref":"Anemos.Channel.html#c:publish/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Channel.ComnBus","doc":"Channel connector that publishes envelopes via `Comn.EventBus`.\n\nSubscribers receive `{:event, channel_path, %Anemos.Envelope{}}` messages.\nRequires `Comn.Supervisor` (or a Registry named `Comn.EventBus`) in the\nsupervision tree.","ref":"Anemos.Channel.ComnBus.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Usage - Anemos.Channel.ComnBus","doc":"Anemos.Runtime.connect(:anemos, Anemos.Channel.ComnBus)\n\n    # In a subscriber process:\n    Comn.EventBus.subscribe(\"sensors/temperature\")\n    receive do\n      {:event, _topic, %Anemos.Envelope{} = env} -> handle(env)\n    end","ref":"Anemos.Channel.ComnBus.html#module-usage"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Channel.Logger","doc":"Default channel connector that logs emitted messages via `Logger.info/1`.\n\nUsed when no connector is explicitly registered. Ensures emits to\nchannels are never silent — they always produce observable output.","ref":"Anemos.Channel.Logger.html"},{"type":"module","title":"What is logged - Anemos.Channel.Logger","doc":"Routing metadata only: channel, envelope type, and label. The payload is\ndescribed by shape (`binary(23 bytes)`, `map(2 keys)`) via\n`Anemos.Redact.summarize/1`, never by content.\n\nThis connector is the default, so it runs in any deployment that has not\nregistered its own. The spec's own `SECRET::unlock` example obtains a\ncredential by emitting it; logging payload contents at `:info` would write\nthat credential to the application log and onward to wherever logs ship.\n\"Never silent\" is not the same as \"fully disclosed\". A connector that needs\nthe payload should be written deliberately — see `Anemos.Channel`.","ref":"Anemos.Channel.Logger.html#module-what-is-logged"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Channel.Logger.publish/2","doc":"Logs the envelope's routing metadata and payload shape to the Elixir Logger.","ref":"Anemos.Channel.Logger.html#publish/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.CompilationResult","doc":"Result of compiling an Anemos program.\n\nAll fields default to empty lists — no fields are required at construction.","ref":"Anemos.CompilationResult.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.CompilationResult.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.CompilationResult.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.CompiledCapability","doc":"A compiled capability: name, context scope, and handler closure for on-demand execution.","ref":"Anemos.CompiledCapability.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.CompiledCapability.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.CompiledCapability.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.CompiledCondition","doc":"A compiled condition: name, trigger subscriptions, evaluator function, and side effects.","ref":"Anemos.CompiledCondition.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.CompiledCondition.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.CompiledCondition.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.CompiledData","doc":"A compiled data block.","ref":"Anemos.CompiledData.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.CompiledData.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.CompiledData.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.CompiledQuery","doc":"A compiled query block.","ref":"Anemos.CompiledQuery.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.CompiledQuery.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.CompiledQuery.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.CompiledRule","doc":"A compiled rule: name, event subscriptions, handler closure, and optional dwell duration.\n\nThe handler takes `(event_name, context, persistent_bindings)` and returns\n`{:ok, [operation], updated_persistent_bindings}`. Persistent bindings hold\n`set` values that survive across firings.\n\nWhen `dwell_duration_ms` is non-nil, the rule is dispatched through the\nDispatcher's dwell-timer state machine instead of being fired immediately.\nSee `Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher` for the state-machine semantics.\n\n`trigger` is non-nil for a compound `when (A AND B)` / `when (A OR B)`:\nthe operator, the operand events, and the index of the `when` block within\nits rule, which keys the reports the dispatcher keeps for it. See\n`Anemos.Runtime.Report`.","ref":"Anemos.CompiledRule.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.CompiledRule.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.CompiledRule.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.CompiledSchedule","doc":"A compiled schedule: event name, time spec, recurrence, and handler.\n\nProduced by the compiler from `at` blocks. The scheduler uses these\nto set timers and dispatch synthetic `AT_*` events.","ref":"Anemos.CompiledSchedule.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.CompiledSchedule.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.CompiledSchedule.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Compiler","doc":"Walks the AST produced by `Anemos.Parser` and produces `%Anemos.CompiledRule{}`,\n`%Anemos.CompiledCondition{}`, and `%Anemos.CompiledCapability{}` structs with\nexecutable closures. No code generation, no Module.create.","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Boolean comparison (A-46) - Anemos.Compiler","doc":"`true` and `false` are boolean literals. `?flag == true` compares against the\nboolean `true`, not the string `\"true\"`.\n\n**Comparison does not coerce.** `?flag == \"true\"` still means *the string*\n`\"true\"` and keeps working for a host whose payload is string-valued; it does\nnot match a payload carrying the boolean. The two are different values and\n`==` says so.\n\nCoercion was rejected because the operand types are not knowable at compile\ntime. Context data arrives from arbitrary host payloads, so a rule making\n`\"true\" == true` answer `true` would also have to make `\"1\"`, `\"yes\"` and\n`\"TRUE\"` answer something, and the choice would be invisible to the policy\nauthor. Making the mismatch a *compile* error was rejected for the same\nreason: the parser sees a literal on one side and an unresolved `?var` on the\nother, and cannot know what the host will send.\n\nWhat is not acceptable is the silent wrong answer that A-46 reported. So a\ncomparison in which one side is a boolean and the other is the string\n`\"true\"` or `\"false\"` logs a warning at the moment it is evaluated, naming\nboth operands, and then answers as the values dictate — `==` false, `!=`\ntrue. The author gets told the shapes disagree rather than reading a guard\nthat never fires.\n\n## `AND` compounds; it does not merge (A-73)\n\n`AND` is two operators sharing a spelling, and which one you get is decided\nby the operands.\n\nOver booleans it is logical conjunction and means what it has always meant:\n`if ?x eq 1 AND ?y eq 2` fires only when both comparisons hold.\n\nOver anything else it **compounds** — `let update = (A) AND (B)` means \"let\n`update` have the value `A` *and* the value `B`\", not \"let `update` have the\nvalue `A`-combined-with-`B`\". The binding is a collection of both operands:\n\n    let update = (:h IN A \"1.1.1.1\") AND (:h IN AAAA \"::1\")\n\n    @update == [[\"h\", \"IN\", \"A\",    \"1.1.1.1\"],\n                [\"h\", \"IN\", \"AAAA\", \"::1\"]]\n\nThe defect this replaced was `l && r`, which in Elixir *answers `r`*\nwhenever `l` is truthy. Over non-boolean operands the left one was evaluated\nand silently thrown away, so `priv/examples/ddns.rule` read as \"update both\nrecords\" and updated one.","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html#module-boolean-comparison-a-46"},{"type":"module","title":"Why the spine is flattened before evaluation - Anemos.Compiler","doc":"A parenthesised triple pattern evaluates to a **list** —\n`[\"h\", \"IN\", \"A\", \"1.2.3.4\"]` — so a compound of triples is a list of lists,\nand a *runtime* rule such as \"if the left operand is already a list, append\nto it\" cannot tell a triple from an already-compounded pair. It would turn\n`[\"h\", \"IN\", \"A\", \"x\"] AND C` into `[\"h\", \"IN\", \"A\", \"x\", C]`.\n\n`AND` is left-associative, so `(A) AND (B) AND (C)` parses as\n`((A AND B) AND C)` and naive nesting yields `[[A, B], C]` rather than\n`[A, B, C]`.\n\nBoth problems are the same problem — runtime values are ambiguous where the\nAST spine is not. So `flatten_and_spine/1` collects the whole chain of\n`%AST.BinaryOp{op: :and}` nodes into one operand list *before* any of them\nis evaluated, and the decision is taken once over the whole list. There is\nno runtime wrapper struct and no marker tuple: a compound is an ordinary\nlist by the time it reaches a binding, because it flows on into calls like\n`[INFRA::ddns_update ...]` as an argument and host code must not have to\nunderstand an Anemos-internal type.","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html#module-why-the-spine-is-flattened-before-evaluation"},{"type":"module","title":"Mixed operands - Anemos.Compiler","doc":"Not settled by the A-73 decision; chosen here, and stated so it can be\noverruled cheaply. `true` is the **identity of conjunction**, and a falsey\noperand is **absorbing**:\n\n  * Any operand that is `false` or `nil` answers that value for the whole\n    `AND`, and nothing is compounded. This keeps every truthiness guard\n    working — `if ?missing AND ?present` stays false rather than becoming\n    the truthy list `[nil, \"here\"]`.\n  * `true` operands are dropped from the compound, since \"the value `true`\n    and the value `X`\" carries nothing `X` does not. `true AND (A)` is `A`;\n    `true AND true` is `true`.\n\nBoth alternatives were worse. Compounding `true` in would put a boolean in\nthe middle of a DNS payload; making the mix a compile error is not available\nfor the same reason it was not available to A-46 — operand types are not\nknowable at compile time, since a `?var` is whatever the host sent.\n\n### `&&` is unchanged\n\n`&&` remains purely logical `l && r`, which also leaves an author a way to\nask for the old answer-the-right-operand behaviour deliberately.\n\n## `OR` is alternation, made explicit\n\n`AND` answers *every* operand; `OR` answers the *first available* one. That\nwas already roughly true — Elixir's `||` answers its left operand whenever\nthat operand is truthy — so this is not a change of meaning the way `AND`\nwas. What changes is that the algebra is now stated and applied over the\nwhole spine at once instead of pairwise:\n\n    let pick = ?missing OR ?present            # \"here\"\n    let pick = ?a OR ?b OR ?c                  # the first one the host sent\n\n`false` and `nil` are the **identity** of disjunction and drop out. The first\noperand carrying a value answers. When every operand is falsey there is\nnothing to choose between them, so the *first* one answers — pairwise `||`\nreturned the *last*, and that single case is the whole observable behaviour\nchange.\n\n`true` answers only when it is the first available operand. Strict duality\nwith `AND`, where a falsey operand is absorbing wherever it sits, would make\n`(A) OR true` answer `true`; it answers `A`. Alternation is ordered, and a\nfallback that discarded the value it was a fallback for would make `OR`\nuseless in value position.\n\n`:or` and `:||` flatten into one spine. Unlike `AND` and `&&` there is no\ndivergence in meaning to preserve between the two spellings, because `OR` is\nonly becoming explicit about what `||` was already doing.\n\nEvaluation stays eager over the whole spine: A-56 forbids short-circuiting,\nsince an operand may be a `[MODULE::verb]` call whose side effect is the\npoint, and that holds whether or not its value turns out to be reachable.","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html#module-mixed-operands"},{"type":"module","title":"Expression position invokes modules (A-56) - Anemos.Compiler","doc":"A `[MODULE::verb]` written where a *value* is expected — `if\n[FILE::extension] eq \"pdf\"`, `let x = [PDF::title]`, `emit [EVENT::fire]`,\nan argument of another call — is dispatched at the moment the expression is\nevaluated, and the verb's return value *is* the value of the expression.\nIt is not also emitted as a separate operation; the call happens once.\n\n**Evaluating an expression therefore has side effects, and that is\ndeliberate, not an oversight.** It follows directly from the decision that a\nmodule call must have a usable return value: the value can only be had by\nmaking the call, and the call is host code that may write to a device, a\nfile or a socket. The consequences bind anyone changing this module:\n\n  * **Do not reorder expression evaluation.** Operand order is the order\n    the calls happen in.\n  * **Do not memoise or common-subexpression it.** Two occurrences of the\n    same call are two calls. A call in a `while` condition is one call per\n    iteration, including the final iteration that ends the loop. A call in\n    a `for` body is one call per element.\n  * **Do not short-circuit around it.** `and`/`or` here resolve both\n    operands before combining them, so both sides' calls happen.\n\nDispatch goes through `Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.invoke_verb/5`, which is\nthe same function the statement path uses. That is the whole point of it\nbeing shared: argument-schema validation, bounds, allow-lists and path\nconfinement (A-11) apply to a call in expression position exactly as they\ndo to one written as a statement. Calling `handle_verb/3` from here\ninstead would have been a fourth way to reach host code around a check.\n\nThe registry arrives in the evaluation context as `:registry`, and the\ncontext to hand host code as `:_verb_context` — the same shape by which\n`:data_store` already arrives for `memberof()`. Those keys are authority\nfor *evaluation*; they are not in `:_verb_context` and must never be\nforwarded to a verb.","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html#module-expression-position-invokes-modules-a-56"},{"type":"module","title":"When the verb fails - Anemos.Compiler","doc":"A failed call in expression position **aborts the rule body** and appends a\n`%{type: :verb_error, error: error}` operation, which the dispatcher turns\ninto `{:error, error}` in the results list — the same place a\nstatement-position failure lands.\n\nSubstituting `nil` and carrying on was rejected. That is precisely the\nA-25 shape: a guard comparing `nil` is merely false, so a `SECRET::unlock`\nthat refused and an unlocked secret that did not match would be\nindistinguishable, and the rule would take its `else` branch as though it\nhad asked a question and got an answer. There is no value that honestly\nrepresents \"the call did not happen\", so evaluation stops instead of\ninventing one.\n\nAborting is also why operations the body computed *before* the failure are\nkept: they are returned along with the error rather than discarded, so the\nresults list shows how far the rule got.\n\nCondition bodies and capability bodies are given the same two keys and\ndispatch the same way. A condition *predicate* is the one place with no\nchannel for an error — its answer is a boolean and nothing else — so a\nfailure there is logged and the condition answers false.","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html#module-when-the-verb-fails"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Compiler.compile/1","doc":"Compile an AST into an `%Anemos.CompilationResult{}` containing compiled rules,\nconditions, and capabilities.\n\nEach rule with `when` blocks produces one `CompiledRule` per event subscription.\nThe handler closure takes `(event_name, context, persistent_bindings)` and\nreturns `{:ok, [operation], updated_persistent_bindings}`.\nPersistent bindings hold `set` values that survive across firings.\n\nSupported statement types: `let`, `set`, `log`, `emit`, `demand`, `if`,\n`while`, `for`/`each`/`every`/`foreach`, `return`, `done`, `nextevent`,\n`nextrule`, `context`, commands (`[MODULE::verb ...]`), and triple patterns.\nGuard modifiers (`unless`, `until`) on `when`, `if`, and `at` blocks.\n\n## `nextevent` and `nextrule` carry arguments\n\nBoth take an optional `with { key: expr }` block. The pairs are resolved\nagainst the *sending* body's bindings and merged into the target's event\ncontext, so the target reads them as `?key` exactly as it would read a real\nevent payload. Merged, not replaced: the target still sees the event that got\nit there.\n\nOne resolver (`resolve_block_args/3`) and one merge\n(the dispatcher's `with_block_args` helper) serve both statements. A\nsecond path that mostly does what the first does, minus a check, is the shape\nthat accounted for four separate defects on this branch (A-39, A-59, and two\nothers), and a symmetry feature is where it gets introduced.","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html#compile/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Compiler.compile/1","doc":"iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"rule r { when EV { log \\\"hi\\\" } }\")\n    iex> {:ok, %{rules: [compiled]}} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)\n    iex> compiled.name\n    \"r\"\n    iex> compiled.subscriptions\n    [\"EV\"]\n    iex> {:ok, [%{type: :log, value: \"hi\"}], %{}} = compiled.handler.(\"EV\", %{}, %{})\n\n    iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"rule r { when EV { set i = 0 while $i < 3 { set i = $i + 1 } log $i } }\")\n    iex> {:ok, %{rules: [compiled]}} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)\n    iex> {:ok, [%{type: :log, value: 3}], _} = compiled.handler.(\"EV\", %{}, %{})\n\n`AND` answers every operand and `OR` the first available one, which is easier\nto read as code than as prose:\n\n    iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(~S|rule r { when EV { let x = (:h IN A \"1\") AND (:h IN AAAA \"2\") log @x } }|)\n    iex> {:ok, %{rules: [compiled]}} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)\n    iex> {:ok, [%{type: :log, value: value}], _} = compiled.handler.(\"EV\", %{}, %{})\n    iex> value\n    [[\"h\", \"IN\", \"A\", \"1\"], [\"h\", \"IN\", \"AAAA\", \"2\"]]\n\n    iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(~S|rule r { when EV { let x = (:h IN A \"1\") OR (:h IN AAAA \"2\") log @x } }|)\n    iex> {:ok, %{rules: [compiled]}} = Anemos.Compiler.compile(ast)\n    iex> {:ok, [%{type: :log, value: value}], _} = compiled.handler.(\"EV\", %{}, %{})\n    iex> value\n    [\"h\", \"IN\", \"A\", \"1\"]","ref":"Anemos.Compiler.html#compile/1-examples"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Anemos.DataModel","doc":"Behaviour for pluggable data format and query engine handlers.\n\nImplementations register a keyword (e.g., \"edn\", \"xml\", \"sql\")\nthat becomes valid inside data and query block bodies. The runtime\ndelegates parsing, evaluation, and transduction to the registered handler.\n\n## `transclude` and `transduce` are not the same word\n\nThey are two halves of one feature and both spellings are deliberate:\n\n  * `transclude` is the *language* construct — the optional clause in a data\n    block, `transclude to shape( ) with   as  `. It\n    survives as the `:transclude` field on `Anemos.AST.DataDecl` and\n    `Anemos.CompiledData`, and as `:transcluded` (the computed result) on a\n    `Anemos.Runtime.DataStore` entry.\n\n  * `transduce/3` is the *handler operation* that carries the clause out. The\n    runtime hands the parsed data plus the clause's shape and transducer to\n    the handler; what comes back is the transcluded value.\n\nSo a data block declares a transclusion, and the handler performs a\ntransduction to satisfy it. Handlers with no transformation support return\n`{:error, :not_supported}`.","ref":"Anemos.DataModel.html"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.DataModel.eval/2","doc":"Evaluates a parsed value against the dispatch context. Returns `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, :eval_failed}`.","ref":"Anemos.DataModel.html#c:eval/2"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.DataModel.keyword/0","doc":"Returns the keyword string identifying this data format (e.g., `\"edn\"`, `\"xml\"`).","ref":"Anemos.DataModel.html#c:keyword/0"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.DataModel.parse/1","doc":"Parses a raw string body into an internal representation. Returns `{:ok, parsed}` or `{:error, :parse_failed}`.","ref":"Anemos.DataModel.html#c:parse/1"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.DataModel.transduce/3","doc":"Transduces data into a new shape. Returns `{:ok, result}`, `{:error, :not_supported}`, or `{:error, :transduce_failed}`.","ref":"Anemos.DataModel.html#c:transduce/3"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Envelope","doc":"Message envelope for emitted values.\n\nEvery emit that targets a channel produces an envelope carrying\nthe payload, metadata, and routing information specified by\nthe language spec (section 10.3).","ref":"Anemos.Envelope.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Envelope.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Envelope.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"protocol","title":"Anemos.Event","doc":"Protocol for types that can be dispatched as Anemos events.\n\nImplement this protocol for your application's event types to make them\ndispatchable into an Anemos runtime without translation.","ref":"Anemos.Event.html"},{"type":"protocol","title":"Example - Anemos.Event","doc":"defimpl Anemos.Event, for: MyApp.MQTTMessage do\n      def name(msg), do: msg.topic |> String.upcase() |> String.replace(\"/\", \"_\")\n      def context(msg), do: msg.topic\n      def payload(msg), do: msg.payload\n    end\n\n    # Then dispatch directly:\n    Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, mqtt_message)","ref":"Anemos.Event.html#module-example"},{"type":"protocol","title":"Required callbacks - Anemos.Event","doc":"* `name/1` — the event name for rule matching (e.g., `\"SENSOR_MOTION_DETECTED\"`)\n  * `context/1` — the context path for listener filtering (e.g., `\"garage.motion\"`)\n  * `payload/1` — map of key-value data for `?` variable resolution","ref":"Anemos.Event.html#module-required-callbacks"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Event.t/0","doc":"All the types that implement this protocol.","ref":"Anemos.Event.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Event.context/1","doc":"Context path for listener-side filtering. Return `nil` for unscoped events.","ref":"Anemos.Event.html#context/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Event.name/1","doc":"Event name for rule matching.","ref":"Anemos.Event.html#name/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Event.payload/1","doc":"Payload map — values are available via `?` variable resolution in policies.","ref":"Anemos.Event.html#payload/1"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Lexer","doc":"NimbleParsec-based tokenizer for the Anemos DSL.\n\nProduces a flat list of `{type, metadata, value}` tuples.\n\n`true` and `false` produce `:boolean` tokens carrying real booleans, not\nidentifiers. `Anemos.Compiler`'s moduledoc records what that means for\n`?var == \"true\"` — the short version is that comparison does not coerce and a\nboolean/boolean-string mismatch warns at evaluation time.","ref":"Anemos.Lexer.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Lexer.raw_tokens/2","doc":"Parses the given `binary` as raw_tokens.\n\nReturns `{:ok, [token], rest, context, position, byte_offset}` or\n`{:error, reason, rest, context, line, byte_offset}` where `position`\ndescribes the location of the raw_tokens (start position) as `{line, offset_to_start_of_line}`.\n\nTo column where the error occurred can be inferred from `byte_offset - offset_to_start_of_line`.","ref":"Anemos.Lexer.html#raw_tokens/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Lexer.raw_tokens/2","doc":"* `:byte_offset` - the byte offset for the whole binary, defaults to 0\n  * `:line` - the line and the byte offset into that line, defaults to `{1, byte_offset}`\n  * `:context` - the initial context value. It will be converted to a map","ref":"Anemos.Lexer.html#raw_tokens/2-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Lexer.tokenize/1","doc":"Tokenize the given input string.\n\nReturns `{:ok, tokens}` or `{:error, %Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct{}}` with code\n`anemos.lexer/invalid_token`, whose `field` is `\"line:column\"`.","ref":"Anemos.Lexer.html#tokenize/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Lexer.tokenize/1","doc":"iex> {:ok, tokens} = Anemos.Lexer.tokenize(\"rule test { }\")\n    iex> Enum.map(tokens, &elem(&1, 0))\n    [:keyword, :identifier, :lbrace, :rbrace]\n\n    iex> {:ok, tokens} = Anemos.Lexer.tokenize(\"when EVENT for 5min { }\")\n    iex> Enum.map(tokens, &elem(&1, 0))\n    [:keyword, :module_id, :keyword, :duration, :lbrace, :rbrace]\n\n    iex> {:error, error} = Anemos.Lexer.tokenize(\"@@@\")\n    iex> error.code\n    \"anemos.lexer/invalid_token\"","ref":"Anemos.Lexer.html#tokenize/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Module","doc":"Macro for defining Anemos modules — named sets of verb handlers.\n\nMost modules are thin wrappers around existing functions. This macro\neliminates the boilerplate of implementing the `Anemos.Runtime.Module`\nbehaviour by hand.","ref":"Anemos.Module.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Usage - Anemos.Module","doc":"# lib/my_app/anemos/secret.ex — inside MyApp.Anemos.Secret\n    use Anemos.Module, name: \"SECRET\"\n\n    verb :unlock, &MyApp.Vault.unlock/2\n\nThen register it:\n\n    Anemos.Runtime.register(:anemos, MyApp.Anemos.Secret)\n\n> #### Wrap secret-bearing return values {: .warning}\n>\n> A verb that returns credential material should wrap it in\n> `Anemos.Secret.new/2`. The wrapper redacts through `inspect/1`,\n> string interpolation and `Logger`, which is what keeps a secret out of\n> the application log when a policy does `log @token` or emits through\n> the default channel connector. Nothing else on that path will redact\n> it for you.\n\nThe module carries its own name — no string to fat-finger at the call site.","ref":"Anemos.Module.html#module-usage"},{"type":"module","title":"Verb functions - Anemos.Module","doc":"Each verb function receives `(args, context)` where `args` is the list\nof resolved arguments from the policy and `context` is the dispatch context\nmap. It should return `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, reason}`.\n\nIf your function takes a different arity, wrap it:\n\n    verb :get_ip, fn args, _ctx -> MyApp.Infra.get_ip(hd(args)) end","ref":"Anemos.Module.html#module-verb-functions"},{"type":"macro","title":"Anemos.Module.verb/2","doc":"Registers a verb handler.\n\nThe function should accept `(args :: list(), context :: map())`.","ref":"Anemos.Module.html#verb/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Parser","doc":"Recursive descent parser for the Anemos DSL.\n\nConsumes the token stream from `Anemos.Lexer.tokenize/1` and produces\nan `Anemos.AST.Program` struct.\n\nSupported declarations: `rule`, `condition`, `capability`, `data`, `query`,\nand source-level `signed_by` (A-60).\n\nSupported trigger blocks: `when`, `if`, `at`, `context`.\nGuard modifiers: `unless`, `until` (on `when`, `if`, `at`).\n\nSupported statements: `let`, `set`, `log`, `emit`, `demand`,\n`while`, `for`, `each`, `every`, `foreach`,\n`return`, `done`, `nextevent`, `nextrule`,\nand `[MODULE::verb ...]` command expressions.\n\n## `parse/1` is total, and that is a security property\n\nFor **any** binary, `parse/1` answers `{:ok, %AST.Program{}}` or\n`{:error, message}`. It does not raise, and it terminates.\n\nThis is not tidiness. `parse/1` is the only path from source text to an AST —\n`Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate/3` runs it *before* deciding whether the\nsource may be trusted — and under the multi-tenant decision that text is\nuntrusted input. Every caller matching `{:error, _}` gets an exception\ninstead if a clause is missing, and a truncated or hostile `.rule` file is\nexactly the input this boundary is most likely to meet.\n\nTotality has been broken three times (A-65, A-74, A-75), so the rule is\nwritten down:\n\n> **Whoever knows what must come next owns the end-of-input check.**\n\nA function that dispatches on token shape must therefore be total over\n*both* an empty token list **and** a token it does not expect. Missing either\nis the same defect: `collect_simple_list/2` had its `[]` clause and still\ncrashed, on a token type it had no clause for (A-75).\n\nThe converse matters too, or the rule just breeds dead code. Where a caller\nselects a function by matching the token itself, the caller has already\nanswered and the callee must *not* answer again. Exactly three functions here\nhave no `[]` clause for that reason — `parse_declaration/1`,\n`parse_bracket_expr/1` and `parse_format_block/1` — and each carries a\ncomment saying which caller answered, so a totality sweep does not \"fix\"\nthem. When in doubt, add the clause and let Dialyzer adjudicate: it reports\nan unreachable pattern, which is precisely the question being asked (A-71).\n\nBoth classes are covered by tests rather than by inspection —\n`Anemos.ParserTruncationTest` sweeps every prefix of every shipped example,\nand `Anemos.ParserFuzzTest` mutates them.","ref":"Anemos.Parser.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Parser.parse/1","doc":"Parse a source string into an AST.\n\nReturns `{:ok, %Anemos.AST.Program{}}` or `{:error, message}`.","ref":"Anemos.Parser.html#parse/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Parser.parse/1","doc":"iex> {:ok, %Anemos.AST.Program{declarations: [rule]}} =\n    ...>   Anemos.Parser.parse(\"rule test { when EVENT { log \\\"hello\\\" } }\")\n    iex> rule.name\n    \"test\"\n    iex> [%Anemos.AST.When{event: \"EVENT\", body: [%Anemos.AST.Log{}]}] = rule.body\n\n    iex> {:error, %Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct{code: \"anemos.parser/syntax_error\"}} =\n    ...>   Anemos.Parser.parse(\"rule { }\")\n\nA source-level `signed_by` is a declaration in its own right, so a file\ncarrying no `rule` can still make a provenance claim (A-60):\n\n    iex> {:ok, %Anemos.AST.Program{declarations: declarations}} =\n    ...>   Anemos.Parser.parse(\"signed_by :operator\\ncondition c { when EV { log \\\"x\\\" } }\")\n    iex> Enum.find(declarations, &match?(%Anemos.AST.SignedBy{}, &1))\n    %Anemos.AST.SignedBy{signer: \"operator\", line: 1}\n\nTruncated input returns an error rather than raising. `parse/1` is total over\nbinaries because it runs before the trust boundary, not after it:\n\n    iex> {:error, error} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"rule r { when\")\n    iex> error.message\n    \"Expected event name after 'when' but got end of input\"\n\n    iex> {:ok, %Anemos.AST.Program{declarations: []}} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"\")","ref":"Anemos.Parser.html#parse/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Redact","doc":"Shape summaries for values that must be observable without being disclosed.\n\nAnemos emits are deliberately never silent, but \"not silent\" must not mean\n\"fully disclosed\". `summarize/1` describes a term's type and size — enough\nto see that an emit happened and roughly what it carried — while never\nincluding the term's contents, keys, or any `Anemos.Secret` plaintext.","ref":"Anemos.Redact.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Redact.for_trace/1","doc":"A value as the trace may keep it: every `Anemos.Secret` replaced by\n`\"[REDACTED]\"`, strings cut at 1000 bytes, lists and maps cut at\n50 entries, nesting cut at depth 4, and anything that is none of\nthose described by `summarize/1`. The table the trace lives in is public\nand the ring holds hundreds of entries; what goes in has to be safe to\nread and small enough to keep.","ref":"Anemos.Redact.html#for_trace/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Redact.summarize/1","doc":"Describes a term by type and size, never by content.","ref":"Anemos.Redact.html#summarize/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Redact.summarize/1","doc":"iex> Anemos.Redact.summarize(\"hello\")\n    \"binary(5 bytes)\"\n\n    iex> Anemos.Redact.summarize(%{a: 1, b: 2})\n    \"map(2 keys)\"\n\n    iex> Anemos.Redact.summarize(Anemos.Secret.new(\"hunter2\"))\n    \"secret(redacted)\"","ref":"Anemos.Redact.html#summarize/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime","doc":"Supervisable runtime interpreter for Anemos policies.\n\nStart a blank interpreter in your supervision tree, then load modules\nand policies into it dynamically.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Usage - Anemos.Runtime","doc":"# Start the interpreter\n    children = [\n      {Anemos.Runtime, name: :anemos}\n    ]\n    Supervisor.start_link(children, strategy: :one_for_one)\n\n    # Register modules\n    Anemos.Runtime.register(:anemos, \"DEVICE\", MyApp.Device)\n    Anemos.Runtime.register(:anemos, \"INFRA\", MyApp.Infra)\n\n    # Load policies\n    Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, File.read!(\"policies/ddns.rule\"))\n    Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, File.read!(\"policies/garage.rule\"))\n\n    # Dispatch events\n    Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"SENSOR_MOTION_DETECTED\")","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#module-usage"},{"type":"module","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime","doc":"* `:name` — required, used to register and address the runtime\n  * `:keyring` — `%{signer_name => Comn.Secrets.Key.t()}` of **public**\n    keys, used by `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed` to verify the\n    detached signature of a policy that declares `signed_by` (A-05).\n    Defaults to empty, which means every declared signer is unknown and\n    therefore refused. See `Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore`.\n  * `:unsigned_policy` — `:strict | :warn | :permit` for sources that\n    declare no signer at all. Defaults to `:permit`; see\n    `Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore` for why the default is the insecure one\n    and when that must be revisited.\n  * `:watch` — a directory of `*.rule` files to keep deployed: saving a\n    file deploys it, deleting it unloads it. See `Anemos.Runtime.Watcher`.\n  * `:modules` — `%{\"NAME\" => module}` bound from the start, so that a\n    policy deployed at boot already has them. `register/3` after start\n    does the same thing later.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#module-options"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.load_failure/0","doc":"One declaration that loaded but will never do anything.\n\nA structured error. Its `code` says what kind of declaration and why —\n`anemos.scheduler/ ` for a schedule, `anemos.data/ ` for a\ndata block — and its `field` names the declaration: the synthetic `AT_*`\nevent name for a schedule, the block name for a data block.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#t:load_failure/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.condition_state/2","doc":"Returns the current boolean state of a named condition, or `nil` if not found.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#condition_state/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.connect/2","doc":"Connects a channel connector to the runtime.\n\nThe connector must implement the `Anemos.Channel` behaviour.\nReplaces the current connector (default: `Anemos.Channel.Logger`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#connect/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.describe/2","doc":"Everything the runtime holds, as it stands now.\n\n`%{policies, rules, conditions, capabilities, data_blocks, schedules,\nmodules}`. Each rule carries the policy that declared it, the events it\nlistens for, its context, its `set` bindings and the state of its dwell\ntimers; each condition its current value.\n\nBindings are returned as they are. An `Anemos.Secret` stays wrapped.\n\nOptions: `:timeout` (default 5000 ms), for each of the calls this makes.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#describe/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.dispatch/4","doc":"Dispatches an event into the running interpreter.\n\nAccepts either a string event name (with optional context map) or any\nvalue implementing the `Anemos.Event` protocol.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#dispatch/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.dispatch/4","doc":"# String API\n    Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"SENSOR_MOTION_DETECTED\")\n    Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"EVENT\", %{context_path: \"garage\"})\n\n    # Protocol API — any type that implements Anemos.Event\n    Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, my_mqtt_message)\n\n    # Map shorthand\n    Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, %{name: \"MOTION\", context: \"garage\", payload: %{}})\n\nOptions: `:timeout`, how long to wait for the rules to run (default\n5000 ms). A context map's `:correlation_id` is carried into the ambient\n`Comn` context the rules run under, so what they emit shares it.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#dispatch/4-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.explain/4","doc":"What dispatching `event_name` would do, without doing it.\n\n`%{rules: [...], conditions: [...]}`. Each rule that listens for the event\nis listed with the policy that declared it and an `outcome`: `:fires`,\n`{:dwell, ms}` for a `when ... for` rule that would arm or hold its timer,\n`:not_true` for a compound `when (A AND B)` whose state the report would\nleave false,\nor `:context_mismatch` when its `context` does not admit `context`'s\n`:context_path`. `conditions` names the conditions that would evaluate.\n\nNothing is evaluated: a guard, an `if`, or a verb called in an expression\ncan still change what a rule that `:fires` goes on to do.\n\nOptions: `:timeout` (default 5000 ms).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#explain/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.load/3","doc":"Loads a policy source string into the running interpreter. **Privileged.**\n\nParses and compiles the source, then adds the resulting rules,\nconditions, and capabilities to the dispatcher and arms any schedules.\nCan be called multiple times — policies accumulate.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#load/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Deploying: policy: id - Anemos.Runtime.load/3","doc":"A load that names its policy replaces whatever was last loaded under that\nname, exactly: afterwards the runtime holds what this source declares and\nnothing the previous version declared that this one does not.\n\n    Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, File.read!(path), policy: \"garage/lights.rule\")\n\nA rule or condition whose text did not change keeps its state — `set`\nbindings, armed dwell timers, the condition's boolean — however much of\nthe file around it was edited. One whose text changed starts from nothing.\nA source that fails to load changes nothing; the deployed version keeps\nrunning. Declaring a rule, condition or capability name that another\nnamed policy holds is refused, and nothing is loaded.\n\nWithout `:policy` the load is anonymous: it can only add, and replace\nrules by name — and not a name a named policy holds, which is refused the\nsame way. The name is passed to the load authority as\n`Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest`'s `policy`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#load/3-deploying-policy-id"},{"type":"function","title":"This is the trust boundary - Anemos.Runtime.load/3","doc":"Whoever controls `source` controls every verb registered in this runtime.\nThe call is therefore gated (A-12): it is checked against a source-size\nceiling, then handed to the module configured as `config :anemos,\n:load_authority` as an `Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest`. It is also *not*\nreachable through `act/1` — see `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority` for why\neach of those three is here and what each is worth.\n\nPass whatever your authority needs to identify the caller as\n`:authorization`:\n\n    Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source, authorization: %{tenant: \"acme\", token: tok})\n\nThe default authority permits everything, so an embedder that has not\nconfigured one sees the pre-A-12 behaviour unchanged.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#load/3-this-is-the-trust-boundary"},{"type":"function","title":"Return values - Anemos.Runtime.load/3","doc":"* `:ok` — the whole policy loaded and every schedule armed.\n\n  * `{:partial, failures}` — the policy loaded, but the listed\n    declarations are inert and will never fire. `failures` is a list of\n    `t:load_failure/0`. The rules, conditions and capabilities in the\n    same source *did* load and will fire; this is not a rollback.\n\n  * `{:error, reason}` — nothing loaded. The source failed to parse or\n    compile, or the load was refused. A refusal is a\n    `Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct` with code `anemos.runtime/load_denied`; a\n    parse or compile failure is one with a code under `anemos.lexer/`,\n    `anemos.parser/` or `anemos.compiler/`.\n\nThe distinction between the last two is the point. Arming a schedule and\nstarting a data block subscription are per-declaration best-effort — an\ninterval below the floor or a protocol with no registered handler stops\nthat one declaration, not the policy. Collapsing that into `{:error, _}`\nwould claim the rules had not loaded, when they had; collapsing it into\n`:ok` (as this function used to) hides a policy that will never fire.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#load/3-return-values"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.load/3","doc":"case Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source) do\n      :ok -> :ready\n      {:partial, failures} -> Logger.warning(\"inert declarations: \" <> inspect(failures))\n      {:error, reason} -> {:error, reason}\n    end","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#load/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.policies/1","doc":"The loaded policies in load order, each as\n`%{id, rules, conditions, capabilities, schedules, data_blocks}` with the\nnames it declares. A load that named no policy has `id: nil`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#policies/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.put_context/3","doc":"Stores context data for `?` variable resolution.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#put_context/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.register/2","doc":"Registers a module with the runtime.\n\nAccepts either a module built with `use Anemos.Module` (which carries\nits own name), or a name string and module pair.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#register/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.register/2","doc":"# Module with embedded name\n    Anemos.Runtime.register(:anemos, MyApp.Anemos.Secret)\n\n    # Explicit name\n    Anemos.Runtime.register(:anemos, \"DEVICE\", MyApp.Device)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#register/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.register/3","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#register/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.register_data_model/2","doc":"Registers an `Anemos.DataModel` handler under the keyword it answers from\n`keyword/0`. The keyword then names a format in a `data` block\n(`data d { edn { ... } }`) and an engine in a `query` block.\n\nLike a module, a handler is the host's to supply: the runtime ships none.\nRegister it before loading a policy that names it — a `data` block whose\nformat has no handler is a failure of that `load/3`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#register_data_model/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the runtime supervisor.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.trace/2","doc":"The most recent dispatches, newest first.\n\nEach is `%{at, event, context_path, steps, conditions, results,\nduration_us}`. `steps` is every rule the event reached, in order, with\nwhat happened — `:fired`, `:dwell`, `:dwell_fired`, `:not_true` or\n`:context_mismatch` — including rules reached through `nextevent`, `nextrule` and `emit ...\nas`. `conditions` names the conditions that became true. `results` gives\nthe kind of each operation; a `log` carries its text, since that was\nwritten to the log anyway, and nothing else carries a value.\n\nThe last 200 are kept, and they outlive a dispatcher crash — which is\nwhen they are wanted.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#trace/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.unload/2","doc":"Removes a policy that was loaded with `policy: policy_id`, and everything\nit declared: rules and their state, conditions, capabilities, global data\nblocks and their live feeds, and any schedule no other policy declares.\n\nNot gated: whoever can call it can remove any policy by name. The gated\nway is from inside a policy — `[POLICY::unload \"id\"]`, see\n`Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Policy` — where the authority that admitted the\ncalling policy stands for the removal (A-81).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#unload/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.watcher_name/1","doc":"The registered name of the directory watcher started by `watch:`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.html#watcher_name/1"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema","doc":"Validates the arguments of a `[MODULE::verb ...]` command before they\nreach a host module.\n\nArguments arrive from two untrusted places — policy literals and event\npayloads — and used to be handed to `handle_verb/3` verbatim (A-11). This\nmodule is the check, and the dispatcher is the only caller, so a host\nmodule cannot be reached by a path that skipped it.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html"},{"type":"module","title":"The schema format, and why it is this small - Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema","doc":"A schema is a **list of positional argument specs**, one per argument.\nEach spec is a keyword list drawn from a fixed five-key vocabulary:\n\n  * `:type` — `:string`, `:integer`, `:float`, `:number`, `:boolean`,\n    `:atom` or `:any` (default `:any`)\n  * `:max_bytes` — upper bound on a string argument's byte size\n  * `:in` — allow-list of permitted values\n  * `:path` — the name of a root from `config :anemos, :path_roots`; the\n    argument is confined to it by `Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard`\n  * `:optional` — the argument may be absent (only meaningful on a\n    trailing run of specs)\n\nPlus one whole-schema form: a trailing spec carrying `rest: true` applies\nto every remaining argument, for genuinely variadic verbs.\n\n    def arg_schema(\"read\"),  do: [[type: :string, path: :spool]]\n    def arg_schema(\"set\"),   do: [[type: :string, max_bytes: 128], [type: :any]]\n    def arg_schema(\"mode\"),  do: [[type: :string, in: [\"on\", \"off\"]]]\n    def arg_schema(\"log\"),   do: [[type: :string, max_bytes: 4096, rest: true]]\n    def arg_schema(_verb),   do: nil\n\nThere is no nesting, no predicate function, no cross-argument\ndependency and no custom error text. That is deliberate: the vocabulary\nis exactly what an untrusted-input boundary needs — *is it the right\nshape, is it bounded, is it one of the values I allow, and if it names a\nfile does it stay in its root* — and nothing beyond it. A schema\nexpressive enough to encode business rules would be a second policy\nlanguage inside the policy engine, and every key added to it is another\nkey a reviewer has to reason about when auditing whether a verb is safe.\nAnything a host module needs past this vocabulary belongs in that host\nmodule's own body, where it can return a domain error.\n\nArity is `length(schema)` less any trailing `optional: true` specs; a\n`rest: true` tail makes the upper bound unlimited.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html#module-the-schema-format-and-why-it-is-this-small"},{"type":"module","title":"Validation is normalizing for paths only - Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema","doc":"`validate/3` returns the argument list to pass onward. For every spec but\n`:path` that list is the input unchanged. A `:path` argument is replaced\nby its expanded, confined absolute path, because passing the original\nstring on would let the host module re-resolve it and undo the check.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html#module-validation-is-normalizing-for-paths-only"},{"type":"module","title":"Messages never quote the value - Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema","doc":"A rejection names the position and the rule it broke, and describes the\noffending value only through `Anemos.Redact.summarize/1`. The value is\nattacker-controlled and the error text ends up in logs.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html#module-messages-never-quote-the-value"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.spec/0","doc":"One positional argument spec.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html#t:spec/0"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.t/0","doc":"A verb's argument schema, or `nil` for an unvalidated verb.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.validate/3","doc":"Checks `args` against `schema`, returning the arguments to pass onward.\n\n`nil` means the verb declared no schema and the arguments pass through\nunchanged — that is what keeps existing modules working.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html#validate/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.validate/3","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.validate(nil, [\"anything\", 5], \"v\")\n    {:ok, [\"anything\", 5]}\n\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.validate([[type: :string]], [\"ok\"], \"v\")\n    {:ok, [\"ok\"]}\n\n    iex> {:error, error} = Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.validate([[type: :integer]], [\"nope\"], \"v\")\n    iex> {error.code, error.message}\n    {\"anemos.runtime/invalid_argument\", \"verb 'v' argument 1: expected integer, got binary(4 bytes)\"}","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema.html#validate/3-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins","doc":"Built-in collection-processing functions for pipeline expressions.\n\nCalled directly by the compiler when evaluating pipe expressions\nwith filter/map/reduce/emit. Not user-facing — no module registration.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.emit/4","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.html#emit/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.filter/5","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.html#filter/5"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.map/5","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.html#map/5"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.reduce/7","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Builtins.html#reduce/7"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer","doc":"GenServer for a single named capability.\nPassive until demanded. Executes its `on demand` handler and returns the result.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.html"},{"type":"module","title":"The context check lives here - Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer","doc":"A capability's `context` line names who may demand it. The check is made\nin this process rather than in `Anemos.Runtime.Demand` because this is\nwhere the capability's own declared context lives: a caller cannot reach\nthe handler without passing an argument that says where it is standing,\nand an argument it forgets to pass is a denial rather than a pass (A-07).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.html#module-the-context-check-lives-here"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.execute/3","doc":"Executes the capability's on-demand handler with the given context.\n\n`demanding_context` is the context path of the rule making the demand.\nIt must lie within the capability's declared context — see\n`Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope`. A capability declared without a context is\ndemandable from anywhere; a capability *with* one refuses a demander that\nnames no context at all.\n\nReturns `{:ok, operations}`, `{:error, {:context_denied, error}}`, or\n`{:error, reason}` from the handler.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.html#execute/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.execute/3","doc":"iex> {:ok, pid} = Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.start_link(name_str: \"k\", context: nil, handler: fn _ -> {:ok, []} end)\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.execute(pid, %{})\n    {:ok, []}\n\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.start_link(name_str: \"k\", context: \"infra.networking\", handler: fn _ -> {:ok, [:secret]} end)\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.execute(pid, %{}, \"infra.networking.dns\")\n    {:ok, [:secret]}\n    iex> {:error, {:context_denied, _}} = Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.execute(pid, %{}, nil)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.html#execute/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a capability server.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name_str` — the capability name (required)\n  * `:context` — the context scope, or `nil` for an unscoped capability (required)\n  * `:handler` — 1-arity function `(context) -> {:ok, ops} | {:error, reason}` (required)\n  * `:name` — process registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer","doc":"GenServer for a single named condition.\nHolds boolean state. Evaluates incoming events against trigger logic.\nBroadcasts `CONDITION_ ` on false->true transition.\nQueryable for current state at any time.\n\nOn a false->true transition the condition body's side effects run and yield a\nlist of operations. Those operations only go somewhere if an `:on_side_effects`\ncallback was supplied — nothing here can execute a `:command` or route an\n`:emit`, which is the dispatcher's job. Without the callback they are dropped.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.get_state/1","doc":"Returns the current boolean state of the condition.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.html#get_state/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.get_state/1","doc":"iex> {:ok, pid} = Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.start_link(name_str: \"test\", subscriptions: [\"EV\"], evaluator: fn _, _ -> true end)\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.get_state(pid)\n    false","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.html#get_state/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.handle_event/3","doc":"Sends an event to the condition server for evaluation.\n\nIf the event matches a subscription and the evaluator returns `true`,\nthe condition transitions to true. Only false->true transitions trigger\nthe `on_transition` callback.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.html#handle_event/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a condition server.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name_str` — the condition name (required)\n  * `:subscriptions` — list of event names this condition listens to (required)\n  * `:evaluator` — 2-arity function `(event_name, context) -> boolean` (required)\n  * `:side_effects` — AST nodes to evaluate on transition (optional)\n  * `:side_effect_handler` — function called on false->true (optional). The\n    compiler produces a 1-arity function taking the triggering event context;\n    a 0-arity function is also accepted.\n  * `:on_side_effects` — callback `(name, [operation]) -> any` receiving the\n    operations the side-effect handler produced (optional). Without it the\n    operations are discarded — see the moduledoc.\n  * `:on_transition` — callback `(name) -> any` called on false->true (optional)\n  * `:name` — process registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ConditionServer.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope","doc":"The one comparison that decides whether one context path lies inside another.\n\nUnder the 2026-08-06 multi-tenant decision `context` is a security\nboundary, so this predicate is the boundary check itself, and two\nproperties matter more than convenience:\n\n**It compares path segments, not string prefixes.** `garage` and\n`garageband.amp` share a prefix and share no context; treating\n`String.starts_with?/2` as containment let the second reach rules scoped\nto the first (A-06).\n\n**It fails closed.** An absent inner path is not \"unscoped and therefore\nallowed\" — it is a caller that did not say where it was standing, and a\ndeclared boundary has to refuse it. The one direction that opens is an\nabsent *outer* path, which means no boundary was declared at all.\n\nBoth the dispatcher's rule filter (A-06, A-39) and the capability\nserver's demand gate (A-07) call this, deliberately: two copies of a\nboundary check are two chances to fix only one of them.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope.within?/2","doc":"True when `inner` names the same context as `outer` or a context beneath it.\n\n`outer` is the declared boundary — a rule's `context` block, or a\ncapability's `context` line. `inner` is whoever is trying to cross it —\nthe event's `:context_path`, or the demanding rule's context.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope.html#within?/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope.within?/2","doc":"iex> alias Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope\n    iex> ContextScope.within?(\"garage\", \"garage\")\n    true\n    iex> ContextScope.within?(\"garage.door\", \"garage\")\n    true\n    iex> ContextScope.within?(\"garageband.amp\", \"garage\")\n    false\n    iex> ContextScope.within?(nil, \"garage\")\n    false\n    iex> ContextScope.within?(nil, nil)\n    true","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope.html#within?/2-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore","doc":"Agent holding external key-value context data.\nKeys are dotted paths (e.g., \"infra.networking.dns.fqdn\").\nResolution joins the context path with the variable name to form the lookup key.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.put/3","doc":"Stores a key-value pair.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.html#put/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.resolve/3","doc":"Resolves a variable name against a context path.\n\nJoins the context path and name with `.` to form the full key,\nthen looks it up in the store.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.html#resolve/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.resolve/3","doc":"iex> {:ok, store} = Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.start_link(data: [{\"infra.dns.fqdn\", \"host.example.tld\"}])\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.resolve(store, \"infra.dns\", \"fqdn\")\n    {:ok, \"host.example.tld\"}\n\n    iex> {:ok, store} = Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.start_link([])\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.resolve(store, \"infra\", \"missing\")\n    :error","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.html#resolve/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a context store.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.start_link/1","doc":"* `:data` — initial key-value pairs as a list of `{key, value}` tuples (optional)\n  * `:name` — process registration name (optional)\n  * `:state_table` — an `Anemos.Runtime.StateTable` the data is kept in,\n    so that this process restarting does not lose it (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ContextStore.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry","doc":"Agent mapping data model keyword strings to handler modules.\n\nHandler modules must implement the `Anemos.DataModel` behaviour and\nexpose a `keyword/0` function that returns their format identifier.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.list/1","doc":"Returns all registered keyword strings.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#list/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.list/1","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.list(reg)\n    []","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#list/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.lookup/2","doc":"Looks up a handler module by keyword string.\n\nReturns `{:ok, module}` or `:error`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#lookup/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.lookup/2","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.lookup(reg, \"missing\")\n    :error","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#lookup/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.register/2","doc":"Registers a handler module under its own keyword.\n\nThe module must implement `Anemos.DataModel` and return a keyword from `keyword/0`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#register/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.register/2","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.register(reg, MyHandler)\n    :ok","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#register/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a data model registry.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore","doc":"GenServer holding named datasets for data blocks.\n\nEntries are keyed by `{name, scope_tag}` where `scope_tag` is `:global`\nor `{:local, discriminator}`. Local entries shadow global entries in every\nlookup.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html"},{"type":"module","title":"What a scope discriminator is - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore","doc":"Any term. It is *not* required to be a pid, and in the interpreter it\nusually is not: rules do not run as separate processes — every rule body\nexecutes inside the dispatcher — so a pid discriminator would collapse\nevery rule's inline `data` block into one scope. `Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher`\ntherefore scopes rule-local blocks by rule *name*, and the compiled\n`memberof()` closure passes the same rule name as `:scope_hint`.\n\nEvery lookup takes that hint the same way. `get/3` previously hardcoded\n`self()` while `member?/4` and `member_any?/4` honoured a `:scope_hint`,\nso `get` could not reach a rule-scoped entry at all — two lookup functions\nwith two different scope models (A-24). The hint defaults to `self()`,\nwhich is what a host process registering data for itself wants.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#module-what-a-scope-discriminator-is"},{"type":"module","title":"Cleanup - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore","doc":"Two paths, because only one of them can exist for a given discriminator:\n\n  * **pid discriminators are monitored.** When the process dies, every\n    entry scoped to it is removed. This is automatic and needs no caller.\n\n  * **every other discriminator must be dropped explicitly**, with\n    `drop_scope/2` or `deregister/3`. There is no process to watch, so\n    there is nothing to make cleanup automatic — an unloaded policy's\n    rule-scoped entries persist until something says to remove them, and\n    before `drop_scope/2` existed nothing could. Reloading a policy\n    re-registered under the same key, so entries did not multiply, but\n    they also never went away.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#module-cleanup"},{"type":"module","title":"Registering a dataset - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore","doc":"DataStore.register(store, \"reserved-ips\", :global,\n      MapSet.new([\"10.0.0.0/8\", \"192.168.0.0/16\"]))","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#module-registering-a-dataset"},{"type":"module","title":"Membership check (caller's local scope shadows global) - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore","doc":"DataStore.member?(store, \"reserved-ips\", \"10.0.0.0/8\")\n    # => true","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#module-membership-check-caller-s-local-scope-shadows-global"},{"type":"module","title":"Union membership across multiple sets - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore","doc":"DataStore.member_any?(store, [\"blocklist\", \"denylist\"], ip)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#module-union-membership-across-multiple-sets"},{"type":"module","title":"Fetch full dataset - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore","doc":"DataStore.get(store, \"reserved-ips\")\n    # => %MapSet{...}","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#module-fetch-full-dataset"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.deregister/3","doc":"Removes a named entry for the given scope.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#deregister/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.drop_scope/2","doc":"Removes every entry registered under `scope_tag`.\n\nThis is the cleanup path for scopes that cannot be monitored — a rule\nname, or any other non-pid discriminator. Idempotent.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#drop_scope/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.get/3","doc":"Returns the full dataset for `name`, or `nil` if not found.\n\nChecks local scope first (by `scope_hint` if provided, otherwise `self()`),\nthen global — the same resolution `member?/4` performs.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#get/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.member?/4","doc":"Returns true if `value` is a member of the named dataset.\n\nChecks local scope first (by `scope_hint` if provided, otherwise `self()`),\nthen global. `get/3` resolves scope identically.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#member?/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.member_any?/4","doc":"Returns true if `value` is a member of any of the named datasets.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#member_any?/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.register/5","doc":"Registers (or replaces) a named dataset under a scope tag.\n\n`scope_tag` is `:global` or `{:local, discriminator}` — see the moduledoc\nfor what may be a discriminator and how each kind is cleaned up.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#register/5"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.register/5","doc":"* `:source` — string describing origin\n  * `:transcluded` — transcluded ref term\n  * `:subscription` — monitor ref for live feeds","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#register/5-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a DataStore process.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — optional registration name","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.update/4","doc":"Replaces the dataset for an existing entry. Used by subscription callbacks.\n\nNo-op if the entry does not exist.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.DataStore.html#update/4"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Demand","doc":"Evaluates a stack of demand operations against capability servers\nusing PAM-style control flag semantics.\n\nFlags:\n- `:requisite` (default) — must succeed, fail immediately if not\n- `:required` — must succeed, but evaluate rest of stack before failing\n- `:optional` — continue whether it succeeds or fails\n- `:sufficient` — if succeeds, skip rest of stack","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Demand.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Missing capabilities are not failures - Anemos.Runtime.Demand","doc":"The flags above govern how *failure* is handled. A capability that is not\npresent in the registry has not failed — it was never loaded, which is a\nconfiguration error in the policy text (typically a typo in the capability\nname). Such a demand is fatal under every flag, including `:optional` and\n`:sufficient`.\n\nWere it otherwise, a stack composed entirely of `:optional` demands naming\ncapabilities that do not exist would return `{:ok, []}` — a gate that reads\nas an authorization check while authorizing nothing.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Demand.html#module-missing-capabilities-are-not-failures"},{"type":"module","title":"Neither is a context denial - Anemos.Runtime.Demand","doc":"For the same reason, a capability refused because the demanding rule sits\noutside its declared context is fatal under every flag. Failure flags\ndescribe how much a policy may tolerate a capability that *tried and could\nnot*; they do not get to describe how much it may tolerate crossing a\nsecurity boundary. The demanding rule's context is read from the\n`:_rule_source` the dispatcher stamps into the execution context, and an\nabsent one denies — see `Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Demand.html#module-neither-is-a-context-denial"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Demand.evaluate/3","doc":"Evaluates a demand stack against capability servers using PAM-style control flags.\n\nEach demand in the list must have `:capability` (name) and `:flag` (one of\n`:requisite`, `:required`, `:optional`, `:sufficient`).\n\nReturns `{:ok, operations}` or `{:error, reason}`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Demand.html#evaluate/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.Demand.evaluate/3","doc":"iex> {:ok, pid} = Anemos.Runtime.CapabilityServer.start_link(name_str: \"k\", context: nil, handler: fn _ -> {:ok, [%{type: :log, value: \"ok\"}]} end)\n    iex> {:ok, ops} = Anemos.Runtime.Demand.evaluate([%{capability: \"k\", flag: :requisite}], %{\"k\" => pid}, %{})\n    iex> [%{type: :log, value: \"ok\"}] = ops\n    [%{type: :log, value: \"ok\"}]","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Demand.html#evaluate/3-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher","doc":"GenServer holding compiled rules indexed by subscription.\n\nDispatches events to matching rules, executes resulting operations\nvia the ModuleRegistry. Also routes synthetic `CONDITION_ ` events\nwhen conditions transition.\n\nHandled operation types: `:command`, `:log`, `:emit`, `:demand`,\n`:assert_triple`, `:triple_query`, `:nextevent`, `:nextrule`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Owns no process lifecycles - Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher","doc":"The dispatcher `start_link`s nothing. Condition servers, capability\nservers, the triple store, the data store and the subscribable registry\nall live under `Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor`, and are resolved by\nchild id at the head of every callback (`refresh_stores/1`). The\ndispatcher's own state is a *derived* view, rebuildable at any moment\nfrom `Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore` — which is what `init/1` does, so that\na crash costs a dispatch cycle rather than every loaded policy.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#module-owns-no-process-lifecycles"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.condition_state/2","doc":"Returns the current boolean state of a named condition, or `nil` if not found.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#condition_state/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.data_member?/3","doc":"Returns true if `value` is a member of the named data block dataset.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#data_member?/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.deploy/4","doc":"Deploys `compiled` as the policy `id`, over whatever was last deployed\nunder that id.\n\nAfterwards the dispatcher holds what `compiled` declares and nothing the\nprevious version declared that it does not. A rule or condition whose\nfingerprint is unchanged keeps its state; see the `{:deploy, ...}`\ncallback.\n\nAnswers what `load/2` would. A policy that declares a name another policy\nholds is not loaded at all: `{:refused, error}`.\n\nSchedules are not touched: the scheduler is not the dispatcher's to call.\nThe caller follows with `Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.sync/1`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#deploy/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.describe/1","doc":"See `Anemos.Runtime.describe/1`. Everything but schedules and modules.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#describe/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.dispatch/4","doc":"Dispatches an event to all matching rules and conditions.\n\nReturns a list of `{:ok, operation}` or `{:error, reason}` tuples.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#dispatch/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.explain/4","doc":"See `Anemos.Runtime.explain/3`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#explain/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.invoke_verb/5","doc":"Resolves `mod_name` in `registry`, validates `args`, and invokes `verb`.\n\n**This is the only path from a `[MODULE::verb]` call to host code**, and\nit is deliberately public so that it can be shared rather than copied.\nBoth positions the language offers go through it:\n\n  * statement position — `execute_op/4`, on the operation list a rule\n    handler returned;\n  * expression position — `Anemos.Compiler`, from inside the compiled\n    closure, where the call's return value becomes the value of the\n    expression (A-56).\n\nSharing it is the point. A second implementation would be the fourth\ninstance of the defect shape A-39, A-59 and the duplicated `gate/3` all\nhad: a caller reaching the same destination by a path that skipped a\ncheck. Here the checks are `Anemos.Runtime.Module.schema_for/2`, the\n`Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema` bounds and allow-lists, and\n`Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard` confinement — all of them A-11's, and all of\nthem applied before `handle_verb/3` regardless of which position the call\nwas written in.\n\n`context` is the *verb* context — the event context stamped with\n`:_rule_source`. It is not the closure's evaluation context: `:data_store`\nand `:registry` reach the closure and must not reach host verb code. See\n`verb_context/2`.\n\nA `nil` registry answers `unknown_module` rather than raising. A compiled\nclosure invoked outside a dispatcher — a unit test, an embedder calling\n`handler.(event, %{}, %{})` directly — has no registry to be given, so\nthis is a reachable state and it fails closed with a message that says why\nrather than resolving to `nil` and letting a guard read false.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#invoke_verb/5"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.load/2","doc":"Loads compiled rules, conditions, and capabilities into a running dispatcher.\n\nDeclarations from different policies accumulate. A rule name that is\nalready loaded is **replaced**, not appended — see the `{:load, _}`\ncallback for why replacement rather than rejection.\n\nA name held by a policy deployed under an identity is not replaced: the\nload is refused whole, `{:refused, error}`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#load/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.policies/1","doc":"The loaded policies in load order, each with the names it declares. A load\nthat named no policy has `id: nil`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#policies/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.register_data_model/2","doc":"Registers an `Anemos.DataModel` handler under the keyword it answers from\n`keyword/0`. That keyword then names a format in a `data` block and an\nengine in a `query` block.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#register_data_model/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.register_subscribable/3","doc":"Registers a `Subscribable` handler module for the given protocol string.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#register_subscribable/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.rules/1","doc":"Returns the names of the rules currently loaded, without duplicates.\n\nA rule with several `when` blocks compiles to several rules sharing one\nname and is reported once.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#rules/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a dispatcher.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.start_link/1","doc":"* `:registry` — pid or name of a `ModuleRegistry` (required)\n  * `:store_supervisor` — pid or name of a `StoreSupervisor` (required)\n  * `:policy_store` — pid or name of a `PolicyStore` (required)\n  * `:rule_supervisor` — pid or name of a `RuleSupervisor` (optional)\n  * `:runtime` — the `Anemos.Runtime` name, handed to host verbs as\n    `:_runtime` so a verb can address the runtime it runs in (optional)\n  * `:name` — process registration name (optional)\n\nRules, conditions and capabilities are *not* passed here. They are read\nfrom the policy store, which is the only thing that survives a restart.\nUse `Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.start_link/1` to wire up a dispatcher and\nits stores outside a full `Anemos.Runtime` tree.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.topology/1","doc":"Returns the store supervisor and policy store this dispatcher reads from.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#topology/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.undeploy/2","doc":"Removes the policy `id` and everything it declared.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#undeploy/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.unload/2","doc":"Unloads the named rules.\n\nRemoves them from the rule set and the index, cancels any dwell timer they\nhave armed, drops their rule-scoped data blocks, and removes them from the\npolicy store so a dispatcher restart does not bring them back. Rule names\nthat are not loaded are ignored.\n\nRules only. Conditions, capabilities and global data blocks are not owned\nby a rule — a policy-level unload would need a policy identity, which\n`Anemos.CompilationResult` does not carry.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#unload/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.verb_context/2","doc":"Builds the context a host verb sees: the event context plus `:_rule_source`,\n`:_runtime` (the runtime's name, when run under one) and, added at the\ncall, `:_module` (the name the verb was addressed by).\n\nOne definition, used by `run_ops/5` for statement-position calls and by\n`dispatch_immediate/6` to precompute `:_verb_context` for expression-position\nones, so the two positions cannot drift into showing host code different\nthings. Note what it does *not* add: `:data_store` and `:registry` are\nclosure-evaluation authority and stop at the closure.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.html#verb_context/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration","doc":"Converts duration tuples (e.g., `{5, :min}`) to milliseconds.\n\nSupported units: `:ms`, `:s`, `:seconds`, `:min`, `:minutes`,\n`:h`, `:hours`, `:d`, `:days`.\n\n`to_ms/1` never raises: an unrecognised unit or a malformed term yields an\nerror tuple. This matters because callers run inside long-lived processes —\na `FunctionClauseError` here would take the scheduler down rather than\nreject one bad schedule. Use `to_ms!/1` where a bad unit should abort the\ncaller instead (compile time, say).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms/1","doc":"Converts a duration tuple to milliseconds.\n\nReturns the millisecond count, or `{:error, %Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct{}}` with\ncode `anemos.duration/unknown_unit` for an unrecognised unit, or\n`anemos.duration/invalid` for anything that is not a `{amount, unit}` pair.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration.html#to_ms/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms/1","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms({5, :min})\n    300_000\n\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms({2, :s})\n    2_000\n\n    iex> {:error, %Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct{code: code}} = Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms({2, :fortnights})\n    iex> code\n    \"anemos.duration/unknown_unit\"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration.html#to_ms/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms!/1","doc":"Converts a duration tuple to milliseconds, raising on anything unrecognised.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration.html#to_ms!/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms!/1","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.Duration.to_ms!({5, :min})\n    300_000","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Duration.html#to_ms!/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Errors","doc":"Compile-time error code registry for the Anemos runtime.\n\nDeclares structured error codes following the `namespace/error_name`\nconvention. All errors auto-enrich with `Comn.Contexts` when created\nduring a dispatch cycle.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Errors.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Introspection","doc":"Answers the introspection statements — `show`, `list`, `graph`, `trace`,\n`explain`, `exists`, `infer` — from the dispatcher's state.\n\nThe subject decides what is asked: a bare name is a module if one is\nbound to it, else an event if anything listens for it; a catalogue word\nnames what the runtime holds; anything else is a value. The statement\ndecides the question, after Comn: `show` is look, `explain` is recon,\n`list` is choices. See `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-03-introspection-design.md`.\n\nEvery answer is the operation with `value` filled in, and for a name,\n`found`: `:module`, `:event` or `nil`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Introspection.html"},{"type":"module","title":"A policy sees itself, and the modules - Anemos.Runtime.Introspection","doc":"Rules, conditions, schedules, policies and listeners are answered\nwithin the asking rule's own policy: what it declared, and who among\nits own rules listens. Another policy's names, contexts and condition\nstates are that policy's; the runtime-wide view belongs to the host\n(`Anemos.Runtime.describe/1`). Modules are the affordances every policy\nmay ask about. A rule loaded with no policy name sees the other\nanonymous loads, which share its standing.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Introspection.html#module-a-policy-sees-itself-and-the-modules"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Introspection.answer/3","doc":"The operation, answered for the rule named in `context[:_rule_source]`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Introspection.html#answer/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Introspection.statements/0","doc":"The statement types this answers.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Introspection.html#statements/0"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority","doc":"The trust boundary on loading policy (A-12).\n\n`Anemos.Runtime.load/3` takes an arbitrary string, compiles it to\nclosures, and installs it into a live dispatcher. Whoever controls that\nstring controls every verb the host has registered — including, per the\nworked example in `Anemos.Module`, the one that unlocks credentials.\nUnder the multi-tenant decision that makes loading *the* privileged\noperation of the runtime, and this module is where the privilege is\nchecked.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"What the gate actually consists of - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority","doc":"Three things, and it is worth being precise about which of them is a\nboundary and which is hygiene:\n\n1. **Loading is no longer reachable from the generic action surface.**\n   The runtime's `act` callback used to accept `%{action: :load, ...}`, so\n   anything that could reach the Comn action dispatcher could install\n   policy. It no longer does, and `choices/0` no longer advertises it.\n   This is a real reduction in reachability.\n\n2. **Loading names its authority.** `load/3` consults the module\n   configured as `config :anemos, :load_authority`, handing it an\n   `Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest`. That is what makes a load\n   *distinguishable* — an embedder can point the authority at a token, a\n   mTLS peer identity, a keyring, or a flat refusal, and every load in\n   the system goes through it. In-VM this is an API boundary, not a\n   sandbox: a caller who already has the BEAM can call the dispatcher\n   directly. The boundary that matters is the one between the runtime and\n   whatever accepts policy from outside it, and this is the seam that\n   boundary attaches to.\n\n3. **A ceiling no authority can waive.** `config :anemos,\n   :max_policy_bytes` (default 1 MiB) is checked before the source is\n   parsed, and is checked whether the authority permits or not. An\n   authority is an authorization decision; a resource ceiling is not\n   something an authorization decision should be able to lift.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#module-what-the-gate-actually-consists-of"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"The default is permissive, and says so - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority","doc":"`Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Permissive` grants everything, which\npreserves the contract every current embedder was written against. It is\nnamed rather than implicit precisely so that \"we never configured an\nauthority\" is a visible fact — `configured/0` and\nthe runtime's `recon` callback both report it — rather than an absence nobody\nnotices. An operator running multi-tenant is expected to replace it.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#module-the-default-is-permissive-and-says-so"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Writing one - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority","doc":"# in a module declaring @behaviour Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority\n    @impl true\n    def authorize(%{authorization: %{tenant: t}, signers: signers}) do\n      if Enum.all?(signers, &MyApp.Keyring.trusted?(t, &1)) do\n        :ok\n      else\n        {:error, :untrusted_signer}\n      end\n    end\n\n    def authorize(_request), do: {:error, :unauthenticated}\n\nRefuse by default and permit explicitly, not the other way round: an\nauthority whose fallthrough clause returns `:ok` is not an authority.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#module-writing-one"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Where A-05 slotted in - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority","doc":"`authorize/1` runs *after* the source is parsed, which is the only reason\n`LoadRequest.signers` can be populated at all. Signature verification is\ntherefore an authority implementation — it needs the signer names, the\nsource bytes to verify against, and a keyring, and it has all three. It\nlanded as `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed` and required no change to\n`load/3`, to `LoadRequest`, or to this behaviour, which is the evidence\nthe seam was in the right place.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#module-where-a-05-slotted-in"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.verdict/0","doc":"An authority's verdict.\n\n`:ok` permits the load. `{:error, reason}` refuses it; `reason` is logged\nand named in the caller's error, so it must not carry a credential.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#t:verdict/0"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.authorize/1","doc":"Decides whether `request` may be installed into the runtime. Refuse by default.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#c:authorize/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.authorize/1","doc":"Asks the configured authority to authorize `request`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#authorize/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.check_size/1","doc":"Enforces the ceiling. Runs before the source is parsed and regardless of\nthe authority.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#check_size/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.configured/0","doc":"Returns the configured authority module.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#configured/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.configured/0","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.configured()\n    Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Permissive","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#configured/0-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate/3","doc":"The gate. Checks the ceiling, parses, and authorizes; returns the AST.\n\n**This is the only supported way to obtain an AST for the compiler.**\nThat property is the point of the function, and it is stronger than any\namount of documentation asking callers not to skip the boundary. A\ncaller that parses on its own and compiles the result has reimplemented\na subset of loading and skipped the authority — which is precisely what\n`Anemos.run/2` did (A-59), and the same shape as A-39, where `nextrule`\nreimplemented a subset of dispatch and skipped the context filter. Both\nwere fixed by making one path the only path. Two assemblies of the same\n`LoadRequest` would reintroduce it at the next field added to the\nstruct, so there is one.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#gate/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Order, and why it is that order - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate/3","doc":"1. **Ceiling first**, before the parser is handed anything. Bounding\n   what an untrusted caller can make the parser allocate is the entire\n   point of having a ceiling; checking it after the parse would be\n   checking it after the damage.\n2. **Parse second.** This is what makes `signers` exist at all.\n3. **Authority third** — after the parse, because `LoadRequest.signers`\n   is the A-05 seam and cannot be populated any earlier; before the\n   compile, because compiling is what turns text into closures and\n   nothing unauthorized should get that far. Compiling is the caller's\n   job, and it happens on the AST this function returns.\n\n## `runtime`\n\nNames which runtime the load is destined for, so an authority can decide\nper tenant. `Anemos.run/2` passes `:standalone`: it builds a throwaway\ndispatcher and tears it down again, so it is not loading into any named\nruntime and does not claim to be. An authority keying on `runtime`\ntherefore sees the standalone path as its own thing and can refuse it\noutright — the correct default for a host that only means to permit\nloads into a runtime it named.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#gate/3-order-and-why-it-is-that-order"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate/3","doc":"* `:authorization` — passed through as `LoadRequest.authorization`. A\n    token, a peer identity, whatever the configured authority checks.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#gate/3-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Return values - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate/3","doc":"`{:ok, ast}` when the load may proceed. `{:error, reason}` otherwise:\na refusal — by the ceiling or by the authority — is a\n`Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct` with code `anemos.runtime/load_denied`, while\na parse failure keeps whatever shape `Anemos.Parser.parse/1` returned.\nThe two are worth telling apart: one is a boundary decision, the other\nis a broken rule file.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#gate/3-return-values"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate/3","doc":"iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate(:demo, ~s(rule r { when EV { log \"hi\" } }), [])\n    iex> length(ast.declarations)\n    1\n\n    iex> Application.put_env(:anemos, :max_policy_bytes, 4)\n    iex> {:error, err} = Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.gate(:demo, ~s(rule r { when EV { log \"hi\" } }), [])\n    iex> Application.delete_env(:anemos, :max_policy_bytes)\n    iex> err.code\n    \"anemos.runtime/load_denied\"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#gate/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.max_bytes/0","doc":"Returns the configured source-size ceiling in bytes.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#max_bytes/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.signers/1","doc":"Collects the `signed_by` names an AST declares, from both placements.\n\nSource-level `signed_by` (A-60) is the one that describes what a detached\nsignature actually covers, and the only one available to a source carrying no\n`rule`. Rule-level `signed_by` is still read: it names a signer the whole\nsource must carry, which is what it always did, and dropping it would silently\nweaken every policy already written against it.\n\nThe two are unioned and de-duplicated. All named signers are required — see\n`Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed` on why that is conjunctive — so which\nplacement a name arrived by cannot change the decision.\n\nEmpty when nothing in the source claims a signer, which is itself a fact\nan authority may want to refuse on.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#signers/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.signers/1","doc":"Source level, on a file that declares no rule at all — the case rule-only\nsyntax made impossible:\n\n    iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"signed_by :operator\\ncondition c { when EV { log \\\"x\\\" } }\")\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.signers(ast)\n    [\"operator\"]\n\nBoth placements union, and a name declared twice is counted once:\n\n    iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"signed_by :operator\\nrule r { signed_by :auditor\\nwhen EV { log \\\"x\\\" } }\")\n    iex> Enum.sort(Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.signers(ast))\n    [\"auditor\", \"operator\"]\n\n    iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"signed_by :op\\nrule r { signed_by :op\\nwhen EV { log \\\"x\\\" } }\")\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.signers(ast)\n    [\"op\"]\n\nA source claiming nothing answers `[]`, which is what `:unsigned_policy`\nacts on:\n\n    iex> {:ok, ast} = Anemos.Parser.parse(\"rule r { when EV { log \\\"x\\\" } }\")\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.signers(ast)\n    []","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.html#signers/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Denied","doc":"Refuses every load.\n\nFor a runtime whose policy set is installed once at boot through a\nprivileged path and must never change afterwards.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Denied.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Permissive","doc":"The default authority: grants every load.\n\nPreserves the pre-A-12 contract for embedders that have not configured\nanything. Replace it before accepting policy from anyone you would not\nhand a shell to.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Permissive.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"A load authority that verifies detached Ed25519 signatures over the\npolicy source (A-05).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html"},{"type":"module","title":"What was wrong - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"`signed_by :operator` was parsed into rule metadata and read by nothing.\nA rule declaring it looked authenticated to every human and every tool\nthat inspected the file, and was installed identically to one that\ndeclared nothing. That manufactures false provenance, which is worse\nthan having no signing at all — an unsigned file at least tells the\ntruth about itself.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#module-what-was-wrong"},{"type":"module","title":"The shape of the check - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"`Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.authorize/1` runs *after* the source is\nparsed and *before* it is compiled. After, because parsing is the only\nthing that can produce the `signed_by` names; before, because compiling\nis what turns text into closures, and nothing unauthorized should get\nthat far. That is precisely the position a signature check needs, so\nthis is an authority implementation and required no change to\n`Anemos.Runtime.load/3`, to `Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest`, or to the\nbehaviour.\n\nThe signature is **detached**: it travels in the existing\n`:authorization` option, not in the source. `signed_by` continues to\ncarry identity only, and the `.rule` file stays clean and diffable.\n\n    Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source,\n      authorization: %{signatures: %{\"operator\" => sig}})\n\nA single-signer shorthand is accepted too, and resolves to the sole\ndeclared signer:\n\n    Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source, authorization: %{signature: sig})\n\nEach signature is a raw 64-byte Ed25519 signature or its Base64\nencoding, computed over the **exact source bytes** — the same\n`request.source` the parser was handed, byte for byte, whitespace and\nall. Anything else and a re-serialised source could verify against a\nsignature made over different text.\n\nOther keys in the `:authorization` map are ignored, so this composes\nwith an authority scheme that also carries a tenant or a token.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#module-the-shape-of-the-check"},{"type":"module","title":"Public keys only, deliberately - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"Keys come from `Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore`, which holds\n`Comn.Secrets.Key` structs with `private: nil` and refuses anything\nelse. Producing signatures is explicitly **out of scope for this\nrepository**: Anemos verifies, something else signs.\n\nThat is not squeamishness. `comn` finding C-3 records that the Ed25519\nsigning private key is currently reused as ChaCha20 encryption key\nmaterial — a key-separation violation. A verifier that never holds a\nprivate key cannot compound it. Do not \"helpfully\" add a signing\nfunction here later; add it to whatever owns the private key, which is\nnot this runtime.\n\nOnly `:ed25519` keys verify. `Comn.Secrets.Key` also declares\n`:rsa_4096` and `:ecdsa_p256`; a signer whose key is one of those is\n**refused by name**, not skipped, because a signer that is silently\nnever checked is a signer that can never fail.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#module-public-keys-only-deliberately"},{"type":"module","title":"Every decision fails closed - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"| Situation | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Runtime has no trust store | refused |\n| Signer not in the keyring | refused |\n| Signer declared, no signature supplied | refused |\n| Signature not 64 bytes, raw or Base64 | refused |\n| Key is not Ed25519 | refused |\n| Signature does not verify | refused |\n| No `signed_by` anywhere | per `:unsigned_policy` |\n\nAn unknown signer is refused rather than permitted because under the\nmulti-tenant decision a name nobody has vouched for is a name with no\nauthority behind it. Permitting it would let any tenant mint provenance\nby inventing a signer.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#module-every-decision-fails-closed"},{"type":"module","title":"Multiple signers are conjunctive — all must verify - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"A source whose rules declare `:operator` and `:auditor` must carry a\nvalid signature from both. Not one, not a quorum: both.\n\nThe alternative reading — any one signature suffices — is weakened by\nthe attacker rather than by the defender. Under it, adding a\n`signed_by` naming a key you already control turns a source you could\nnot get loaded into one you can, and the union of every named signer's\ntrust becomes available to whoever names the most. Conjunctive is the\nonly reading where adding a name to a source cannot make it easier to\nload.\n\nNote that the signature covers the *whole source*, so per-rule\nattribution is not representable: `signed_by` names who must have\nsigned the file, not which rule they vouched for. A source that names\nany signer is never treated as unsigned, whatever `:unsigned_policy`\nsays — it is making a provenance claim, and `:permit` must not launder\nit.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#module-multiple-signers-are-conjunctive-all-must-verify"},{"type":"module","title":"Where signed_by may be written - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"At **source level**, outside any declaration, which is the placement\nthat matches what the signature covers:\n\n    signed_by :operator\n\n    condition night { ... }\n\nIt is also still accepted as rule metadata, where it means the same\nthing — a signer the whole source must carry — because that is what it\nhas always meant and every policy already written against it keeps\nworking. The two placements are unioned by\n`Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.signers/1`.\n\nSource level exists because rule metadata was the *only* placement\n(A-60), so a source carrying no `rule` — conditions, capabilities, data\nblocks — could not make a provenance claim and `:strict` could never\nload one. Since condition bodies execute side effects, such a file is a\nfull payload, so exempting it from `:strict` was not available either.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#module-where-signed_by-may-be-written"},{"type":"module","title":"Configuring it - Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed","doc":"config :anemos, :load_authority, Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed\n\n    {Anemos.Runtime,\n      name: :anemos,\n      keyring: %{\"operator\" => operator_public_key},\n      unsigned_policy: :strict}\n\nRefusals surface through `Anemos.Runtime.load/3` as a\n`Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct` with code `anemos.runtime/load_denied` —\n`:auth`, which is what a signature failure is. The reason names the\nsigner and the failure mode and carries no key material.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#module-configuring-it"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.refusal/0","doc":"Why a load was refused, inside this module. `decide/1` answers the same\nthing as a structured error whose code ends in the tag.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#t:refusal/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.decide/1","doc":"The verdict. A refusal is a structured error whose `code` is\n`anemos.trust/ ` — the modes are `t:refusal/0`'s tags — and whose\n`field` is the signer it concerns, where there is one.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed.html#decide/1"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest","doc":"Everything an `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority` gets to decide on.\n\nDeliberately a closed struct rather than a bare map: an authority is a\nsecurity decision point, and a decision point that pattern-matches on a\nmap is one field rename away from matching nothing and — depending on how\nit was written — failing open.\n\n`signers` is the seam for A-05, and it is now filled: an authority\nimplementation checks these names against a keyring, and neither this\nstruct nor `Anemos.Runtime.load/3` had to change to let it. See\n`Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Module","doc":"Behaviour for Anemos runtime modules (DEVICE, FILE, etc.).\n\nEach module handles verbs dispatched from compiled rule operations.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Argument validation - Anemos.Runtime.Module","doc":"`arg_schema/1` is optional. A module that implements it declares, per\nverb, what shape of argument list it is willing to receive; the\ndispatcher enforces that declaration *before* `handle_verb/3` is called,\nso a malformed or hostile argument never reaches host code. Returning\n`nil` for a verb — or not implementing the callback at all — leaves that\nverb unvalidated, which is what keeps modules written before A-11 working\nunchanged.\n\nThe check lives in the framework rather than in each module on purpose:\narguments come from untrusted policy text and untrusted event payloads,\nand a boundary that every host module author has to remember to build for\nthemselves is a boundary that will be missing somewhere.\n\nSee `Anemos.Runtime.ArgSchema` for the schema vocabulary, and\n`Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard` for confining a path-taking verb to a root.\n\n    # in a module implementing this behaviour\n    @impl true\n    def arg_schema(\"read\"), do: [[type: :string, path: :spool]]\n    def arg_schema(_verb), do: nil\n\n    @impl true\n    def handle_verb(\"read\", [path], _context), do: File.read(path)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html#module-argument-validation"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.verb_result/0","doc":"What a verb answers: a host value, or a reason the verb refused.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html#t:verb_result/0"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.arg_schema/1","doc":"Returns the argument schema for `verb`, or `nil` to leave it unvalidated.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html#c:arg_schema/1"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.describe/1","doc":"What a policy learns from `show NAME`, `explain NAME` and `list NAME`:\n`%{look: String.t(), recon: map(), choices: map()}`, Comn's three\nquestions. For a module bound under several names — a bridge that serves\nevery discovered tool — this answers per name, which Comn's own\nnullary callbacks cannot.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html#c:describe/1"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.handle_verb/3","doc":"Executes `verb` with `args` already validated against `arg_schema/1`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html#c:handle_verb/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.card/2","doc":"A module's card for the name it is bound under: `describe/1` if it has\none, else its `Comn` callbacks, else what the runtime knows — the name,\nthe Elixir module, and the verbs declared with `use Anemos.Module`.\n\nThe module's own code runs in a process of its own with a deadline\n(1000 ms): it is host code, called from inside the dispatcher on a\npolicy's say-so, and a card that raises, blocks or comes back in the\nwrong shape is a card the runtime did not get, not a dead dispatcher.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html#card/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.schema_for/2","doc":"Returns `module`'s schema for `verb`, or `nil` when it declares none.\n\nAnswers `nil` for a module that does not implement the optional callback,\nwhich is the pre-A-11 behaviour and is why old modules keep working.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Module.html#schema_for/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry","doc":"Maps module names (e.g. \"DEVICE\", \"FILE\") to Elixir modules\nimplementing the `Anemos.Runtime.Module` behaviour.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Trust model - Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry","doc":"This table decides where every `[MODULE::verb ...]` in every loaded policy\nexecutes. It is reachable by a documented public name\n(`:\"#{runtime_name}.registry\"`), so any process in the VM can address it.\nRegistration is therefore **write-once**: binding a name that is already\nlive is refused and logged, so a later `register/3` cannot silently\nredirect `\"SECRET\"` or `\"INFRA\"` at a module of someone else's choosing\nwhile the policy continues to look normal from outside.\n\nThree outcomes:\n\n  * name unbound — bound, logged at `:info`\n  * name bound to the *same* module — idempotent `:ok` (host code and\n    tests re-register defaults freely)\n  * name bound to a *different* module — an\n    `anemos.runtime/already_registered` error, logged at `:warning`,\n    binding unchanged\n\nA deliberate rebind is still possible, but it has to say so:\n`register(reg, name, module, replace: true)`. That path also logs at\n`:warning`, because a verb changing destination mid-flight is an event an\noperator should see either way.\n\nThis raises the bar from \"any process can rebind\" to \"any process can\nrebind loudly, and only by asking\". It is not an authorization check —\nthere is no caller identity to check against. A registry that must not be\nwritten by arbitrary code should not be given a public name.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#module-trust-model"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.lookup/2","doc":"Looks up a module by name.\n\nReturns `{:ok, module}` or `:error`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#lookup/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.lookup/2","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.start_link([])\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.lookup(reg, \"MISSING\")\n    :error","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#lookup/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.names/1","doc":"Lists every bound module name, sorted. For auditing what a runtime can reach.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#names/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.names/1","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.start_link([])\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.names(reg)\n    []","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#names/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.register/4","doc":"Registers an Elixir module under the given name (e.g., `\"DEVICE\"`).\n\nWrite-once. Re-registering the same module is idempotent; binding a live\nname to a different module is refused unless `replace: true` is given.\nEvery outcome is logged. See the module docs for the trust model.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#register/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.register/4","doc":"* `:replace` — when `true`, deliberately rebind a live name (default `false`)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#register/4-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.register/4","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.start_link([])\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.register(reg, \"DEVICE\", Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device)\n    :ok\n    iex> {:error, error} =\n    ...>   Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.register(reg, \"DEVICE\", Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File)\n    iex> error.code\n    \"anemos.runtime/already_registered\"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#register/4-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.registered?/2","doc":"Returns true if `name` is currently bound.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#registered?/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.registered?/2","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.start_link([])\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.registered?(reg, \"DEVICE\")\n    false","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#registered?/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a module registry.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (optional)\n  * `:state_table` — an `Anemos.Runtime.StateTable` the bindings are kept\n    in, so that this process restarting does not lose them (A-43).\n    Optional; without it a restarted registry is empty.\n  * `:modules` — `%{name => module}` bound at start, under whatever the\n    table remembers (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device","doc":"Stub DEVICE module for development and testing.\n\nAll verbs echo back their inputs as `%{module: \"DEVICE\", verb: verb, args: args}`.\nReplace with a real implementation for production use.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Device.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example","doc":"Demonstration doubles for the module verbs used by `priv/examples/`.\n\nThese exist for one reason: the shipped examples referenced `PDF`,\n`STRING`, `INFRA`, `SECRET` and `EVENT`, nothing registered them, and so\ntwo of the three examples could not run at all. They are doubles, not\nimplementations — every verb here computes from its arguments and from\nthe `:context_data` the caller supplied, and none of them opens a file,\na socket, or a keystore.\n\nThey are **not** registered by default. `Anemos.example_modules/0`\nreturns them and `mix anemos.run --stub-modules` opts into them, because\na `SECRET` handler that answers plausibly is precisely the thing that\nshould never be ambient. `Example.Secret.unlock/2` therefore returns a\nself-identifying marker rather than anything secret-shaped: a rule that\ndepends on real key material will visibly get a stub, not a value it can\nmistake for one.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Supplying inputs - Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example","doc":"Read verbs resolve against `:context_data` — the same map `?var` reads —\nso an example is driven entirely from the command line:\n\n    mix anemos.run priv/examples/normalize-pdfs.rule \\\n      --event FILE_CREATED --context /papers --stub-modules \\\n      --context-data file=/papers/Draft_Report.pdf\n\nWrite verbs echo the operation they *would* perform, as the shipped\n`DEVICE` and `FILE` stubs already do. An echo is the honest answer for a\ndouble: it reports the decision the rule reached without pretending the\neffect happened.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.html#module-supplying-inputs"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.datum/3","doc":"Reads one key from the dispatch context's `:context_data`, or `default`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.html#datum/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.datum/3","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.datum(%{context_data: %{\"file\" => \"a.pdf\"}}, \"file\", \"\")\n    \"a.pdf\"\n\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.datum(%{}, \"file\", \"none\")\n    \"none\"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.html#datum/3-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Event","doc":"`EVENT` double. Verb names are dotted event names (`ip.addr.changed`),\nso `handle_verb/3` accepts any verb and echoes it rather than\nenumerating a fixed list.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Event.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.File","doc":"`FILE` double that answers `extension` from the path under evaluation.\n\nOverrides `Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File`, which echoes every verb. That\necho is what made `normalize-pdfs.rule`'s outer guard\n(`if [FILE::extension] eq \"pdf\"`) permanently false: a map never equals\n`\"pdf\"`. Here `extension` is real string computation over a path taken\nfrom the argument list or, when called with no arguments, from\n`context_data[\"file\"]`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.File.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.File.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.File.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.File.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.File.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Infra","doc":"`INFRA` double for `ddns.rule`.\n\n`get_ip` reads addresses out of `:context_data` rather than querying an\ninterface, keyed `ip_ _ ` (for example\n`--context-data ip_eth0_ipv4=203.0.113.7`). `ddns_update` echoes the\nupdate it was handed; it contacts no provider.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Infra.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Infra.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Infra.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Infra.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Infra.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Pdf","doc":"`PDF` double distinguishing document content from stored metadata.\n\n`normalize-pdfs.rule` exists to demonstrate that the two can disagree,\nso this double keeps them in separate keys: `title` is the title the\ndocument *content* implies (defaulting to a human-readable form of the\nfilename), while `metadata title` is what is *recorded*. Supply\n`--context-data meta_title=...` to make them differ and drive the\nexample's inner branch.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Pdf.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Pdf.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Pdf.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Pdf.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Pdf.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Secret","doc":"`SECRET` double that deliberately yields nothing usable.\n\nEvery other double here answers plausibly, because a plausible answer\nis what lets an example demonstrate its rule. This one must not: a\nstub that returns something secret-shaped lets a policy run to\ncompletion against fake credentials and report success. It returns a\nself-describing marker instead, so the substitution is visible in the\noutput wherever the value lands.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Secret.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Secret.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Secret.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Secret.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.Secret.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String","doc":"`STRING` double. `slugify` is a genuine pure function, not a stand-in —\nthere is nothing to fake about lowercasing and hyphenating a string.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.slugify/1","doc":"Lowercases, strips anything that is not alphanumeric or a separator,\nand collapses separators into single hyphens.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.html#slugify/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.slugify/1","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.slugify([\"Draft Report (v2)\"])\n    \"draft-report-v2\"\n\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.slugify([])\n    \"\"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Example.String.html#slugify/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File","doc":"Stub FILE module for development and testing.\n\nAll verbs echo back their inputs as `%{module: \"FILE\", verb: verb, args: args}`.\nReplace with a real implementation for production use.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Before you make this real - Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File","doc":"The schemas below bound *shape* — the argument is a string, and it is not\na megabyte long. That is all a stub can honestly claim, because a stub\ntouches no filesystem.\n\nA real implementation must add `path: :some_root` to every path-taking\nverb and configure the root:\n\n    config :anemos, :path_roots, %{files: \"/var/lib/anemos/files\"}\n\n    def arg_schema(\"read\"), do: [[type: :string, max_bytes: 4096, path: :files]]\n\nWithout it, `[FILE::read \"../../etc/shadow\"]` is a working traversal the\nmoment this module calls `File.read/1` — the host repo layer this would\nsit on does no normalization and no confinement of its own (A-11, C-4).\n`Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard` is the check; the schema is how you ask the\ndispatcher to apply it for you.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File.html#module-before-you-make-this-real"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.File.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Policy","doc":"`[POLICY::unload \"id\"]` — a policy that removes another.\n\nRegistered as `POLICY` in every `Anemos.Runtime`. This is the gated way\nto unload: the rule that calls it came through `Anemos.Runtime.load/3`\nand whatever authority stands there, so a signed policy can retire\nanother by name without `unload/2` needing an authority of its own\n(A-81).\n\nThe unload is asked for, not done on the spot: the verb runs inside the\ndispatcher, which is the process that has to do the removing. It is\ncarried out as soon as the dispatch that asked for it has finished.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Policy.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Policy.__anemos_name__/0","doc":"Returns the module name as registered in Anemos (e.g., `\"SECRET\"`).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Policy.html#__anemos_name__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Policy.__anemos_verbs__/0","doc":"Returns the list of verb names this module handles.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Modules.Policy.html#__anemos_verbs__/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD","doc":"Natural Language Date resolver.\n\nResolves `NLD_ ` expressions to `NaiveDateTime` values.\nAll times are system-local.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Supported expressions - Anemos.Runtime.NLD","doc":"| Expression | Resolves to |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| `tomorrow` | Next day at 00:00:01 |\n| `yesterday` | Previous day at 00:00:01 |\n| `today` | Current day at 00:00:01 |\n| `next.monday` .. `next.sunday` | Next occurrence of that day at 00:00:01 |\n| `next.week` | Next monday at 00:00:01 |\n| `next.month` | First of next month at 00:00:01 |\n| `next.year` | January 1 of next year at 00:00:01 |\n| `end.of.month` | Last day of current month at 23:59:59 |\n| `end.of.week` | Next sunday at 23:59:59 |","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD.html#module-supported-expressions"},{"type":"module","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.NLD","doc":"iex> {:ok, dt} = Anemos.Runtime.NLD.resolve(\"today\")\n    iex> dt.hour\n    0\n\n    iex> {:error, %Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct{code: \"anemos.nld/unknown_expression\"}} =\n    ...>   Anemos.Runtime.NLD.resolve(\"gobbledygook\")","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD.html#module-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD.resolve/1","doc":"Resolves a natural language date expression to a `NaiveDateTime`.\n\nReturns `{:ok, naive_datetime}` or an `anemos.nld/unknown_expression` error.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD.html#resolve/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD.resolve_ms/1","doc":"Resolves a natural language date to milliseconds from now.\n\nReturns `{:ok, milliseconds}` or an `anemos.nld/unknown_expression` error.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.NLD.html#resolve_ms/1"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard","doc":"Confines a caller-supplied path to a configured root.\n\nPolicy text is untrusted, so every path that reaches a host filesystem\ncall arrived from an attacker-controlled string. `Comn.Repo.File.Local`\ncalls `File.open/2`, `File.stream!/1` and `File.rm/1` on whatever it is\ngiven, with no normalization and no root — wire that to a real `FILE`\nverb and `[FILE::read \"../../etc/shadow\"]` is a working traversal on day\none (A-11, C-4).\n\nThis module is the one place that decides whether a path is inside a\nroot, for the same reason `Anemos.Runtime.ContextScope` is the one place\nthat decides whether a context is inside another: two copies of a\nboundary check are two chances to fix only one of them.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Roots are named, not passed - Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard","doc":"Host modules name a root; the operator configures where it points:\n\n    config :anemos, :path_roots, %{spool: \"/var/lib/anemos/spool\"}\n\nA verb declares `path: :spool` in its `arg_schema/1` and the dispatcher\nconfines the argument before `handle_verb/3` is called. A module doing\nits own filesystem work calls `confine/2` or `confine_root/2` directly.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#module-roots-are-named-not-passed"},{"type":"module","title":"It fails closed - Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard","doc":"An unconfigured root is an `anemos.path/unknown_root` error, not \"no root\ndeclared, therefore allow\". A verb that asked for confinement and did not\nget it must not run — that is the whole point of asking.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#module-it-fails-closed"},{"type":"module","title":"Segment comparison, not string prefix - Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard","doc":"`/srv/root` and `/srv/rootless` share a string prefix and share no\ncontainment. The check appends a separator before comparing, so a sibling\ndirectory whose name extends the root's cannot pass (the same defect\n`ContextScope` was fixed for in A-06).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#module-segment-comparison-not-string-prefix"},{"type":"module","title":"What it does not defend against - Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard","doc":"Symlinks. `Path.expand/2` is lexical: it resolves `.` and `..` textually\nand never touches the filesystem, so a symlink *inside* the root pointing\noutside it still escapes. Closing that needs `:file.read_link_info/1` at\nevery level and is racy against a concurrent rename regardless. The\ndurable answer is a filesystem-level confinement (a bind mount, a\ncontainer, an unprivileged uid whose reachable set *is* the root); this\nfunction stops the string-level traversal, which is the one a policy\nauthor can perform without already having filesystem access.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#module-what-it-does-not-defend-against"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine/2","doc":"Expands `path` against `root` and asserts the result lies inside it.\n\nReturns the expanded absolute path, which is what the caller should use\nfrom then on — handing the unexpanded string onward would let the callee\nre-resolve it differently and reopen the hole this just closed.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#confine/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine/2","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine(\"notes/a.txt\", \"/srv/data\")\n    {:ok, \"/srv/data/notes/a.txt\"}\n\n    iex> {:error, error} = Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine(\"../../etc/shadow\", \"/srv/data\")\n    iex> error.code\n    \"anemos.path/outside_root\"\n\n    iex> {:error, error} = Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine(\"/etc/shadow\", \"/srv/data\")\n    iex> error.code\n    \"anemos.path/outside_root\"\n\n    iex> {:error, error} = Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine(\"/srv/dataless/x\", \"/srv/data\")\n    iex> error.code\n    \"anemos.path/outside_root\"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#confine/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine_root/2","doc":"Same as `confine/2`, but names a root from `config :anemos, :path_roots`.\n\nAn unconfigured root denies. See the moduledoc.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#confine_root/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine_root/2","doc":"iex> {:error, error} = Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.confine_root(\"a.txt\", :nowhere)\n    iex> {error.code, error.field}\n    {\"anemos.path/unknown_root\", \"nowhere\"}","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#confine_root/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.roots/0","doc":"Returns the configured root map.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard.html#roots/0"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore","doc":"Durable record of everything that has been loaded into a runtime.\n\nThe dispatcher holds a *derived* view — a rule index, a set of condition\nservers, a set of capability servers. That view is disposable. This store\nholds the source it is derived from, so that a dispatcher which crashes\nand is restarted by its supervisor can rebuild rather than come back\nblank.\n\nWithout it, re-parenting the dispatcher's children (see\n`Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor`) only narrows the window: any *other*\ndispatcher crash still produced a restarted dispatcher with `rules: []`,\nand `dispatch/3` then returned `{:ok, []}` forever — byte-identical to\n\"no rule matched\".\n\nIt also holds the channel connector, which is set through the dispatcher\nat runtime and would otherwise silently revert to the default on restart.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.entry/0","doc":"One loaded policy. `id` is `nil` for a load that named no policy.\n`fingerprints` maps `{:rule | :condition, name}` to a digest of the\ndeclaration's text, which is how a deploy tells an edited declaration from\none that merely sits in an edited file.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#t:entry/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.all/1","doc":"Returns every loaded compilation result in load order.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#all/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.connector/1","doc":"Returns the recorded channel connector.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#connector/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.delete/2","doc":"Forgets the policy `id`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.drop_rules/2","doc":"Removes the named rules from every stored compilation result.\n\nThis is what makes `Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.unload/2` durable. The\ndispatcher's rule list is derived and rebuilt from this store on restart,\nso an unload that touched only the derived view would be undone by the\nnext dispatcher crash — the rule would come back.\n\nOnly rules are removed. A compilation result also carries conditions,\ncapabilities and data blocks, which are not owned by any one rule and are\nnot implied by unloading one. A result left with no rules is kept for the\nsame reason.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#drop_rules/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.entries/1","doc":"Every loaded policy in load order, with its identity.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#entries/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.put/2","doc":"Appends a compilation result. Load order is preserved — rebuilding replays\nit, so later policies shadow earlier ones exactly as they did on first load.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#put/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.put/4","doc":"Records `compiled` as the policy `id`, in place of whatever was recorded\nunder that id. The policy moves to the end of the load order, which is\nwhere its rules go in the dispatcher's live list when they are replaced.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#put/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.put_connector/2","doc":"Records the channel connector so it survives a dispatcher restart.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#put_connector/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a policy store.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (optional)\n  * `:state_table` — an `Anemos.Runtime.StateTable` the record is kept\n    in. This store survives a dispatcher crash on its own; the table is\n    what lets it survive its *own* restart, which the supervisor forces\n    whenever a child ahead of it — the module registry, say — dies\n    (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Report","doc":"What an event *reports*, and what a compound trigger makes of the reports\nit has heard (A-47).\n\nAn event arriving is a report: true or false at the moment it is made. It\nis true unless its payload carries a falsy `state` — the convention the\ndwell timer already reads. That report stands as the event's state until\nthe next report of the same event; there is no other way for it to change,\nand an event that has never reported is false.\n\n`when (A AND B)` names a compound state over those reports. It is true if\nand only if every operand is true as last reported, and it is evaluated at\neach report of any operand. `OR` is true if any operand is.\n\nThe listener keeps the reports, not the event: a rule hears only what its\ncontext admits, so two rules may hold different reports of the same event.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.reports/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.html#t:reports/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.truthy?/1","doc":"Whether the event arriving with `context` reports true.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.html#truthy?/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.Report.truthy?/1","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.Report.truthy?(%{})\n    true\n\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.Report.truthy?(%{context_data: %{\"state\" => false}})\n    false","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.html#truthy?/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.value/3","doc":"The compound state of `events` under `op`, given the last `reports`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.html#value/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.Report.value/3","doc":"iex> Anemos.Runtime.Report.value(:and, [\"A\", \"B\"], %{\"A\" => true})\n    false\n\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.Report.value(:and, [\"A\", \"B\"], %{\"A\" => true, \"B\" => true})\n    true\n\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.Report.value(:or, [\"A\", \"B\"], %{\"A\" => false, \"B\" => true})\n    true","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Report.html#value/3-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor","doc":"Second supervision tier: rule-scoped processes.\n\n`Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor` holds everything whose lifetime is the\nruntime's. This supervisor holds everything whose lifetime is a *rule's*.\nThe distinction matters on failure: a rule-scoped store that dies should\ntake down that rule's scope and nothing else, whereas a shared store\ncoming back empty affects every rule at once.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html"},{"type":"module","title":"What actually runs here - Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor","doc":"Nothing, until a rule needs it.\n\n`ensure_scope/2` starts a child `Supervisor` for one named rule and\nreturns its pid; a second call for the same rule returns the same pid\nwithout starting anything. Rules that consume only shared infrastructure\n— which, in the current interpreter, is all of them — never cause a child\nto be started. `scope/2` returns `nil` for such a rule rather than\ncreating one on read.\n\nThis replaces the previous moduledoc claim that the module was \"ready to\nsupervise per-rule GenServers\": it was an empty `DynamicSupervisor` that\nnothing started children in and nothing resolved children from.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#module-what-actually-runs-here"},{"type":"module","title":"Restart strategy - Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor","doc":"`:one_for_one` at this tier — rule scopes are independent of one another,\nand one policy's failure must not restart another policy's state.\n\nEach per-rule scope is `:transient`: it is restarted if it crashes, but a\nscope that is deliberately torn down by `stop_scope/2` (policy unloaded)\nstays down.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#module-restart-strategy"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.ensure_scope/2","doc":"Returns the scope supervisor for `rule_name`, starting it if absent.\n\nThe returned pid is a plain `Supervisor` with no children; rule-scoped\nstores are added to it with `Supervisor.start_child/2`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#ensure_scope/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.scope/2","doc":"Returns the scope supervisor for `rule_name`, or `nil` if the rule has\nnever needed one. Never starts anything.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#scope/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.scopes/1","doc":"Returns `[{rule_name, pid}]` for every live rule scope.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#scopes/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the rule supervisor.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (defaults to `__MODULE__`)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.stop_scope/2","doc":"Tears down a rule's scope and everything in it. Idempotent.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.RuleSupervisor.html#stop_scope/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler","doc":"GenServer that manages time-triggered schedules.\n\nHolds compiled `at` specs, manages timers via `Process.send_after/3` and\n`:timer.send_interval/2`, and dispatches synthetic `AT_*` events into the\nruntime when timers fire.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Resource limits - Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler","doc":"Schedule specs come from policy text, which is not necessarily trusted.\nTwo limits bound what a policy can cost the runtime:\n\n  * **Interval floor** (`:min_interval_ms`, default 1000). Every firing of\n    an interval schedule becomes a synchronous dispatch through a single\n    dispatcher process, so a `1ms` interval is a 1000 Hz loop that starves\n    every other rule. Intervals below the floor are **rejected, not\n    clamped**: silently running a schedule at a rate the policy did not ask\n    for makes the runtime's behaviour diverge from its policy text with\n    nothing to observe. Rejection tells the author their schedule is\n    unrunnable; clamping does not.\n\n  * **Schedule cap** (`:max_schedules`, default 1000) on the number of\n    armed timers per scheduler, enforced cumulatively across calls.\n\nBoth default from application env (`:anemos`, `:min_schedule_interval_ms`\nand `:max_schedules`) and may be overridden per scheduler at `start_link/1`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#module-resource-limits"},{"type":"module","title":"Schedules that cannot be armed - Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler","doc":"A past-dated schedule, an unresolvable natural-language date, or an\nunrecognised duration unit yields a schedule that will never fire. These do\nnot report `:ok` — `schedule/2` returns `{:error, failures}` and logs a\nwarning for each. Arming is otherwise best-effort per spec: one bad entry\ndoes not prevent the rest of the batch from being armed, since a batch is\nthe compiled output of a whole policy and discarding all of it for one bad\ndate would be worse than partial arming plus a loud report.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#module-schedules-that-cannot-be-armed"},{"type":"module","title":"One timer per event name - Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler","doc":"A schedule is identified by its `event_name`, and that name is the whole\nof its identity here: the compiler derives it from the time spec alone, so\n`at 08:00` in two unrelated rules — in two unrelated policy files —\nproduces two specs both named `AT_08_00`.\n\nArming both would be wrong twice over. The timers map is keyed by event\nname, so the second `Map.put` used to displace the first entry while its\ntimer stayed armed: an uncancellable timer firing forever, dispatching an\nevent nothing in the map accounts for (A-19). And the fix is not to key\nthe map more finely, because the *event* is genuinely shared — one\n`AT_08_00` dispatch already reaches every rule subscribed to `AT_08_00`.\nTwo timers would fire every such rule twice.\n\nSo arming an event name that is already armed **replaces** it: the\ndisplaced timer is cancelled first, and the replacement does not consume\ncap headroom. Re-loading the same policy is therefore idempotent with\nrespect to timers.\n\nWhat this cannot fix from here: because the event name carries no rule\nidentity, the scheduler has no way to distinguish `at 08:00` in rule A\nfrom `at 08:00` in rule B, and `unschedule/2` therefore disarms the time,\nnot the rule. That requires the compiler to derive schedule names from\nrule identity as well as time spec.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#module-one-timer-per-event-name"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.failure_reason/0","doc":"Why a schedule spec could not be armed.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#t:failure_reason/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.fired_at/2","doc":"When the schedule `event_name` last fired, or nil.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#fired_at/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.forget/2","doc":"Forgets that the named schedules fired, so a one-shot among them can arm again.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#forget/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.schedule/2","doc":"Adds schedule specs to the running scheduler. Timers start immediately.\n\nReturns `:ok` when every spec was armed, or `{:error, failures}` where\n`failures` is a `Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct` for each spec that was not: its `code` is\n`anemos.scheduler/ ` and its `field` the schedule's event name.\nSpecs not named in `failures` were armed.\n\nA spec whose `event_name` is already armed replaces it — see the moduledoc.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#schedule/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.schedules/1","doc":"Returns the specs currently armed, one per event name.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#schedules/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the scheduler.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (optional)\n  * `:runtime_name` — the Anemos.Runtime name for dispatching events (required)\n  * `:policy_store` — the runtime's `PolicyStore`; a scheduler given one\n    arms every loaded schedule when it starts, so its own restart does\n    not leave `at` blocks that never fire (optional)\n  * `:state_table` — an `Anemos.Runtime.StateTable`. Each firing is\n    recorded there, and a one-shot schedule that has fired is not armed\n    again when the scheduler restarts or reconciles (optional)\n  * `:dispatch_fn` — override dispatch function for testing (optional)\n  * `:min_interval_ms` — interval floor (optional, see moduledoc)\n  * `:max_schedules` — cap on armed timers (optional, see moduledoc)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.sync/1","doc":"Makes what is armed match what is loaded: arms every schedule in the\npolicy store that is not armed, disarms every timer no loaded policy\ndeclares, and leaves the rest running.\n\nThe policy store is read here, inside the scheduler, and not by the\ncaller. Two deploys finishing at once each call this after recording\nthemselves, so whichever call runs last sees both — a caller that computed\n\"arm this, disarm that\" from its own view could disarm a time the other\nhad just picked up.\n\nAnswers like `schedule/2`, with a failure for every loaded spec that could\nnot be armed. A scheduler started without a `:policy_store` does nothing.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#sync/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.unschedule/2","doc":"Disarms the timers for the named events and forgets their specs.\n\nIdempotent: an event name that is not armed is skipped. This is the\ncounterpart `load/2` needed and did not have — without it, unloading a\npolicy left its `at` blocks firing indefinitely.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Scheduler.html#unschedule/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Standalone","doc":"Wires a dispatcher and its stores together outside a full `Anemos.Runtime`\ntree.\n\n`Anemos.Runtime.Dispatcher` deliberately starts nothing: its stores belong\nto `Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor` and its policy set to\n`Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore`. That is the right shape for a supervised\nruntime, but it makes the one-shot case — `Anemos.run/2`, and tests that\nwant a dispatcher and nothing else — a three-step dance. This module is\nthat dance, in one call.\n\nEverything it starts is linked to the caller, so a test process exiting\ntakes the whole set with it. `stop/1` tears the set down explicitly for\ncallers that outlive it — without which `Anemos.run/2` leaked its stores\non every invocation, since `GenServer.stop/1` exits `:normal` and\nnon-trapping linked processes ignore that signal.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a policy store, a store supervisor and a dispatcher, and returns\nthe dispatcher.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.start_link/1","doc":"* `:registry` — pid or name of a `ModuleRegistry` (required)\n  * `:rules`, `:conditions`, `:capabilities`, `:data_blocks` — seeded into\n    the policy store before the dispatcher starts, so a dispatcher restart\n    rebuilds them (all optional)\n  * `:channel_connector` — recorded in the policy store (optional)\n  * `:subscribable_registry` — an existing registry whose contents are\n    copied into the supervised one (optional)\n  * `:name` — dispatcher registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.stop/1","doc":"Stops a dispatcher started by `start_link/1` and everything under it.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Standalone.html#stop/1"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable","doc":"Where a runtime keeps what its processes have accumulated, so that the\nrestart of one of them does not lose it.\n\n`Anemos.Runtime.PolicyStore` holds what was *loaded*. This holds what has\n*happened* since: `set` bindings and armed dwell timers (A-40, A-41), each\ncondition's boolean (A-42), and the module registrations (A-43).\n\nIt is an ETS table owned by the runtime's supervisor process. Every child\nthat writes to it can crash and be restarted without the table going\nanywhere; it dies only with the runtime itself. A process could not do\nthis job: the module registry is already a process, and a second one\nbehind it would just move the question.\n\nThe table is public, because its writers are the supervisor's children.\nThat grants nothing new — everything in it was already reachable by\ncalling the process that held it, under a name just as public.\n\nEvery function accepts `nil` for the table and does nothing, so a\ndispatcher, registry or condition server started on its own, outside an\n`Anemos.Runtime`, works as before and simply keeps no durable state.\n\nIt does not survive the node. Persisting it to disk is a separate\ndecision — a `set` binding can hold an `Anemos.Secret`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.delete/2","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.get/3","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#get/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.name_for/1","doc":"The table of the runtime `runtime`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#name_for/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.new/1","doc":"Creates the table. Call from the process that should own it.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#new/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.push/4","doc":"Appends `entry` to the bounded log `kind`, dropping the oldest once there\nare more than `limit`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#push/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.put/3","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#put/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.recent/3","doc":"The newest `limit` entries of the log `kind`, newest first.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StateTable.html#recent/3"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor","doc":"Supervisor for stores-as-infrastructure.\n\nOwns every process the dispatcher reads from or writes to but does not\nitself represent: the `TripleStore`, the `DataStore`, the\n`SubscribableRegistry`, and one `ConditionServer` / `CapabilityServer`\nper declaration in the loaded policy set.\n\nThese processes were previously `start_link`ed from inside\nthe dispatcher's initialization callback. Because the dispatcher does not trap\nexits, the death of any one of them killed the dispatcher, and the\ndispatcher's own restart came back with an empty rule set — silently\ndestroying every loaded policy. Re-parenting them here severs that link:\na store crash is now a local, recoverable event.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Resolution, not caching - Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor","doc":"Children are addressed by supervisor child id, never by cached pid:\n\n  * `:triple_store`, `:data_store`, `:subscribable_registry`\n  * `{:condition, name}`, `{:capability, name}`\n\n`whereis/2` and `snapshot/1` read the live child list, so a restarted\nchild is picked up on the next dispatch rather than leaving the caller\nholding a dead pid. The cost is one supervisor call per dispatch cycle,\nwhich is cheap next to the rule bodies it precedes.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#module-resolution-not-caching"},{"type":"module","title":"Restart strategy - Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor","doc":"`:one_for_one` — the children are mutually independent. A `TripleStore`\ncrash tells you nothing about the `DataStore`, and restarting healthy\nstores would discard state for no reason.\n\nEvery child is `:permanent`. All of them are infrastructure the runtime\nneeds in order to interpret a policy at all; none has a \"finished\nnormally\" state. Restarted stores come back empty, which is the correct\nsafe default: a condition that has lost its history reads `false`, and a\ndata block that has lost its dataset reports no members.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#module-restart-strategy"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.delete/2","doc":"Stops and forgets the server for a condition or capability that is no\nlonger declared. Idempotent.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.put_capability/2","doc":"Starts (or replaces) the capability server for a compiled capability.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#put_capability/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.put_condition/5","doc":"Starts (or replaces) the condition server for a compiled condition.\n\nReplacement rather than reuse is deliberate: `on_transition` closes over\nthe dispatcher pid, so a dispatcher that has just restarted must install\ncallbacks pointing at itself. The condition's boolean state resets to\n`false` in the process, which is the safe reading.\n\n`on_side_effects` is the second such callback, and it exists for the same\nreason `on_transition` does: the condition server can compute a body's\noperations but cannot execute one — routing an `emit` or a `[MOD::verb]`\nis the dispatcher's job. Omitting it leaves the operations dropped, which\nis what A-50 was.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#put_condition/5"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.snapshot/1","doc":"Reads the whole child list in one call and shapes it for the dispatcher.\n\nReturns `%{triple_store: pid, data_store: pid, subscribable_registry: pid,\nconditions: %{name => pid}, capabilities: %{name => pid}}`. Any child that\nis mid-restart appears as `nil` (infrastructure) or is simply absent from\nthe maps.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#snapshot/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the store supervisor.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.whereis/2","doc":"Returns the live pid for a child id, or `nil` if it is absent or restarting.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.StoreSupervisor.html#whereis/2"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry","doc":"Agent mapping protocol strings to `Anemos.Subscribable` handler modules.\n\nThis registry is a companion to `Anemos.Runtime.DataModelRegistry`, not a\nreplacement for it. The two key different things for different behaviours:\nthis one maps a *protocol* (`\"webhook\"`, `\"rss\"`, `\"amqp\"`) to an\n`Anemos.Subscribable` handler for `dynamic { subscribe ... }` blocks;\n`DataModelRegistry` maps a *format keyword* (`\"edn\"`, `\"xml\"`, `\"sql\"`) to an\n`Anemos.DataModel` handler for format bodies and query engines. One handler\nmodule may implement both behaviours and appear in both registries.\n\nThe registration signature differs for the same reason. `DataModelRegistry`\nderives its key from the handler via `keyword/0`, because a format handler\nowns its format. Here the protocol string is supplied externally at\nregistration time, because `Subscribable` handlers receive the protocol as a\nparameter and so may serve more than one.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.list/1","doc":"Returns all registered protocol strings.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#list/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.list/1","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.list(reg)\n    []","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#list/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.lookup/2","doc":"Looks up a handler module by protocol string.\n\nReturns `{:ok, module}` or `:error`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#lookup/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.lookup/2","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.lookup(reg, \"missing\")\n    :error","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#lookup/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.register/3","doc":"Registers a handler module under the given protocol string.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#register/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.register/3","doc":"iex> {:ok, reg} = Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.register(reg, \"webhook\", MyHandler)\n    :ok","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#register/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a subscribable registry.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.SubscribableRegistry.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore","doc":"Agent-based triple store for RDF-like facts.\n\nStores `{subject, predicate, object}` triples and supports\npattern matching with query variables (`?` bindings).","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Asserting facts - Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore","doc":"TripleStore.assert(store, \"p001\", \"knows\", \"p002\")","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#module-asserting-facts"},{"type":"module","title":"Querying facts - Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore","doc":"TripleStore.query(store, :_, \"knows\", :_)\n    # => [{\"p001\", \"knows\", \"p002\"}]\n\n    TripleStore.match(store, :_, \"worksOn\", :_)\n    # => [%{\"?a\" => \"p001\", \"?p\" => \"proj1\"}, ...]\n\nUse `:_` as a wildcard in queries.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#module-querying-facts"},{"type":"module","title":"Storage and result order - Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore","doc":"Triples are held in a `MapSet`. Assertion is a set insert, so building n\ndistinct triples costs O(n log n) rather than the O(n²) the previous\nlist-plus-`in`-check cost: every `assert_triple/4` used to scan the whole\nstore before prepending.\n\nThe consequence is that **result order is unspecified**. The list\nimplementation happened to return triples in reverse insertion order, and\nnothing in the runtime relied on it — `query/4` results are returned to a\npolicy as an opaque result set and `match/4` results as unordered\nbindings. Callers that need a stable order must sort.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#module-storage-and-result-order"},{"type":"module","title":"Retention - Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore","doc":"The store is still unbounded: nothing evicts. `retract_triple/4` exists as\na store primitive but no DSL verb reaches it, so a policy asserting one\ntriple per event grows the store without limit. Choosing a retention\npolicy is a language decision, not a storage one — see the report on A-23.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#module-retention"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.assert_triple/4","doc":"Asserts a triple (fact) into the store.\n\nIdempotent: asserting the same triple twice leaves one fact.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#assert_triple/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.match/4","doc":"Matches triples against a pattern with named bindings.\n\nTakes a pattern as `{subject, predicate, object}` where each element is\neither a literal value (matches exactly) or a `{:var, name}` tuple\n(binds the matched value to that name).\n\nReturns a list of binding maps in unspecified order — sort if you need\na stable one, as this example does.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#match/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.match/4","doc":"iex> {:ok, store} = Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.assert_triple(store, \"p001\", \"worksOn\", \"proj1\")\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.assert_triple(store, \"p002\", \"worksOn\", \"proj1\")\n    iex> store |> Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.match({:var, \"a\"}, \"worksOn\", {:var, \"p\"}) |> Enum.sort()\n    [%{\"a\" => \"p001\", \"p\" => \"proj1\"}, %{\"a\" => \"p002\", \"p\" => \"proj1\"}]","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#match/4-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.query/4","doc":"Queries the store for triples matching a pattern.\n\nUse `:_` as a wildcard for any position. Returns matching triples in\nunspecified order.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#query/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.query/4","doc":"iex> {:ok, store} = Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.assert_triple(store, \"p001\", \"knows\", \"p002\")\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.query(store, \"p001\", \"knows\", :_)\n    [{\"p001\", \"knows\", \"p002\"}]\n\n    iex> {:ok, store} = Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.start_link()\n    iex> Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.query(store, :_, :_, :_)\n    []","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#query/4-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.retract_triple/4","doc":"Retracts a triple from the store.\n\nRetracting a triple that was never asserted is not an error. No DSL verb\nreaches this yet; it is the storage primitive an explicit `retract` would\nbe built on.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#retract_triple/4"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.size/1","doc":"Returns the number of distinct triples held.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#size/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a triple store.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.start_link/1","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TripleStore.html#start_link/1-options"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore","doc":"Per-runtime trust configuration: a keyring of **public** verification\nkeys, and the policy for sources that declare no signer at all (A-05).\n\nOne of these is started for every `Anemos.Runtime`, whether or not the\nembedder configures anything. That is deliberate: a runtime that has no\ntrust store is a runtime `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed` cannot\nmake a decision about, and the only safe answer to \"I cannot decide\" on\na security boundary is refusal. Always starting one means refusal is\nreserved for genuine failures rather than being the normal case.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Public keys only, and it is enforced here - Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore","doc":"A key handed to this store must have `private: nil`. This is not\nhygiene, it is the invariant the whole design rests on: `comn` finding\nC-3 records that the Ed25519 *signing* private key is currently reused\nas ChaCha20 encryption key material, a key-separation violation. A\nverifier that never holds a private key cannot compound that mistake,\nand cannot be quietly extended into a signer later. Anemos verifies;\nsomething else signs.\n\nA key carrying private material is therefore refused — loudly, at\ningest, by `start_link/1` raising and by `put_key/3` answering\n`{:error, :private_key_material}` — rather than accepted and ignored.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#module-public-keys-only-and-it-is-enforced-here"},{"type":"module","title":"What this store does not decide - Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore","doc":"Which algorithms are acceptable. A `Comn.Secrets.Key` may declare\n`:rsa_4096` or `:ecdsa_p256`, and this store will hold one; it is\n`Signed` that refuses to verify against it, explicitly, on the load\npath. The split is intentional — refusing private material protects an\ninvariant of *the store*, whereas which signatures count is *the\nverifier's* policy, and putting it here would hide the refusal from the\nload that triggered it.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#module-what-this-store-does-not-decide"},{"type":"module","title":"Signer names are strings - Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore","doc":"`signed_by :operator` reaches an authority as the **string**\n`\"operator\"`, not the atom `:operator` — the lexer's atom token payload\nis a binary and `Anemos.Parser` passes it straight through. A keyring\nkeyed on atoms would therefore never match, and a verifier that never\nmatches a signer is one that never rejects one: fail-open, on the\nboundary itself. Every name entering this store is normalised to a\nbinary so that either spelling in configuration behaves identically.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#module-signer-names-are-strings"},{"type":"module","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore","doc":"* `:name` — process registration name.\n  * `:keyring` — `%{signer_name => Comn.Secrets.Key.t()}`. Names may be\n    atoms or strings; they are stored as strings. Defaults to empty,\n    which means every declared signer is unknown, which means refused.\n  * `:unsigned_policy` — `:strict | :warn | :permit`, default\n    `:permit`. See `unsigned_policy/1`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#module-options"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.keyring/0","doc":"Signer name to public key. Names are always binaries.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#t:keyring/0"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.unsigned_policy/0","doc":"What to do with a source that names no signer at all.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#t:unsigned_policy/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.delete_key/2","doc":"Removes the key trusted for `signer`. Revocation.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#delete_key/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.fetch/1","doc":"Returns the whole trust configuration for `runtime`.\n\nAn `anemos.trust/no_trust_store` error when the runtime has none — which a caller\non the load path must treat as a refusal, not as an absence of policy.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#fetch/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.key/2","doc":"Looks up the public key trusted for `signer` in `runtime`.\n\n`signer` may be an atom or a string; both resolve to the same entry.\n`:error` means the signer is unknown, and an unknown signer is refused\nrather than permitted — under multi-tenancy, a name nobody has vouched\nfor is a name with no authority behind it.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#key/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.name_for/1","doc":"The registered name of the trust store belonging to `runtime`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#name_for/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.put_key/3","doc":"Adds or replaces the public key trusted for `signer`. Supports rotation\nwithout restarting the runtime.\n\nAnswers an `anemos.trust/private_key_material` error rather than raising, because\nunlike `start_link/1` this runs against a live system and a rejected\nrotation should not take the runtime down with it. The old key stays in\nforce, which is the fail-safe outcome.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#put_key/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a trust store.\n\nRaises `ArgumentError` on a keyring entry carrying private key material\nor on an unrecognised `:unsigned_policy`. Both are configuration\nmistakes on a security boundary, and a boundary that silently corrects\nits own configuration is not one.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.unsigned_policy/1","doc":"Returns the unsigned-rule policy in force for `runtime`.\n\n  * `:strict` — refuse a source that declares no signer.\n  * `:warn` — admit it, and log that it was admitted unverified.\n  * `:permit` — admit it silently. The default; see the module note.\n\nA runtime with no trust store reports the default, because this is a\nquestion about *policy* rather than a decision about a load; the load\npath itself fails closed via `fetch/1`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.TrustStore.html#unsigned_policy/1"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher","doc":"Keeps a directory of `*.rule` files deployed into a runtime.\n\nEach file is a policy, named by its path relative to the directory. Saving\na file deploys it over the running version; deleting it unloads it. See\n`Anemos.Runtime.load/3` for what a deploy keeps and what it replaces.\n\n    {Anemos.Runtime, name: :anemos, watch: \"/etc/anemos/policies\"}\n\nor, under your own supervisor, after the runtime:\n\n    {Anemos.Runtime.Watcher, runtime: :anemos, dir: \"/etc/anemos/policies\"}","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html"},{"type":"module","title":"A file that does not load - Anemos.Runtime.Watcher","doc":"The version already running keeps running. The failure is logged once and\nheld for `status/1`. It is tried again on every pass, because what stopped\nit may not be in the file: a rule name still held by a file that is about\nto lose it, a feed handler the host has not registered yet. A file caught\nhalf-written is this case too, and corrects itself on the next pass.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#module-a-file-that-does-not-load"},{"type":"module","title":"A directory that is not there - Anemos.Runtime.Watcher","doc":"Nothing is unloaded. A missing directory is not an empty one.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#module-a-directory-that-is-not-there"},{"type":"module","title":"Signatures - Anemos.Runtime.Watcher","doc":"` .sig`, if present, is passed to the load authority as the detached\nsignature (see `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed`). Replacing it counts\nas a change to the policy. It holds one signature, Base64, so it serves a\npolicy with a single declared signer; one that declares several has to be\nloaded through `Anemos.Runtime.load/3`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#module-signatures"},{"type":"module","title":"Options - Anemos.Runtime.Watcher","doc":"* `:runtime` — the `Anemos.Runtime` name (required)\n  * `:dir` — the directory to watch, searched recursively (required)\n  * `:interval_ms` — how often to look, default 1000; `:manual` to look\n    only when `scan/1` is called\n  * `:name` — process registration name (optional)","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#module-options"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.status/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#t:status/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.scan/1","doc":"Looks at the directory now and deploys what changed.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#scan/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.start_link/1","doc":"Starts a watcher. See the moduledoc for options.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.status/1","doc":"The outcome of the last deploy of every policy file present.","ref":"Anemos.Runtime.Watcher.html#status/1"},{"type":"module","title":"Anemos.Secret","doc":"A wrapper for secret-bearing values that refuses to disclose itself.\n\nAnemos routes module results into logs, envelopes, channel connectors and\nerror messages. Any of those paths will happily stringify whatever a host\nmodule returned. A value wrapped in `Anemos.Secret` renders as\n`#Anemos.Secret ` through `inspect/1`, `\"#{}\"` interpolation,\n`to_string/1`, `to_charlist/1` and therefore `Logger` — there is exactly one\nway to recover the plaintext, `expose/1`, and it is greppable.\n\nNote: unrelated to `Comn.Secret`, which is a serialization protocol for\nencrypting blobs. This struct is a disclosure control, not a crypto type.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html"},{"type":"module","title":"For host module authors - Anemos.Secret","doc":"A verb that returns a credential should wrap it:\n\n    defmodule MyApp.Anemos.Secret do\n      use Anemos.Module, name: \"SECRET\"\n\n      @impl true\n      def handle_verb(\"unlock\", [key_id], _context) do\n        {:ok, Anemos.Secret.new(fetch_key(key_id), label: key_id)}\n      end\n    end\n\nThe wrapper then survives its trip through the runtime — emit, envelope,\nchannel connector, log line — without the plaintext appearing anywhere.\nWhatever finally consumes it (an HTTP client, a config writer) calls\n`expose/1` at the last possible moment.\n\nWrapping is opt-in: nothing in the runtime wraps values on your behalf,\nbecause nothing in the runtime knows which values are sensitive.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#module-for-host-module-authors"},{"type":"module","title":"Label - Anemos.Secret","doc":"The optional `:label` is rendered in place of the value, so it must be a\nnon-sensitive identifier (\"ddns-key\", \"operator-did\"), never the secret or\nanything derived from it.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#module-label"},{"type":"module","title":"What this does not protect against - Anemos.Secret","doc":"`expose/1`, `Map.get(s, :__secret__)`, `inspect(s, structs: false)`,\n`:erlang.term_to_binary/1` and a process-heap dump all still see the\nplaintext. Each of those is an explicit request for the raw term —\n`structs: false` in particular asks Elixir to bypass every `Inspect` impl in\nthe language, and no struct can defend against it. This type stops\naccidental disclosure through ordinary formatting, which is how secrets\nactually escape. It is not a sealed enclave.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#module-what-this-does-not-protect-against"},{"type":"type","title":"Anemos.Secret.t/0","doc":"","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Secret.expose/1","doc":"Recovers the plaintext. The only disclosure path — call it as late as possible.\n\nNon-secret values pass through unchanged, so callers can unwrap\nunconditionally.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#expose/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Secret.expose/1","doc":"iex> Anemos.Secret.expose(Anemos.Secret.new(\"hunter2\"))\n    \"hunter2\"\n\n    iex> Anemos.Secret.expose(\"plain\")\n    \"plain\"","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#expose/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Secret.map/2","doc":"Applies `fun` to the plaintext and re-wraps the result, preserving the label.\n\nUse this for derivations (encoding, signing) that must stay secret rather\nthan exposing and re-wrapping by hand.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#map/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Secret.map/2","doc":"iex> Anemos.Secret.new(\"abc\") |> Anemos.Secret.map(&String.upcase/1) |> Anemos.Secret.expose()\n    \"ABC\"","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#map/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Secret.new/2","doc":"Wraps `value` so it cannot be disclosed by formatting.\n\nWrapping an existing `Anemos.Secret` returns it unchanged, so `new/1` is\nsafe to apply defensively.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#new/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - Anemos.Secret.new/2","doc":"* `:label` — non-sensitive identifier shown in place of the value","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#new/2-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Secret.new/2","doc":"iex> inspect(Anemos.Secret.new(\"hunter2\"))\n    \"#Anemos.Secret \"\n\n    iex> inspect(Anemos.Secret.new(\"hunter2\", label: \"ddns-key\"))\n    \"#Anemos.Secret \"","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#new/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Anemos.Secret.secret?/1","doc":"Returns true if `term` is a wrapped secret.","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#secret?/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Anemos.Secret.secret?/1","doc":"iex> Anemos.Secret.secret?(Anemos.Secret.new(\"x\"))\n    true\n\n    iex> Anemos.Secret.secret?(\"x\")\n    false","ref":"Anemos.Secret.html#secret?/1-examples"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"Anemos.Subscribable","doc":"Behaviour for data sources that support dynamic subscription.\n\nImplementations register a protocol identifier (e.g., \"webhook\",\n\"rss\", \"amqp\") used in `dynamic { subscribe     }` blocks.\n\nThe adapter owns the feed's lifecycle, including any polling. A source\nthat has to be pulled on a cadence starts its own timer inside\n`subscribe/3` and calls the callback on each pull; the policy language\nhas no vocabulary for that cadence, by design. If the cadence should be\nvisible in the policy, write an `at interval(...)` rule that asks the\nhost to refresh — see the manual, chapter 7.","ref":"Anemos.Subscribable.html"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.Subscribable.subscribe/3","doc":"Subscribes to the given URI using the named protocol. Returns `{:ok, ref}` on success or `{:error, reason}` on failure.","ref":"Anemos.Subscribable.html#c:subscribe/3"},{"type":"callback","title":"Anemos.Subscribable.unsubscribe/1","doc":"Cancels an active subscription identified by `ref`. Always returns `:ok`.","ref":"Anemos.Subscribable.html#c:unsubscribe/1"},{"type":"task","title":"mix anemos.run","doc":"Parse and execute a `.rule` file against a given event.\n\n    mix anemos.run priv/examples/garage-lights.rule \\\n      --event SENSOR_MOTION_DETECTED --context garage.motion.sensor","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.html"},{"type":"task","title":"Options - mix anemos.run","doc":"* `--event`, `-e` — event name to dispatch (required)\n  * `--context`, `-c` — the context path the caller occupies. `context`\n    is a security boundary and fails closed, so a rule wrapped in\n    `context \"garage\"` is unreachable until the caller says it is\n    standing in `garage` or beneath it. Omitting this is not \"run\n    everything\"; it is \"I decline to say\", and a declared boundary\n    refuses that.\n  * `--context-data KEY=VALUE` — external data that `?var` resolves\n    against. Repeatable. This is a *different* thing from `--context`:\n    one is where the caller stands, the other is what it brought with it.\n  * `--stub-modules` — also register `Anemos.example_modules/0`, the\n    demonstration doubles for `PDF`, `STRING`, `INFRA`, `SECRET` and\n    `EVENT` that `priv/examples/` needs. Off by default: see\n    `Anemos.example_modules/0` for why a `SECRET` double must be asked\n    for rather than inherited.\n  * `--help`, `-h` — print this usage summary.","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.html#module-options"},{"type":"task","title":"Exit status - mix anemos.run","doc":"`0` only when every operation succeeded. `1` on a usage error, an\nunreadable file, a refused load, a parse or compile failure, or any\noperation that returned an error — so the task can gate a script or a CI\nstep.","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.html#module-exit-status"},{"type":"task","title":"The load is gated - mix anemos.run","doc":"The source goes through the same trust boundary as\n`Anemos.Runtime.load/3` — the `:max_policy_bytes` ceiling and the\nconfigured `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority` (A-59). A refusal is reported\nas such, named as the boundary rather than as a broken rule file, and\nexits `1`. The default authority permits everything, so this changes\nnothing until an operator configures one.\n\nAn empty result is reported as one of three distinct outcomes, because\nthey call for three different fixes: nothing subscribed to the event\n(check the event name), something subscribed but the context boundary\ndenied it (pass `--context`), or a rule ran and every branch was false\n(check the guards and the data). The previous single message, \"No rules\nmatched\", was wrong for all three — it describes zero *operations*.","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.html#module-the-load-is-gated"},{"type":"function","title":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.describe/1","doc":"Renders one error for a policy author.\n\n`Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct` carries `request_id` and `trace_id` for\ncorrelating across services. Those are operator fields; `inspect/1` on\nthe struct — which is what the old fallback did — put them in front of\nsomeone trying to fix a rule file, alongside every other nil field.","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.html#describe/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.describe/1","doc":"iex> err = Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct.new(\"anemos.runtime/unknown_module\", nil, \"Module PDF not found in registry\")\n    iex> Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.describe(%{err | trace_id: \"abc123\"})\n    \"Module PDF not found in registry (anemos.runtime/unknown_module)\"\n\n    iex> Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.describe(\"Unexpected token at line 3\")\n    \"Unexpected token at line 3\"","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.html#describe/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.execute/1","doc":"The whole task minus the printing and the exit.\n\nReturns `{:ok | :error, [{:info | :error, String.t()}]}`. Split out so\nthe CLI's behaviour — flag parsing, exit status, every message it can\nemit — is testable without capturing IO or halting the VM. The absence\nof that seam is a large part of why these defects survived.","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.html#execute/1"},{"type":"extras","title":"Programming Anemos","doc":"# Programming Anemos\n\nAn introduction, practical guide, and language reference for Anemos 0.1.0.\n\nAnemos expresses automation as policies: when something happens, decide what\nit means and ask the host application to act. A policy might turn on a light,\nclassify a connection, or arrange a batch of documents for processing. The\nlanguage supplies events, decisions, state, and composition. The application\nthat embeds it supplies access to the outside world.\n\nThis book assumes no knowledge of Anemos. You should be comfortable editing a\ntext file and running a terminal command. The first half teaches policy writing\nwithout requiring Elixir. The integration chapters introduce the Elixir needed\nto run a persistent interpreter and supply your own commands.","ref":"programming-anemos.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Reading the book - Programming Anemos","doc":"Read Chapters 1–4 in order. They establish the execution model that makes the\nrest of the language understandable. Then follow your application: Chapter 5\nfor batches, Chapter 6 for stateful events, Chapter 7 for time, and Chapter 8 for\nshared data. Chapters 9–11 cover embedding, operation, and troubleshooting.\nChapter 12 combines those ideas in a complete application you can run and adapt.\nThe reference and index are intended for return visits.\n\n| Part | Chapter | What you will learn |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Getting started | [1. Your first policy](01-first-policy.md) | Install, run, change, and diagnose a complete program |\n| Getting started | [2. How policies run](02-execution-model.md) | Events, listeners, contexts, state, and operation order |\n| The language | [3. Values and expressions](03-values-and-expressions.md) | Names, sigils, types, operators, and compounding |\n| The language | [4. Decisions and control flow](04-control-flow.md) | Branches, guards, early exits, and handoffs |\n| The language | [5. Collections and pipelines](05-collections.md) | Loops, filtering, mapping, and reduction |\n| Stateful automation | [6. Conditions and capabilities](06-conditions-and-capabilities.md) | Remembered reports, transitions, and on-demand work |\n| Stateful automation | [7. Time and duration](07-time.md) | Dwell timers, intervals, dates, and recurring schedules |\n| Data | [8. Datasets, queries, and facts](08-data.md) | Membership, data handlers, live feeds, and triple patterns |\n| Applications | [9. Embedding and extending Anemos](09-embedding.md) | Runtime setup, commands, events, channels, and secrets |\n| Applications | [10. Deploying and operating policies](10-operation.md) | Replacement, watching, signing, recovery, and limits |\n| Applications | [11. Finding and fixing problems](11-troubleshooting.md) | Reports, introspection, tracing, and practical recipes |\n| Putting it together | [12. Workshop temperature alerts](12-workshop.md) | Build and test a complete application with a host service |\n| Reference | [Language and API reference](reference.md) | Syntax summaries, precedence, defaults, and feature status |\n| Reference | [Glossary and subject index](glossary.md) | Definitions and direct routes back to explanations |","ref":"programming-anemos.html#reading-the-book"},{"type":"extras","title":"Conventions and example files - Programming Anemos","doc":"Complete policies appear in `anemos` code blocks. Each is independent unless\nthe surrounding text says otherwise. The documentation test parses and compiles\nevery such block. Syntax templates and deliberately incomplete fragments use\n`text` blocks. Shell commands use `sh`; host application code uses `elixir`.\nAngle brackets in templates mark a placeholder you must replace; they are not\nliteral Anemos syntax.\n\nDownloadable example files accompany the first chapters. Run commands\nfrom the repository root. The [walkthrough script](examples/walkthrough.exs)\nexecutes the persistent counter, dataset, and pipeline examples and checks their\nanswers:\n\n```sh\nmix run docs/manual/examples/walkthrough.exs\n```\n\nExercises ask you to predict a result before running a change. Answers follow\nthe exercises, so you can check your reasoning without needing another book.","ref":"programming-anemos.html#conventions-and-example-files"},{"type":"extras","title":"What this edition promises - Programming Anemos","doc":"This edition describes the implementation in this repository, version 0.1.0.\nExamples distinguish working language features from syntax that is only parsed\nor only partly implemented. In particular, read the qualifications about\n`demand`, `until`, triple queries, and pipeline `emit` before depending on them.\nThe [feature-status reference](reference.md#feature-status) collects those\nqualifications in one place.\n\nThe design notes under `docs/design-notes/`, historical Tcl sketches, and some\nolder examples discuss a broader language. They are useful background, but they\nare not the contract taught here. Host modules also have their own contracts:\nthe spelling `[DEVICE::set ...]` says how to call a command, not what hardware\nexists or whether a command has permission to operate it.\n\nDocumentation checks live in `test/anemos/manual_test.exs` in the checkout.\nThey check complete policy syntax and selected behavioral promises. They do not\nturn an application-specific module, data format, or external service into a\nbuilt-in feature.\n\nContinue with [Chapter 1: Your first policy](01-first-policy.md).","ref":"programming-anemos.html#what-this-edition-promises"},{"type":"extras","title":"1. Your first policy","doc":"# 1. Your first policy\n\nSuppose a motion sensor reports activity in a garage. You want to write down\nwhat should happen when that report arrives. In Anemos, that instruction is a\n*rule*. One or more declarations saved together form a *policy*.\n\nWe will start by writing to the log. This lets us learn the language without\ninstalling a sensor or connecting a device controller. Later we will replace\nthe log statement with a command supplied by the application.","ref":"01-first-policy.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Prepare the interpreter - 1. Your first policy","doc":"Use a checkout of this repository. Its `.tool-versions` specifies Erlang\n29.1.2 and Elixir 1.20.4 for OTP 29; `mix.exs` requires Elixir `~> 1.20`.\nInstall that toolchain using your usual environment manager and confirm that\n`elixir` and `mix` are on your terminal's path:\n\n```sh\nelixir --version\nmix --version\nmix deps.get\nmix compile\n```\n\nMix is Elixir's build and task runner. You do not need to learn Elixir to use\nthe policy runner. Dependencies are fetched from the declarations and lockfile\nin this checkout; a sibling checkout of `comn` is not needed.","ref":"01-first-policy.html#prepare-the-interpreter"},{"type":"extras","title":"Read a complete rule - 1. Your first policy","doc":"The file [hello.rule](examples/hello.rule) contains:\n\n```anemos\nrule welcome {\n  when MOTION {\n    log \"Motion detected\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`rule` introduces a declaration. `welcome` is the name we give it. The outer\nbraces contain its definition. `when MOTION` subscribes to an event named\n`MOTION`; the inner braces contain the statements to evaluate when that event\narrives. `log` records a value. Double quotes delimit a string.\n\nWhitespace makes this structure readable. A semicolon starts a comment; it\ndoes not terminate a statement. A line beginning with `#` is also a comment.\n\nRun the file:\n\n```sh\nmix anemos.run docs/manual/examples/hello.rule --event MOTION\n```\n\nThe runner prints the file it parsed, a dispatch summary, and an operation\nline containing:\n\n```text\nlog \"Motion detected\"\n```\n\nYou may also see a timestamped Logger message. That is the log operation\nbeing executed; the runner's operation line is its report of the work.\n\nNow change `--event MOTION` to `--event RAIN`. No rule subscribes to `RAIN`,\nso the runner reports that no rule subscribes. Anemos does not guess which\nevent you intended. Event names are case-sensitive, exact strings.","ref":"01-first-policy.html#read-a-complete-rule"},{"type":"extras","title":"Give the event some data - 1. Your first policy","doc":"An event can bring a payload: values such as the sensor name, measured\ntemperature, or person making a request. Read a payload field with `?name`.\nThe question mark means “resolve this name from the data available to me.”\n\n```anemos\nrule greet {\n  when ARRIVAL {\n    log ?visitor\n    if ?visitor == \"Ada\" {\n      log \"Welcome back\"\n    } else {\n      log \"Welcome\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSave this as `greet.rule`, or use [the supplied file](examples/greet.rule):\n\n```sh\nmix anemos.run docs/manual/examples/greet.rule \\\n  --event ARRIVAL --context-data visitor=Ada\n```\n\nThe log values are `\"Ada\"` and `\"Welcome back\"`. Try `visitor=Grace` and\nthe second value becomes `\"Welcome\"`.\n\nRepeat `--context-data` to supply more fields. CLI values are **strings**:\n`--context-data enabled=false` supplies `\"false\"`, not the boolean `false`.\nThe string is truthy. When you need numbers, booleans, lists, or maps, use the\nhost API introduced below or the persistent examples in Chapter 9.\n\nIf a field is missing, `?visitor` ordinarily resolves to `nil`, the host's\nmissing-value representation. Anemos has no `nil` literal. To test for a\npresent value, write `exists ?visitor`; to accept only a particular value,\ncompare it explicitly as we did above.","ref":"01-first-policy.html#give-the-event-some-data"},{"type":"extras","title":"Limit a listener to a context - 1. Your first policy","doc":"A context is a dotted path describing where an event is admitted. In\n[garage.rule](examples/garage.rule), our listener belongs to `garage`:\n\n```anemos\nrule garage-motion {\n  context \"garage\" {\n    when MOTION {\n      log \"Garage motion detected\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```sh\nmix anemos.run docs/manual/examples/garage.rule \\\n  --event MOTION --context garage.sensor\n```\n\nThe rule runs because `garage.sensor` is inside `garage`. `garage` itself is\nalso admitted. `garden.sensor` and `garageband.sensor` are not. A missing\n`--context` is refused by this listener too: the caller must say where it\nstands when a boundary has been declared.\n\n`--context` and `--context-data` answer different questions. The first is\nthe caller's path. The second is its payload. A payload field called\n`context` does not move an event across a boundary.","ref":"01-first-policy.html#limit-a-listener-to-a-context"},{"type":"extras","title":"Ask the host to do something - 1. Your first policy","doc":"Commands use square brackets and a module-qualified verb:\n\n```anemos\nrule garage-lights {\n  context \"garage\" {\n    when MOTION {\n      [DEVICE::set \"garage.lights\" \"on\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`DEVICE` names a registered module; `set` names one of its verbs. The two\nstrings are its arguments. The built-in development `DEVICE` module used by\nthe one-shot runner computes an answer in memory. It does not turn on a real\nlight. A deployed application must register its own device implementation.\n\nThis separation lets the same policy language work with different systems.\nA rule specifies the action; the host decides how that action is implemented.","ref":"01-first-policy.html#ask-the-host-to-do-something"},{"type":"extras","title":"Run a policy from Elixir - 1. Your first policy","doc":"For a single event, start an interactive session with `iex -S mix`, then enter:\n\n```elixir\nsource = File.read!(\"docs/manual/examples/greet.rule\")\n{:ok, results} = Anemos.run(source,\n  event: \"ARRIVAL\",\n  context_data: %{\"visitor\" => \"Ada\"})\nEnum.map(results, &Map.get(&1, :value))\n# => [\"Ada\", \"Welcome back\"]\n```\n\n`Anemos.run/2` builds a temporary interpreter, dispatches one event, and tears\nthe interpreter down. A second call starts fresh. For state across events or\nscheduled work, use a persistent `Anemos.Runtime` (Chapter 9).\n\nThe outer `{:ok, results}` says dispatch completed. Individual results may\nstill contain `{:error, reason}`. We will learn to inspect those in Chapter 11.","ref":"01-first-policy.html#run-a-policy-from-elixir"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exercises - 1. Your first policy","doc":"1. In `greet.rule`, add a second `log ?visitor` after the `if`. How many log\n   values will an arrival produce?\n2. Run `garage.rule` with `--context garageband`. Does the shared prefix help?\n3. Replace the string comparison in `greet.rule` with `if ?visitor`. What does\n   `--context-data visitor=false` do?\n\nAnswers: (1) Three: visitor, greeting, visitor. (2) No; paths compare whole\nsegments. (3) It takes the true branch because `\"false\"` is a string. Only\nboolean `false` and `nil` are falsey.\n\n[Next: How policies run](02-execution-model.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"01-first-policy.html#exercises"},{"type":"extras","title":"2. How policies run","doc":"# 2. How policies run\n\nKnowing a language's syntax tells you what you can write. Knowing its execution\nmodel tells you what the program will do. In Anemos, the important questions\nare which listeners receive an event, which state they share, and when an\nexpression actually invokes host code.","ref":"02-execution-model.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"From text to a running policy - 2. How policies run","doc":"The host loads source text. The load boundary checks its size and authority,\nthe parser recognizes declarations, and the compiler builds executable\nhandlers. The runtime installs those handlers, datasets, and schedules.\nLoading a rule does not execute its `when` body.\n\nAn incoming event supplies an exact name, an optional context path, and a\npayload. The dispatcher finds subscribers, filters them by context, evaluates\nthe eligible handlers, and executes the operations they produce. Conditions\nalso receive events they subscribe to and may generate transition events.\n\nAn event can reach several rules. Rules are listeners rather than an\nexclusive chain of alternatives. If two rules subscribe to `MOTION`, both\neligible rules can run. Use `if`/`elseif`/`else` within a handler when you want\nmutually exclusive choices. Do not make correctness depend on one independent\nlistener having run before another.","ref":"02-execution-model.html#from-text-to-a-running-policy"},{"type":"extras","title":"The four kinds of name - 2. How policies run","doc":"Most policy mistakes become easier to see when names are classified:\n\n| Name | Example | Who supplies it? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Declaration | `garage-motion` | The policy author |\n| Event | `MOTION`, `SENSOR::motion` | The event producer and policy agree |\n| Module and verb | `DEVICE::set` | The host registers the implementation |\n| Binding | `?visitor`, `@message`, `$count` | Event data or policy evaluation |\n\n`when SENSOR::motion` subscribes to that event name. It does not call the\n`SENSOR` module. `[SENSOR::motion]` is a command and does call a module. The\nbrackets distinguish an action from a subscription.","ref":"02-execution-model.html#the-four-kinds-of-name"},{"type":"extras","title":"A context belongs to a listener - 2. How policies run","doc":"Declare one context boundary per rule. Either of these forms expresses it:\n\n```anemos\nrule listen {\n  context \"building.garage\" {\n    when MOTION { log \"heard\" }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```anemos\nrule listen {\n  context building.garage\n  when MOTION { log \"heard\" }\n}\n```\n\nThe latter form is convenient in longer definitions. Use a single path rather\nthan several apparently independent context blocks: the compiler extracts one\ncontext for the declaration, not a separate access domain for every block.\nNested contexts do not provide lexical path composition.\n\nFor a boundary `building.garage`:\n\n| Arriving context | Admitted? |\n| --- | --- |\n| `building.garage` | Yes |\n| `building.garage.sensor` | Yes |\n| `building` | No |\n| `building.garden` | No |\n| `building.garageband` | No |\n| Absent | No |\n\nA rule with no context boundary accepts any arriving context, including an\nabsent one. The host is responsible for authenticating the path it places on\nevents. The language's comparison does not establish who sent an event.","ref":"02-execution-model.html#a-context-belongs-to-a-listener"},{"type":"extras","title":"State has a lifetime and an owner - 2. How policies run","doc":"There are three ways to read values:\n\n| Read | Meaning | Lifetime |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `?name` | External/name resolution | Current event plus visible queries/datasets |\n| `@name` | A `let` or loop binding | Current handler evaluation |\n| `$name` | A `set` binding | Retained for this named rule across events |\n\nSeveral `when` blocks in the same named rule can communicate through `set`.\nDifferent named rules have separate binding state. That state is not\nautomatically divided by sensor, user, or arriving context. If two devices\nreach the same rule, they reach the same `$count`. To maintain per-device\nstate, arrange separate rule identities or keep keyed state in a host module.\n\nThis counter initializes lazily and then increments:\n\n```anemos\nrule count-motion {\n  when MOTION {\n    if not exists $count {\n      set count = 0\n    }\n    set count = $count + 1\n    log $count\n  }\n  when RESET {\n    set count = 0\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn one persistent runtime, `MOTION`, `MOTION`, `RESET`, `MOTION` logs\n`1`, `2`, and `1`. `RESET` logs nothing. Repeating `Anemos.run/2` four times\ncannot reproduce this sequence because each call creates fresh state.\n\nPersistent here means retained in a running runtime. State survives supported\nworker restarts, but is not an on-disk database and does not survive loss of\nthe whole runtime or VM. Chapter 10 explains replacement and recovery.","ref":"02-execution-model.html#state-has-a-lifetime-and-an-owner"},{"type":"extras","title":"Bodies evaluate, then operations execute - 2. How policies run","doc":"This distinction matters whenever host commands have side effects.\n\nA standalone command statement produces an operation for the dispatcher to\nexecute after evaluating the handler. A command in an expression must supply\na value immediately, so it invokes the host while the expression is evaluated.\n\n```anemos\nrule demonstrate-order {\n  when GO {\n    [AUDIT::record \"statement\"]\n    let answer = [LOOKUP::read \"value\"]\n    log @answer\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHere `LOOKUP::read` runs while the handler is being evaluated. Only afterward\ndoes the dispatcher execute the queued `AUDIT::record` operation and log the\nanswer. The source order alone is therefore not the order of all external\nside effects. These modules are illustrative host implementations, not bundled\nservices.\n\nWhen sequencing host calls is essential, express the sequence through value\nbindings whose calls are evaluated in order, or provide a host verb that owns\nthe whole operation. Do not treat a sequence of independent statements as a\ntransaction.\n\nA failed value-position call aborts the rest of that handler's evaluation and\nreports an error. Previously evaluated calls and earlier queued operations\nare not rolled back. Errors returned by queued operations do not, in general,\ncancel later queued operations. This is especially relevant to `demand`.","ref":"02-execution-model.html#bodies-evaluate-then-operations-execute"},{"type":"extras","title":"Put executable statements inside triggers - 2. How policies run","doc":"Rules are compiled from their `when` and `at` blocks. A top-level `set` or\n`log` in a rule is not a rule initializer, even where the parser accepts it.\nPlace initialization inside a handler, guarded by `exists` as in the counter.\nInline `data` and `query` declarations are an exception: they are definitions\ninstalled at load time, not executable statements.","ref":"02-execution-model.html#put-executable-statements-inside-triggers"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exercise: who owns the count? - 2. How policies run","doc":"Give `count-motion` a context of `garage`, then send motion from\n`garage.north` followed by `garage.south`. What does it log?\n\nAnswer: `1`, then `2`. Both paths are admitted to the same named rule.\nContext filtering changes reachability; it does not allocate independent\ncopies of persistent bindings.\n\n[Next: Values and expressions](03-values-and-expressions.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"02-execution-model.html#exercise-who-owns-the-count"},{"type":"extras","title":"3. Values and expressions","doc":"# 3. Values and expressions\n\nA policy spends much of its life naming values, testing them, and passing them\nto commands. This chapter gives those everyday operations precise meanings.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Literal values - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"```anemos\nrule values {\n  when EXAMPLE {\n    log \"a string\"\n    log 42\n    log 3.5\n    log true\n    log false\n    log :garage.light\n    log eth0\n    log 10MB\n    log ~s(a string with \"quotes\" inside)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNumbers are decimal integers or floats. Strings use double quotes and support\n`\\\"`, `\\\\`, `\\n`, and `\\t`. The `~s` form takes the text verbatim between a\npair of delimiters, any of `()`, `[]`, `{}`, `<>`, `//`, `||` or `\"\"`, so a\nstring full of double quotes needs no escaping. Booleans are the lowercase\nwords `true` and `false`.\n\nA size is an integer with a byte unit, `B`, `KB`, `MB`, `GB` or `TB`, and is\nan ordinary integer at runtime: `10MB` is `10485760`. Multiples are binary,\nso `1KB` is `1024`, because every size a policy meets is a file size.\n\nA regular expression is `~r` followed by the pattern between delimiters and\noptional flags, as in `~r/^h/i`. It is compiled once when the policy loads; a\npattern that does not compile is a syntax error naming the line. Use it on\nthe right of `matches_regex`, which still also accepts a plain string. A missing external value is represented by host `nil`; writing the\nbare word `nil` in a policy does not create that value.\n\nColon-prefixed symbols such as `:garage.light`, bare identifiers such as\n`eth0`, and uppercase names in value position all evaluate to strings.\nAnemos symbols are not dynamically created Elixir atoms. For example,\n`:garage` and `\"garage\"` have the same runtime value. Prefer quoted strings\nfor arbitrary text and symbols for stable labels or fact identifiers.\n\nUnquoted addresses such as `192.0.2.1` and `2001:db8::1` have lexer support\nin appropriate positions; quote addresses passed as ordinary command data\nto avoid ambiguities with qualification and time syntax. Durations and time\nliterals are described in Chapter 7.\n\nThe language has no general JSON-style map or square-bracket array literal.\nSquare brackets are for commands and triple patterns. Collections normally\ncome from event data, module results, pipelines, or value compounding. A\nparenthesized sequence such as `(:host IN A \"192.0.2.1\")` is a list-valued\npattern, explained below.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#literal-values"},{"type":"extras","title":"Names and punctuation - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"Ordinary identifiers begin with a lowercase letter and may contain letters,\ndigits, underscores, hyphens, and dots. Thus `garage-lights` is one name,\nnot subtraction. Write spaces around arithmetic operators, especially `-`.\nModule/event names use uppercase forms such as `DEVICE` and `MOTION_DETECTED`;\nnames like `CONDITION_ready` have a supported lowercase tail.\n\nVariable references have a narrower spelling: after `?`, `@`, or `$`, use\nletters, digits, and underscores, with a letter or underscore first. Dots in\n`?user.name` are not a general map-navigation operator. Have the host expose\nflat keys such as `user_name`, or use a module/query to navigate structured data.\n\nData and query declarations may have hyphenated names; since sigil references\ncannot address those names as a whole, use underscore names when you intend\nto read a declaration through `?name`.\n\nKeywords and operators are reserved in many positions. Give bindings simple,\ndescriptive names such as `reading`, `enabled`, or `total`; avoid keywords\nsuch as `state`, `source`, `map`, and `query` as binding names.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#names-and-punctuation"},{"type":"extras","title":"Binding and reading - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"```anemos\nrule temperature {\n  when READING {\n    let celsius = ?temperature\n    let fahrenheit = @celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\n    set latest = @fahrenheit\n    log $latest\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWith a numeric temperature of `20`, this logs `68.0`. `let` evaluates its\nright side once and binds a value for this handler. `set` updates persistent\nstate. Write the plain binding name after the keyword; use its sigil when\nreading it. `let celsius` creates `@celsius`, not `$celsius` or `?celsius`.\n\nThe term *immutable* is used for `let` bindings to distinguish them from\npersistent mutable state. The current evaluator permits a later `let` with\nthe same name to replace the local binding. Write single-assignment `let`\ncode so the intended lifetime stays clear; use `set` for intentional updates.\n\nThe compiler rejects an `@name` or `$name` never bound anywhere in its\ndeclaration. This catches wrong sigils and typos. It does not prove a binding\nexists on every execution path. Reading `$count` before its first executed\n`set count` still yields `nil`.\n\nFor `?name`, lookup first checks the dispatch's context data, then a named query\nvisible to the rule, then a visible data block. If none exists, it yields\n`nil`. Context data can shadow a query or dataset. Choose payload field names\nand declaration names accordingly.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#binding-and-reading"},{"type":"extras","title":"Truth and comparison - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"Only `false` and `nil` are falsey. Zero, empty strings, empty lists, and empty\nmaps are truthy. Consequently `if ?items` is a presence/truthiness check, not\nan emptiness check. `exists` tests non-`nil`, so `exists ?flag` is true even\nwhen `?flag` is boolean false.\n\n| Operation | Spellings | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Equality | `==`, `eq` | Compare values; integer and equal float compare equal |\n| Inequality | `!=`, `ne` | Negated equality |\n| Strict equality | `===`, `!==` | Also distinguish integer from float |\n| Order | `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`; `lt`, `le`, `gt`, `ge` | Compare operands |\n| Negation | `not`, `!` | Boolean negation of truthiness |\n\nComparison does not convert strings to booleans or numbers: `\"1\" == 1` is\nfalse and `\"true\" == true` is false. Comparing a boolean to a boolean-shaped\nstring with ordinary equality/inequality logs a mismatch warning. Relational\ncomparison uses host term ordering for mixed types; ensure both operands have\nthe expected type before relying on a numeric threshold.\n\nUse `infer ?value` as an inspection statement when you are unsure what the\nhost supplied. It reports a kind such as `string`, `number`, `list`, `map`,\n`secret`, or `nothing`.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#truth-and-comparison"},{"type":"extras","title":"Arithmetic, bitwise, and string operations - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and `%` provide arithmetic. Division produces a float;\nremainder requires integers. `&`, `|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>`, and unary `~` operate\non integers. `^` is bitwise XOR, not exponentiation. Unary minus is not\nimplemented; write `0 - 5` for a negative constant or receive a negative\nnumber from host data.\n\n```anemos\nrule arithmetic {\n  when EXAMPLE {\n    log 2 + 3 * 4\n    log (2 + 3) * 4\n    log 5 % 2\n    log 5 & 3\n    log 1 << 3\n    log 0 - 5\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe values are `14`, `20`, `1`, `1`, `8`, and `-5`.\n\n`contains`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`, and `matches_regex` test strings:\n\n```anemos\nrule filenames {\n  when FILE_SEEN {\n    if ?path ends_with \".pdf\" {\n      log \"PDF candidate\"\n    }\n    if ?name matches_regex \"^[a-z]+$\" {\n      log \"lowercase name\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRegex patterns are strings, not slash-delimited literals. Escape backslashes\nfor the string layer. Invalid regexes fail at evaluation time. `matches_glob`\nis recognized by the parser but has no working evaluator; use a regex or a\nhost verb. `++` is tokenized but is not implemented string concatenation.\nGeneral string interpolation is also not implemented. Build formatted text in\na host module when a fixed message and separate value logs are insufficient.\n\n`in` and `notin` test list membership. `memberof(dataset)` checks a named\ndataset (Chapter 8). These operators bind unusually loosely: when combining\nmembership with logical tests, parenthesize each test, for example\n`(?name in ?names) AND (?enabled == true)`.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#arithmetic-bitwise-and-string-operations"},{"type":"extras","title":"Logical operators also carry values - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"For boolean operands, `AND`/`and` and `OR`/`or` behave as conjunction and\ndisjunction. With other values, `AND` compounds operands and `OR` chooses\nthe first available one:\n\n```anemos\nrule records {\n  when UPDATE {\n    let records = (:host IN A \"192.0.2.1\") AND (:host IN AAAA \"2001:db8::1\")\n    log @records\n    let selected = ?preferred OR ?fallback\n    log @selected\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe first log value is the list:\n\n```elixir\n[[\"host\", \"IN\", \"A\", \"192.0.2.1\"],\n [\"host\", \"IN\", \"AAAA\", \"2001:db8::1\"]]\n```\n\nInside a parenthesized multi-value pattern, `IN` is data. The pattern is\npreserved as a list rather than evaluated as a membership test. Do not confuse\nthis record-building form with ordinary `value in collection` expressions.\n\nAn `AND` chain preserves each non-boolean operand as one list element; it\ndoes not flatten a list operand's contents. `true` contributes no value. Any\n`false` or `nil` absorbs the chain and becomes its answer; the first such\noperand wins. With one non-boolean value and otherwise `true`, the answer is\nthat value, not a singleton list. An all-true chain answers `true`.\n\n`OR` discards falsey choices and answers the first truthy operand. If all\noperands are falsey, it answers the first operand. `||` is the same alternation\noperator. `&&` retains its separate behavior: it answers its left operand if\nfalsey, otherwise its right operand. It does not compound values.\n\n**Evaluation is eager.** Both operands of these operators are evaluated even\nwhen the answer can already be determined. `?cached OR [CACHE::fetch]` calls\n`CACHE::fetch` even when `?cached` has a value. Use an `if` when you need to\navoid a call. A condition such as `false AND [DEVICE::set ...]` still invokes\nthe value-position command.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#logical-operators-also-carry-values"},{"type":"extras","title":"Precedence - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"Multiplication binds more tightly than addition, and comparisons more tightly\nthan logical operators. Parentheses override precedence. Membership/string\ntests bind less tightly than logical operators; this differs from many familiar\nlanguages. The [full precedence table](reference.md#operator-precedence) gives\nevery level. Parenthesize mixed tests for readers as well as for the parser.","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#precedence"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exercises - 3. Values and expressions","doc":"1. What is `0 OR 7`? What is `false OR 7`?\n2. What is `\"a\" AND \"b\" AND \"c\"`? What is `\"a\" && \"b\"`?\n3. How do you avoid calling `LOOKUP::read` when `?cached` exists?\n\nAnswers: (1) `0` and `7`. (2) `[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]` and `\"b\"`.\n(3) Branch on `exists ?cached`; place the call only in the other branch.\n\n[Next: Decisions and control flow](04-control-flow.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"03-values-and-expressions.html#exercises"},{"type":"extras","title":"4. Decisions and control flow","doc":"# 4. Decisions and control flow\n\nAn event selects a handler. Control flow decides how much of that handler is\nevaluated and where work goes next. Start with ordinary branches; add guards\nor handoffs when they make the policy's structure clearer.","ref":"04-control-flow.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Branch on a value - 4. Decisions and control flow","doc":"```anemos\nrule classify-temperature {\n  when READING {\n    if ?temperature >= 30 {\n      log \"hot\"\n    } elseif ?temperature >= 20 {\n      log \"comfortable\"\n    } else {\n      log \"cool\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe first true branch runs. Subsequent branches are skipped. A numeric\ntemperature of `32` logs only `\"hot\"`; `22` logs only `\"comfortable\"`.\n`else if` is also accepted. `else` is optional. Blocks may contain nested\ndecisions and any supported handler statements.\n\nWhen a branch needs a value later, decide whether it should be local to this\nevaluation or retained for the next event. A `let` created only in one branch\nwill be absent on a path that never executes that branch. Prefer assigning\nand consuming related values within the same branch.","ref":"04-control-flow.html#branch-on-a-value"},{"type":"extras","title":"Stop a handler early - 4. Decisions and control flow","doc":"```anemos\nrule accept-reading {\n  when READING {\n    if not exists ?temperature {\n      log \"Missing temperature\"\n      return\n    }\n    log ?temperature\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`return` and `done` both stop the current handler immediately, including when\nthey appear inside a loop or nested branch. Earlier queued operations and\n`set` updates are retained. Other independent rules listening for the event\nare still eligible to run.\n\nAlthough `return  ` parses, the current evaluator discards the\nexpression rather than supplying a function return value. Use a host module\nfor value-returning computations, or an explicit handoff payload for another\nrule. A query's `return ?name` is a different construct (Chapter 8).\n\n`assert expression` is a useful related statement: it records whether an\nexpression held and stops this handler if it did not. A failed assertion is\nan inspection result with `held: false`, not an ordinary module error. The\nCLI's absence of operation errors is therefore not proof that every assertion\nheld. Inspect assertion results when using them as test expectations.","ref":"04-control-flow.html#stop-a-handler-early"},{"type":"extras","title":"Skip a trigger with unless - 4. Decisions and control flow","doc":"```anemos\nrule notify-motion {\n  when MOTION unless ?maintenance {\n    log \"Motion notification\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe guard is evaluated afresh for every firing. If the maintenance field is\ntruthy, the body is skipped. A missing field is falsey, so the body runs.\nUse typed booleans in the host payload; the string `\"false\"` suppresses this\nrule just as any other nonempty string does.\n\n`unless` also works on `if` and `at` blocks. On an `if`, a true guard skips\nthe whole construct, including its `else`. It does not choose the false branch.\nGuards run before the body's local `let` bindings exist. Read event data or\nprevious persistent bindings there, not a value the body has yet to create.\n\n## `until` and stopping permanently\n\n`when EVENT until expression` is intended to disable a trigger after the\nexpression first becomes true. In the current runtime its latch is not\nreliably preserved after a later successful handler evaluation. `until` on\n`if` is ignored; it has no persistent branch lifetime. Do not rely on either\nform for irreversible completion.\n\nFor a reliable run-once policy, make the state explicit:\n\n```anemos\nrule announce-once {\n  when READY unless $announced {\n    log \"Ready for work\"\n    set announced = true\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBecause `$announced` remains true, later `READY` events are skipped. This\nmeans once per retained rule state, not once forever: replacing the declaration\nwith changed source or restarting the whole runtime resets it.","ref":"04-control-flow.html#skip-a-trigger-with-unless"},{"type":"extras","title":"Hand work to another event - 4. Decisions and control flow","doc":"```anemos\nrule begin-job {\n  when START {\n    let destination = ?path\n    nextevent PROCESS with { path: @destination, stage: \"processing\" }\n    log \"unreachable\"\n  }\n}\n\nrule process-job {\n  when PROCESS {\n    log ?path\n    log ?stage\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`nextevent` stops the sending handler and redispatches the target event.\nCurrent behavior broadcasts to **all matching rules**, including other named\nrules. It is not confined to other `when` blocks in the sending rule.\nContext filtering still applies, and the arriving context path is retained.\n\nThe optional `with` block resolves expressions in the sender's environment\nand overlays its fields on the originating payload. Unmentioned fields\nsurvive; a supplied field wins on a collision. The receiver reads `?path`\nand `?stage`. `@destination` itself is not shared with the receiver.\n\nKeys are simple names followed by a colon; use comma-separated pairs for\nclarity. This block is a handoff argument form, not a general map literal.","ref":"04-control-flow.html#hand-work-to-another-event"},{"type":"extras","title":"Hand work to a named rule - 4. Decisions and control flow","doc":"```anemos\nrule start-audit {\n  when START {\n    nextrule audit-job with { message: \"started\" }\n  }\n}\n\nrule audit-job {\n  when AUDIT {\n    log ?message\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`nextrule` selects by declaration name and invokes the target's compiled\nhandlers without requiring the incoming event to match their subscriptions.\nA target with several `when` blocks can therefore run several handlers. Keep\nhandoff targets narrow when you want one action. The target still obeys its\ncontext, guards, and dwell behavior. A missing target produces\n`anemos.runtime/unknown_rule`.\n\nBoth handoffs increase dispatch depth. Cycles eventually hit the runtime's\nrecursion ceiling. Depth limiting prevents unbounded nested dispatch; it is\nnot a replacement for a termination condition in your policy.","ref":"04-control-flow.html#hand-work-to-a-named-rule"},{"type":"extras","title":"Choosing a control construct - 4. Decisions and control flow","doc":"Use `if` for a decision within one event. Use `unless` for a whole-trigger\nexclusion. Use `return` or `done` for an early exit. Use `nextevent` when the\nnext stage is an event that any suitable listener may consume. Use `nextrule`\nwhen you deliberately address a named policy component.\n\nFor application workflows that need durable steps, retries, or rollback,\nprovide those guarantees in host services. Anemos handoffs are immediate\nin-process dispatch, not a persistent job queue.\n\n[Next: Collections and pipelines](05-collections.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"04-control-flow.html#choosing-a-control-construct"},{"type":"extras","title":"5. Collections and pipelines","doc":"# 5. Collections and pipelines\n\nBatch work brings two questions: how to perform an action for every item, and\nhow to turn a collection into another value. Loops answer the first question;\npipelines are often clearer for the second.\n\nThe examples below receive lists from the host API. CLI `--context-data`\ncannot construct a typed list from a string.","ref":"05-collections.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Iterate with for - 5. Collections and pipelines","doc":"```anemos\nrule print-items {\n  when BATCH {\n    for item in ?items {\n      log @item\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGiven `%{\"items\" => [\"alpha\", \"beta\"]}`, this logs `\"alpha\"`, then\n`\"beta\"`. The loop variable is an `@` binding. Each iteration replaces that\ntemporary binding, and the evaluator removes it after the iteration. Avoid\nreusing the name of an outer `let`; the current loop cleanup does not restore\nan outer binding of the same name.\n\n`set` changes inside the loop survive into the next iteration and the next\nevent. A local binding with another name can also pass through the loop's\nevaluation; braces do not create general-purpose lexical scopes. Choose loop\nvariables that make their role and lifetime obvious.\n\nA missing iterable yields no iterations. A non-list value is wrapped as a\nsingle item. Maps and sets are not automatically traversed as collections of\nentries: have the host return a list when you intend item-by-item work.\n\nThere is no `break` or `continue`. A `return` inside the loop exits the whole\nhandler. To skip an item, guard that iteration's work with an `if`.","ref":"05-collections.html#iterate-with-for"},{"type":"extras","title":"Loop aliases - 5. Collections and pipelines","doc":"`each item in ?items { ... }` and `every item in ?items { ... }` are aliases\nfor explicit `for` iteration. The shorter forms `each item { ... }` and\n`every item { ... }` parse but currently have no iterable, so they perform\nno work. They do not infer a payload field. Always give an explicit source.\n\n`foreach FILE::list { ... }` invokes the module verb and iterates its returned\nlist as `@item`. The module must be registered. Prefer explicit `for item in\n[FILE::list]` when you need to see the collection expression directly.","ref":"05-collections.html#loop-aliases"},{"type":"extras","title":"Repeat with while - 5. Collections and pipelines","doc":"```anemos\nrule countdown {\n  when START {\n    set remaining = 3\n    while $remaining > 0 {\n      log $remaining\n      set remaining = $remaining - 1\n    }\n    log \"finished\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis logs `3`, `2`, `1`, and `\"finished\"`. The condition is evaluated before\neach iteration. Ensure the body changes something that makes it false.\nThe interpreter stops repeating at 10,000 iterations; hitting that safety\nlimit is not a useful successful outcome. Bound the work yourself.\n\nFor a known collection, use `for`. It describes intent more directly and\navoids a counter whose only job is controlling a loop.","ref":"05-collections.html#repeat-with-while"},{"type":"extras","title":"Filter, map, reduce - 5. Collections and pipelines","doc":"The pipe operator `|>` passes the value on its left into the stage on its\nright. A pipeline is an expression; bind or log its answer.\n\n```anemos\nrule summarize {\n  when BATCH {\n    let total = ?values\n      |> filter ?n (?n > 0)\n      |> map ?n (?n * 2)\n      |> reduce sum 0 ?n ($sum + ?n)\n    log @total\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor `[-1, 2, -3, 4, 5]`, follow the values through the stages:\n\n| Stage | Answer |\n| --- | --- |\n| Input | `[-1, 2, -3, 4, 5]` |\n| `filter` | `[2, 4, 5]` |\n| `map` | `[4, 8, 10]` |\n| `reduce` | `22` |\n\n`filter ?n (predicate)` evaluates its predicate with the current element\navailable as `?n`. It retains elements whose predicates are truthy.\n`map ?n (expression)` replaces each element with the expression's answer.\nThese variables are `?` references, unlike a `for` loop's `@item`.\n\n`reduce sum 0 ?n (expression)` starts an accumulator called `sum` at `0`.\nFor each element, the body reads the current accumulator as `$sum` and the\nelement as `?n`, then returns the next accumulator. This accumulator belongs\nto the reduction. It does not by itself create persistent rule state named\n`sum`.\n\nEmpty-list filtering and mapping return an empty list. Reducing an empty\nlist returns the initial accumulator. The collection built-ins wrap a scalar\nas a singleton list and treat missing `nil` as an empty list.","ref":"05-collections.html#filter-map-reduce"},{"type":"extras","title":"Pass a collection to a host command - 5. Collections and pipelines","doc":"```anemos\nrule submit-batch {\n  when BATCH {\n    let accepted = ?values |> filter ?n (?n > 0)\n    let receipt = @accepted |> [QUEUE::submit \"normal\"]\n    log @receipt\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe pipeline prepends its collection to the explicit command arguments.\n`QUEUE::submit` receives `[accepted_list, \"normal\"]`; it is called once\nfor the collection, not once per element. Its returned value becomes the\npipeline answer. `QUEUE` is supplied by the host.\n\nThe parser also accepts `|> emit [MODULE::verb ...]`. In this version that\nform constructs descriptors that are discarded rather than reliably\ndispatching one operation per item. Use an explicit `for` loop with a command\nstatement for side effects:\n\n```anemos\nrule submit-each {\n  when BATCH {\n    for item in ?items {\n      [QUEUE::submit @item \"normal\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRemember the operation model from Chapter 2: these standalone commands are\nqueued during handler evaluation, then executed. A host verb used inside a\npipeline expression runs during evaluation.","ref":"05-collections.html#pass-a-collection-to-a-host-command"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exercise - 5. Collections and pipelines","doc":"Change the summarizing pipeline to retain values greater than `2`, then\nmultiply by `3` and reduce from `10`. What does the same input produce?\n\nAnswer: `[4, 5]` becomes `[12, 15]`, which reduces from `10` to `37`.\n\n[Next: Conditions and capabilities](06-conditions-and-capabilities.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"05-collections.html#exercise"},{"type":"extras","title":"6. Conditions and capabilities","doc":"# 6. Conditions and capabilities\n\nSome automation is about a change in a continuing situation rather than each\nindividual message. Other automation needs a named piece of work that is\nrequested explicitly. Conditions and capabilities give these ideas names.","ref":"06-conditions-and-capabilities.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Combine reports in a rule - 6. Conditions and capabilities","doc":"A compound trigger remembers the last report of each operand:\n\n```anemos\nrule ready-to-work {\n  when (NETWORK_UP AND STORAGE_READY) {\n    log \"Both prerequisites are available\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe operands start false. An arriving event reports true unless its payload\ncontains a `state` field whose value is boolean `false` or `nil`. A report\nstands until another report of the same event replaces it. Missing `state`\nmeans true; the string `\"false\"` means true too.\n\n| Incoming report | Remembered network | Remembered storage | Body runs? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `NETWORK_UP`, no state field | true | false | No |\n| `STORAGE_READY`, no state field | true | true | Yes |\n| `NETWORK_UP`, `state: true` | true | true | Yes |\n| `STORAGE_READY`, `state: false` | true | false | No |\n\nA compound rule is evaluated on every operand report. It can run repeatedly\nwhile the combined state is true. `OR` instead requires at least one true\nremembered operand. Each listener keeps only reports admitted by its own\ncontext. Use a single homogeneous `AND` or `OR` group; nested mixed event\nalgebra is not a general expression language for triggers.\n\nA plain `when EVENT` means the event arrived. Do not expect a plain trigger\nto filter a false `state` report the same way a compound state expression\ndoes. Branch explicitly when a payload value matters.","ref":"06-conditions-and-capabilities.html#combine-reports-in-a-rule"},{"type":"extras","title":"Name a continuing condition - 6. Conditions and capabilities","doc":"If you want to react once when readiness becomes true, use a condition:\n\n```anemos\ncondition ready {\n  when (NETWORK_UP AND STORAGE_READY) {\n    log \"Readiness became true\"\n  }\n}\n\nrule start-work {\n  when CONDITION_ready {\n    log \"Start the work\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe condition begins false. On a false-to-true transition, it evaluates its\nside-effect body and generates `CONDITION_ready`. A true-to-true evaluation\ndoes neither. A report that makes the compound false resets the condition;\na later true transition can generate another notification.\n\nIn a persistent runtime, use `Anemos.Runtime.condition_state(runtime, \"ready\")`\nto read its current boolean. `nil` means no condition of that name is loaded.\nThe DSL has no general built-in `?ready` lookup of condition state: `?name`\nresolves context, queries, and datasets. Consume `CONDITION_ready`, or have\nthe host expose the current state when other rules need to read it.","ref":"06-conditions-and-capabilities.html#name-a-continuing-condition"},{"type":"extras","title":"Predicates refine a condition - 6. Conditions and capabilities","doc":"A top-level `if` in a condition is a predicate that must hold in addition to\nthe trigger:\n\n```anemos\ncondition hot {\n  when READING {\n    log \"Entered the hot state\"\n  }\n  if ?temperature > 30 {}\n}\n```\n\nReadings `29`, `31`, `32`, `28`, `33` cause two false-to-true transitions:\none at `31` and another at `33`. The top-level `if` body is not an additional\naction block. Put transition actions inside the trigger body. An `if` nested\ninside that body controls actions, not the condition's truth.\n\nA condition with no `when` or `at` trigger cannot become true. Unrelated\nevents do not reset a condition. A single-event condition's truth is based\non its trigger and predicates, so use an explicit predicate if a report's\npayload should turn it false. A compound condition uses remembered reports.\n\nFor predictable action selection, use one trigger body per condition. The\ncurrent compiler combines all `when`/`at` bodies into one transition body;\nseparate bodies are not independently selected by which trigger matched.\nCondition guards are evaluated from event context, without persistent local\nbinding state. `until` there is a fresh exclusion test, not a permanent latch.\n\nConditions may declare a context just as rules do. Only admitted events\naffect their reports and state. Generated transition dispatch retains the\noriginating event context, so a receiving rule must also be reachable there.","ref":"06-conditions-and-capabilities.html#predicates-refine-a-condition"},{"type":"extras","title":"Define work to perform on demand - 6. Conditions and capabilities","doc":"```anemos\ncapability audit-request {\n  context building\n  on demand {\n    log ?request_id\n  }\n}\n\nrule process-request {\n  context building.garage\n  when REQUEST {\n    demand audit-request\n    log \"Request received\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA capability is passive until demanded. Its `on demand` body can read event\ndata. Its declared context restricts who may demand it: the **demanding\ndeclaration's** context must lie inside the capability's context. In this\nexample `building.garage` is inside `building`. A caller event arriving from\n`building.garage.sensor` does not rescue a demanding rule that declares no\ncontext; the rule itself must present an eligible scope.\n\nAn unscoped capability can be demanded from any scope. A nonexistent capability\nand a context denial are errors under every demand flag, including `--optional`.\nOptional means tolerance of execution failure, not tolerance of a misspelled\nname or a crossed boundary.","ref":"06-conditions-and-capabilities.html#define-work-to-perform-on-demand"},{"type":"extras","title":"Demand flags and the current integration limit - 6. Conditions and capabilities","doc":"The demand evaluator defines a stack protocol:\n\n| Flag | Stack meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| Bare or `--requisite` | Failure ends the stack immediately |\n| `--required` | Remember failure and evaluate the remaining stack |\n| `--optional` | Tolerate execution failure |\n| `--sufficient` | Success ends the stack successfully |\n\nHowever, **policy dispatch currently evaluates each `demand` statement as its\nown one-element stack**. A failed demand appears in the operation results;\nit does not stop later queued commands or demands. `--sufficient` does not\nskip later policy statements. The distinction between `--required` and\n`--requisite` therefore does not create a multi-statement gate in a rule.\n\nThere is a second limit: standalone operations returned by a capability body\nare reported, but are not recursively executed by the demand path. An\nexpression-position host call inside that body executes while the body is\nevaluated. A standalone `[MODULE::verb ...]` inside it is only a returned\noperation descriptor on this path. A body error operation is likewise not\nthe same as a failed stack result.\n\nThese limits make `demand` unsuitable as a security gate in this version.\nFor an actual authorization decision, have a host verb return a boolean,\nbind it, and branch before producing protected operations:\n\n```anemos\nrule authorized-request {\n  when REQUEST {\n    let allowed = [AUTH::allowed ?principal]\n    if @allowed == true {\n      [WORK::perform ?job]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe host supplies `AUTH` and `WORK`. A failed value-position authorization\ncall aborts evaluation, and only boolean `true` takes the branch. This makes\nthe required decision explicit without depending on incomplete stack wiring.","ref":"06-conditions-and-capabilities.html#demand-flags-and-the-current-integration-limit"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exercise - 6. Conditions and capabilities","doc":"Send `A=true`, `B=true`, `A=true`, `B=false`, `B=true` to both a rule with\n`when (A AND B)` and a condition with that trigger. How often does each act?\n\nAnswer: the rule acts three times (reports 2, 3, and 5). The condition\ntransitions to true twice (reports 2 and 5).\n\n[Next: Time and duration](07-time.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"06-conditions-and-capabilities.html#exercise"},{"type":"extras","title":"7. Time and duration","doc":"# 7. Time and duration\n\nThere are two different reasons to wait. “The door has remained open for five\nminutes” depends on reports of a continuing state. “Check the door every five\nminutes” depends only on a clock. Anemos expresses the first with a dwell\nduration on `when`, and the second with an `at` schedule.\n\nBoth require a running interpreter. The command-line runner and `Anemos.run/2`\nare one-shot tools; they are not services that stay alive to deliver future\ntimers. Use the supervised runtime from Chapter 9.","ref":"07-time.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"A duration is an amount and a unit - 7. Time and duration","doc":"Use compact suffixes with no intervening space:\n\n| Unit | Meaning | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ms` | Milliseconds | `250ms` |\n| `s` | Seconds | `30s` |\n| `min` | Minutes | `5min` |\n| `h` | Hours | `2h` |\n| `d` | Days of 24 hours | `1d` |\n\nThe duration converter also recognizes the long names `seconds`, `minutes`,\n`hours`, and `days`; use the compact spellings in source because tokenization\nof the longer forms is not uniformly reliable. Amounts are whole numbers.\nDurations are special trigger/schedule syntax, not a general arithmetic unit\nsystem.","ref":"07-time.html#a-duration-is-an-amount-and-a-unit"},{"type":"extras","title":"Wait for a reported state to persist - 7. Time and duration","doc":"```anemos\nrule door-left-open {\n  when DOOR_OPEN for 5min {\n    log \"Door has remained open for five minutes\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe first truthy `DOOR_OPEN` report arms a timer. Repeated truthy reports do\nnot restart it. A falsey `state` report cancels it. If the deadline arrives\nwithout cancellation, the body runs once. Continued true reports after that\ndo not repeatedly fire it; a false report must reset it before a new true\nreport can arm another dwell.\n\nThe host should dispatch both changes under the same event name:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"DOOR_OPEN\",\n  %{context_data: %{\"state\" => true}})\n# Later, if the door closes:\nAnemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"DOOR_OPEN\",\n  %{context_data: %{\"state\" => false}})\n```\n\nSending a different event called `DOOR_CLOSED` does not cancel the\n`DOOR_OPEN` timer. A dwell assumes the last report continues to hold; it\ncannot detect a failed sensor that simply stops reporting.\n\nThe timer uses the event context captured when it was armed. Do not assume\nlater true reports replace its payload. Its state belongs to the named rule\nand trigger, not independently to every device that can reach that rule.\nGive independent monitored entities independent state ownership.\n\nCompound `AND`/`OR` triggers may have dwell durations too. The remembered\ncompound state determines whether the timer remains eligible. Use `for 0s`\nfor immediate behavior; it is compiled as no dwell. A condition's `when ...\nfor` duration is not implemented as a condition dwell timer; use a rule when\nduration is essential.","ref":"07-time.html#wait-for-a-reported-state-to-persist"},{"type":"extras","title":"Run at a time or interval - 7. Time and duration","doc":"```anemos\nrule housekeeping {\n  at interval(5min) {\n    log \"Periodic check\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAn interval repeats while the runtime is alive, with the first firing after\nthe interval. The default minimum interval is 1,000 milliseconds. A request\nbelow that floor is rejected rather than silently slowed down.\n\nThis is the one way to make something happen on a cadence. \"Do this every\nfive minutes, forever\" is an `at interval(5min)` block whose body does the\nthing, including asking the host to refresh a dataset:\n\n```anemos\nrule refresh-gtld-servers {\n  at interval(5min) {\n    [INFRA::refresh \"gtld-servers\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe timer is re-armed when the runtime replays its policy store after a\nprocess restart. It does not survive a VM restart.\n\n```anemos\nrule lunch {\n  at 13:00 {\n    log \"Lunch\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA bare clock time schedules a single future occurrence: today if that time\nis still ahead, otherwise tomorrow. It is not automatically daily. For\nrecurrence, say so:\n\n```anemos\nrule calendar {\n  at 09:00 every day { log \"Morning\" }\n  at 11:00 every weekday { log \"Standup\" }\n  at 09:30 every monday { log \"Weekly planning\" }\n}\n```\n\n`weekday` means Monday through Friday. Named weekdays are lowercase. Valid\nclock times use a two-digit hour and minute, `00:00` through `23:59`.\nInvalid times can parse and still fail when the runtime tries to arm them;\nalways inspect load results.","ref":"07-time.html#run-at-a-time-or-interval"},{"type":"extras","title":"Dates and natural-language dates - 7. Time and duration","doc":"Equivalent prefixed forms and relative dates are available:\n\n```anemos\nrule reminders {\n  at TIME_1300 { log \"At one o'clock\" }\n  at DATE_2099.04.15 { log \"On the specified date\" }\n  at DAY_friday { log \"At the next Friday\" }\n  at NLD_tomorrow { log \"Tomorrow\" }\n  at NLD_next.wednesday { log \"Next Wednesday\" }\n  at NLD_end.of.month { log \"End of month\" }\n}\n```\n\n`DAY_friday` is a one-shot next-weekday occurrence, not a weekly recurrence.\nThe supported `NLD_` expressions are `today`, `tomorrow`, `yesterday`,\n`next.monday` through `next.sunday`, `next.week`, `next.month`, `next.year`,\n`end.of.month`, and `end.of.week`. They are resolved when a schedule is armed;\nthey are not a general natural-language parser. A resolution in the past,\nincluding many uses of `today` or `yesterday`, is refused as inert.\n\nDate and day forms target `00:00:01` on their date. Relative dates have these\ntargets:\n\n| Expression | Target |\n| --- | --- |\n| `today`, `tomorrow`, `yesterday` | That day's `00:00:01` |\n| `next. ` | Next occurrence, strictly after today, at `00:00:01` |\n| `next.week` | Next Monday at `00:00:01` |\n| `next.month` | First of next month at `00:00:01` |\n| `next.year` | January 1 of next year at `00:00:01` |\n| `end.of.month` | Last day of this month at `23:59:59` |\n| `end.of.week` | Next Sunday at `23:59:59`; on Sunday, the following Sunday |\n\nCalendar calculations use the machine's local naive date and time; there is\nno per-policy time-zone setting. Do not infer daylight-saving, cross-zone, or\nmissed-run guarantees. If those guarantees matter, let an external scheduler\ndeliver a named event under a documented time-zone policy.","ref":"07-time.html#dates-and-natural-language-dates"},{"type":"extras","title":"Context and scheduled events - 7. Time and duration","doc":"The built-in scheduler dispatches synthetic `AT_*` events with an empty\ncontext. A context-scoped listener therefore refuses those events. An `at`\ninside a scoped rule can load and arm successfully yet never reach its body.\nFor scoped timed work, have the host schedule an ordinary event and supply\nthe correct context, or use a deliberately unscoped scheduling rule whose\nhost operation has the appropriate authorization.\n\nSchedules with identical specifications share one synthetic event and one\ntimer. Several listeners can consume that event. Different enclosing rule\nnames keep their action handlers distinct; recurrence is part of schedule\nidentity. Avoid duplicate identical `at` blocks in one named rule.\n\nEach `at` body also has a generated handler name, distinct from its enclosing\nrule's ordinary `when` handlers. Its `$` bindings therefore do not share the\nordinary rule's persistent state automatically. For timed work that must\nconsume the same event-driven state, have the host dispatch a scheduled\nordinary event to a `when` block in that rule.","ref":"07-time.html#context-and-scheduled-events"},{"type":"extras","title":"Observe scheduling failures - 7. Time and duration","doc":"`Anemos.Runtime.load/3` returns `{:partial, failures}` if rules loaded but\none or more schedules could not be armed. A past date, unsupported relative\ndate, interval below the floor, or schedule cap can cause this. The failure's\n`field` identifies the synthetic event. `Runtime.describe/1` reports loaded\nschedules and their last firing times.\n\nOne-shot firing records and supported timer state survive scheduler/dispatcher\nworker restarts within the runtime. They are not durable delivery records\nacross a VM restart. External effects are not guaranteed exactly once.\n\n[Next: Datasets, queries, and facts](08-data.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"07-time.html#observe-scheduling-failures"},{"type":"extras","title":"8. Datasets, queries, and facts","doc":"# 8. Datasets, queries, and facts\n\nPayloads are convenient for values that arrive with an event. Reusable\nreference data belongs in a dataset. Computed lookups belong in a query.\nSubject–predicate–object facts belong in the triple store. Each has a\ndifferent access model; this chapter keeps those models explicit.","ref":"08-data.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"A named dataset - 8. Datasets, queries, and facts","doc":"```anemos\ndata allowed_users {\n  alice\n  bob\n  \"Grace Hopper\"\n}\n\nrule check-user {\n  when LOGIN {\n    if ?user memberof(allowed_users) {\n      log \"Allowed\"\n    } else {\n      log \"Denied\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe simple data block creates a set. Duplicate entries collapse. Membership\nis exact; there is no case folding or substring matching. Quoted strings can\ncontain spaces. The simple-list parser represents numeric entries as strings,\nso an entry `42` matches host `\"42\"`, not integer `42`.\n\n`memberof(first, second)` tests the union of named datasets. A value need be\nin only one. A referenced dataset that was never registered is an error,\n`anemos.runtime/unknown_dataset`, rather than an empty set. This matters for\npolicies whose decisions depend on successful data loading.\n\nBare address/CIDR entries are accepted, but membership remains exact. A set\ncontaining `192.0.2.0/24` does not automatically contain `192.0.2.7`. Supply\na host network-membership verb if you need subnet semantics.","ref":"08-data.html#a-named-dataset"},{"type":"extras","title":"Scope and name resolution - 8. Datasets, queries, and facts","doc":"A top-level data block is global within this runtime. A data block directly\ninside a rule or its context wrapper is local to that named rule:\n\n```anemos\nrule restricted-login {\n  data allowed_users {\n    alice\n  }\n  when LOGIN {\n    if ?user memberof(allowed_users) {\n      log \"Local member\"\n    }\n    log ?allowed_users\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnother rule cannot read this local block. A local definition wins over a\nglobal definition of the same name when looked up from its rule. Inside a\nrule, `data global allowed_users { ... }` explicitly installs global data.\nQueries use the same local/global convention.\n\nThese declarations are extracted and registered when the policy loads.\nPutting a data declaration inside a runtime branch does not make it\nconditional data creation. Keep declarations directly in the rule body or\ncontext wrapper, where extraction is supported.\n\n`?allowed_users` reads the visible stored dataset if context data and a query\nof that name do not take precedence. A simple list dataset is a host\n`MapSet`, not necessarily a list suitable for a `for` loop. Have a host\nmodule return a list when enumeration is needed.","ref":"08-data.html#scope-and-name-resolution"},{"type":"extras","title":"Structured data belongs to a handler - 8. Datasets, queries, and facts","doc":"Anemos supplies a plug-in boundary for formats. It does not bundle parsers\nfor every format named in design sketches. The host registers a handler\nimplementing `Anemos.DataModel`:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.register_data_model(:anemos, MyApp.Words)\n```\n\nAfter registration, its keyword can appear in a format block:\n\n```anemos\ndata crew {\n  words { alice bob }\n}\n\nrule check-crew {\n  when LOGIN {\n    if ?user memberof(crew) { log \"Crew member\" }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis policy requires a host handler registered under `\"words\"`. The runtime\nasks the handler to parse the block body, evaluate the parsed representation,\nand optionally transform it. A missing or failed handler makes the data\ndeclaration inert and appears in the load's partial failures.\n\nFormat and query engine bodies are reconstructed from DSL tokens, not passed\nthrough as arbitrary byte-for-byte source. Whitespace, comments, and quoting\ncan change. Do not assume an arbitrary JSON, SQL, XML, or EDN document can be\npasted unchanged merely because you registered a handler with that name.\nThe handler must accept the representation Anemos delivers. Chapter 9 supplies\na complete deliberately simple handler.\n\nAn optional `source resource.path` records provenance metadata. It is not an\nautomatic fetch. A `transclude` clause requests a handler transformation:\n\n```anemos\ndata crew {\n  words { alice bob }\n  transclude to shape(resource.schema.crew)\n    with resource.transducer.upcase as loud_crew\n}\n```\n\nThe original dataset remains `crew`; the transformed result is registered\nas `loud_crew`. The language keyword is `transclude`; the handler callback is\n`transduce/3`. A handler must explicitly implement the requested transformation.","ref":"08-data.html#structured-data-belongs-to-a-handler"},{"type":"extras","title":"Queries name a computation - 8. Datasets, queries, and facts","doc":"```anemos\nquery greet {\n  expect :name\n  greeter { hello }\n  return ?line\n}\n\nrule welcome {\n  when ARRIVAL {\n    let name = ?visitor\n    log ?greet\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe host must register a `greeter` data-model handler. Reading `?greet`\nexecutes the query. For each `expect` name, Anemos looks for a local `let`,\nthen a persistent `set`, then event/context data, using string keys. Several\nexpectations are comma-separated: `expect :name, :tenant`.\n\nThe engine's evaluated result must be a map binding the returned name, here\n`\"line\"`. That field becomes the value of `?greet`. Reading the query again\nexecutes it again; it is not an automatic cache. Missing input, missing engine,\nfailed evaluation, and missing return binding are errors that abort the\ncalling handler's evaluation.\n\nQueries do not make arbitrary dataset or condition names into query engines.\nThe engine keyword identifies registered host code, and its syntax and answer\nshape are part of that host's contract. Event data called `greet` shadows\nthis query, so avoid colliding field names.","ref":"08-data.html#queries-name-a-computation"},{"type":"extras","title":"Live datasets - 8. Datasets, queries, and facts","doc":"A dynamic data block subscribes through a host-supplied protocol adapter:\n\n```anemos\ndata live_users {\n  dynamic {\n    subscribe directory \"directory://users\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`directory` is a registered protocol string, not a built-in network service.\nAn adapter implements `Anemos.Subscribable`; the dispatcher API registers it:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.Dispatcher.register_subscribable(\n  :\"anemos.dispatcher\", \"directory\", MyApp.DirectoryFeed)\n```\n\nThe dataset starts empty after a successful subscription. Each pushed value\nreplaces it; a pushed list is converted to a set. Updates do not automatically\ndispatch a policy event. If reacting immediately to changes matters, arrange\nfor your adapter/application to dispatch one as well.\n\nThe adapter owns polling and lifecycle. A source that must be pulled on a\ncadence, such as a git repository refreshed daily, is an adapter whose\n`subscribe/3` starts its own timer and pushes on each pull. There is no\ncadence vocabulary on a data block. If the cadence belongs in the policy\nrather than the host, use an `at interval(...)` rule that asks the host to\nrefresh; see [chapter 7](07-time.md#run-at-a-time-or-interval). A missing\nprotocol handler is reported as a partial load failure. Unloading the owning\npolicy cancels its live subscriptions.","ref":"08-data.html#live-datasets"},{"type":"extras","title":"Facts and triple patterns - 8. Datasets, queries, and facts","doc":"Anemos can accumulate triples: a subject, predicate, and object.\n\n```anemos\nrule record-facts {\n  when SETUP {\n    let [:alice -> :worksOn -> :project1]\n    let [:bob -> :worksOn -> :project1]\n    graph :alice\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`let [subject -> predicate -> object]` asserts a fact. It is a special\nstatement, not an ordinary named binding. Duplicate assertions do not create\nduplicate facts. `graph :alice` inspects facts involving that identifier.\nThe triple store is shared within the runtime; context does not create\nindependent named graphs or access isolation for facts.\n\nThe parser also accepts query patterns such as:\n\n```text\n[?person :worksOn ?project]\n[:alice :worksOn :project1]\n```\n\nHere `?person` and `?project` are pattern variables, not ordinary payload\nlookups. However, ordinary DSL value evaluation currently produces a query\ndescriptor rather than a bound result collection. `log [?person :worksOn\n?project]` logs the descriptor; it does not bind `?person` for subsequent\nstatements. Standalone triple AST statements are not a complete query path\neither. Use `graph` for policy-level inspection or a host query/module for\nprogrammatic fact matching. The lower-level `TripleStore.match/4` API returns\nbinding maps and can support such a host implementation.\n\nDo not base a policy decision on a triple descriptor's truthiness: a map is\ntruthy even if the intended query would match no facts. The reference records\nthis as a partial feature rather than promising a full fact-query language.\n\n[Next: Embedding and extending Anemos](09-embedding.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"08-data.html#facts-and-triple-patterns"},{"type":"extras","title":"9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"# 9. Embedding and extending Anemos\n\nAnemos is an interpreter library. The host application starts it, installs\nmodules and policies, and supplies events. This chapter builds that host one\npiece at a time. You can run the examples in `iex -S mix` from this checkout.","ref":"09-embedding.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Start a persistent runtime - 9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"```elixir\n{:ok, runtime_pid} = Anemos.Runtime.start_link(name: :anemos)\n```\n\nIn an application, put the runtime in its supervision tree instead:\n\n```elixir\nchildren = [\n  {Anemos.Runtime, name: :anemos}\n]\nSupervisor.start_link(children, strategy: :one_for_one)\n```\n\nChoose one of these approaches; do not start two runtimes with the same name.\nNames are host-controlled atoms. Use a bounded set of configured names rather\nthan generating atoms from user input.\n\nA persistent runtime starts with `POLICY` registered, but without the\ndevelopment `DEVICE` and `FILE` defaults used by `Anemos.run/2`. Register\nwhat your application needs. Supplying modules at startup is especially\nuseful with a watcher that loads policies immediately:\n\n```elixir\n{Anemos.Runtime, name: :anemos, modules: %{\"DEVICE\" => MyApp.Device}}\n```","ref":"09-embedding.html#start-a-persistent-runtime"},{"type":"extras","title":"Define a real command contract - 9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"This small host module performs a deterministic calculation, so you can test\nthe whole path without any external service:\n\n```elixir\ndefmodule Tutorial.Math do\n  use Anemos.Module, name: \"MATH\"\n\n  @impl Anemos.Runtime.Module\n  def arg_schema(\"double\"), do: [[type: :number]]\n  def arg_schema(_verb), do: nil\n\n  verb :double, fn [number], _context -> {:ok, number * 2} end\nend\n\n:ok = Anemos.Runtime.register(:anemos, Tutorial.Math)\n```\n\nEvery handler receives a list of resolved arguments and a context map, and\nreturns `{:ok, value}` or `{:error, reason}`. The macro supplies the module\nname, unknown-verb fallback, and basic introspection. You can also implement\nthe `Anemos.Runtime.Module` behavior directly and register an explicit name:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.register(:anemos, \"MATH\", Tutorial.Math)\n```\n\nNow load a policy:\n\n```anemos\nrule calculate {\n  when NUMBER {\n    let doubled = [MATH::double ?number]\n    log @doubled\n  }\n}\n```\n\nPut that source in a string called `source`, then:\n\n```elixir\n:ok = Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source, policy: \"math.rule\")\n{:ok, results} = Anemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"NUMBER\",\n  %{context_data: %{\"number\" => 21}})\n# results includes %{type: :log, value: 42}\n```\n\nIn value position, the command's unwrapped success value becomes `@doubled`.\nIn statement position, the command's success value is an operation result.\nHost failures in value position stop the current handler's evaluation. Use\nstructured domain errors where possible; the runtime also normalizes ordinary\nmodule failures into its error reporting system.\n\nRegistration is write-once by default. Registering the same name and module\nagain is idempotent; registering a different module under an occupied name\nreturns `anemos.runtime/already_registered`. A deliberate rebind uses the\nlower-level registry API with `replace: true` and is logged:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.ModuleRegistry.register(\n  :\"anemos.registry\", \"MATH\", MyApp.ReplacementMath, replace: true)\n```\n\nTreat changing a module's behavior as an API change for every policy using it.","ref":"09-embedding.html#define-a-real-command-contract"},{"type":"extras","title":"Validate inputs at the module boundary - 9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"The optional `arg_schema/1` callback declares positional arguments. The\ndispatcher applies it to both standalone and value-position calls before host\ncode runs. A `nil` schema leaves the verb unvalidated.\n\n| Spec key | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `type:` | `:string`, `:integer`, `:float`, `:number`, `:boolean`, `:atom`, `:any` |\n| `max_bytes:` | Limit a string's byte length |\n| `in:` | Restrict to an explicit list of values |\n| `optional: true` | Allow a trailing argument to be absent |\n| `rest: true` | Apply the final spec to remaining arguments |\n| `path:` | Normalize and confine a path to a configured root |\n\nRemember that policy symbols evaluate to strings, so `:atom` describes a\nvalue supplied by host data, not the DSL's `:symbol` syntax. Schemas do not\ncoerce CLI strings into numeric or boolean values. Business decisions and\ncross-argument constraints belong in the handler itself.\n\nFor a filesystem verb, configure a root and declare a path argument:\n\n```elixir\n# In config/config.exs:\nconfig :anemos, :path_roots, %{documents: \"/var/lib/my_app/documents\"}\n\n# In the host module:\ndef arg_schema(\"read\") do\n  [[type: :string, max_bytes: 4096, path: :documents]]\nend\n```\n\nThe validated argument becomes a confined absolute path. Pass that value\ndirectly to the filesystem API instead of resolving the original input again.\nThe shipped `FILE` is a stub; its shape checks alone do not make a future\nfilesystem implementation confined. `Anemos.Runtime.PathGuard` performs\nlexical normalization and containment. It does not resolve symlinks: an\nin-root link pointing outside the root can escape it. Use filesystem-level\nconfinement when that boundary must withstand changes to the directory tree.","ref":"09-embedding.html#validate-inputs-at-the-module-boundary"},{"type":"extras","title":"Supply event data - 9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"The string API accepts a context map:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"READING\", %{\n  context_path: \"building.garage.sensor\",\n  context_data: %{\"temperature\" => 24, \"enabled\" => true},\n  correlation_id: \"reading-001\"\n})\n```\n\nUse string payload keys. Supply all fields needed for `?` lookup in this\ndispatch's `context_data`. `Runtime.put_context/3` writes a separate context\nstore, but that store is not currently merged into ordinary dispatch lookup.\nTo supply shared defaults, merge them in the host before dispatch:\n\n```elixir\ndefaults = %{\"site\" => \"west\"}\npayload = Map.merge(defaults, %{\"temperature\" => 24})\nAnemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"READING\", %{context_data: payload})\n```\n\nFor ordinary maps there is also a protocol shorthand:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, %{\n  name: \"READING\",\n  context: \"building.garage.sensor\",\n  payload: %{\"temperature\" => 24}\n})\n```\n\nThe shorthand's outer keys are atoms and use `context`/`payload`; the string\nAPI's context map uses `context_path`/`context_data`. Confusing these shapes\nis a common cause of missing data or denied contexts.\n\nApplication event structs can implement `Anemos.Event` with `name/1`,\n`context/1`, and `payload/1`. Return an exact event-name string, a path or\n`nil`, and a payload map. The protocol form derives those fields from the\nevent; use the string form when you need the richer dispatch context.\n`correlation_id` in the string context is carried into the ambient Comn\ncontext used by operations.","ref":"09-embedding.html#supply-event-data"},{"type":"extras","title":"Emit values and connect a channel - 9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"`emit` currently takes a command expression. It evaluates that command,\nthen produces an emit operation containing its returned value:\n\n```anemos\nrule publish-reading {\n  when READING {\n    emit state [SENSOR::read] to channel(telemetry.readings) as :reading.ready\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe host supplies `SENSOR::read`. The optional type is `state`, `effect`, or\n`stream`; it is an envelope tag, not an automatic storage, streaming, or\ndelivery guarantee. `emit ?payload` is not the implemented emit syntax.\n\nWith a channel destination, the runtime calls the current connector's\n`publish/2` with an `Anemos.Envelope`. Its fields are:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `type` | `:state`, `:effect`, `:stream`, or `nil` |\n| `payload` | The command's returned value |\n| `source` | Declaration name, optionally `name@context` |\n| `timestamp` | UTC emission time |\n| `label` | The `as` label, if any |\n| `channel` | Destination path |\n| `signature` | Reserved field; publishing does not sign automatically |\n\nThe default connector, `Anemos.Channel.Logger`, logs channel emits. To use\nthe bundled in-process event bus connector:\n\n```elixir\n:ok = Anemos.Runtime.connect(:anemos, Anemos.Channel.ComnBus)\nComn.EventBus.subscribe(\"telemetry.readings\")\n# The subscriber receives {:event, topic, %Anemos.Envelope{}}\n```\n\nOr implement `Anemos.Channel.publish(channel, envelope)` and connect your\nmodule. A connector is host infrastructure, so design its retry, buffering,\nserialization, and error reporting explicitly. The current emit path does\nnot turn a connector's returned `{:error, reason}` into a failed operation.\n\nA binary `as` label also redispatches an event internally after the current\noperation list. The redispatch retains the originating context/payload; it\ndoes not automatically install the emitted command result as a new payload.\nUse `nextevent ... with` to send explicit fields. Labels are strings after\nparsing. Interpolated-label spellings are accepted but not dynamically\nresolved; prefer fixed labels with understood subscriptions.","ref":"09-embedding.html#emit-values-and-connect-a-channel"},{"type":"extras","title":"Supply a complete data handler - 9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"This implementation makes the `words` examples in Chapter 8 concrete:\n\n```elixir\ndefmodule Tutorial.Words do\n  @behaviour Anemos.DataModel\n\n  @impl true\n  def keyword, do: \"words\"\n\n  @impl true\n  def parse(raw), do: {:ok, String.split(raw)}\n\n  @impl true\n  def eval(words, _context), do: {:ok, words}\n\n  @impl true\n  def transduce(words, _shape, \"resource.transducer.upcase\") do\n    {:ok, Enum.map(words, &String.upcase/1)}\n  end\n  def transduce(_words, _shape, _name), do: {:error, :not_supported}\nend\n\n:ok = Anemos.Runtime.register_data_model(:anemos, Tutorial.Words)\n```\n\nA query engine uses the same behavior. For the `greeter` example:\n\n```elixir\ndefmodule Tutorial.Greeter do\n  @behaviour Anemos.DataModel\n\n  @impl true\n  def keyword, do: \"greeter\"\n\n  @impl true\n  def parse(raw), do: {:ok, String.trim(raw)}\n\n  @impl true\n  def eval(prefix, %{bindings: %{\"name\" => name}}) do\n    {:ok, %{\"line\" => prefix <> \" \" <> name}}\n  end\n\n  @impl true\n  def transduce(_data, _shape, _name), do: {:error, :not_supported}\nend\n\n:ok = Anemos.Runtime.register_data_model(:anemos, Tutorial.Greeter)\n```\n\nThese handlers intentionally accept simple words, not arbitrary embedded\ndocuments. The [data-model example script](examples/data_models.exs) installs\nboth, loads the relevant policies, and checks their answers.","ref":"09-embedding.html#supply-a-complete-data-handler"},{"type":"extras","title":"Keep credentials wrapped - 9. Embedding and extending Anemos","doc":"A host verb returning a credential should return\n`{:ok, Anemos.Secret.new(value, label: \"non-sensitive-name\")}`. Normal\ninspection, interpolation, and logging redact the wrapped value. The\nconsumer deliberately unwraps it with `Anemos.Secret.expose/1` at the point\nwhere the raw credential is needed.\n\nWrapping is opt-in. The runtime cannot infer that an arbitrary string is a\npassword. A label must not contain the credential. The wrapper prevents\naccidental formatting disclosure; it is not encryption or a sandbox against\nhost code that reads the raw term. Custom connectors must consider serialization\ncarefully: serializing the underlying struct can expose its contents.\n\n[Next: Deploying and operating policies](10-operation.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"09-embedding.html#keep-credentials-wrapped"},{"type":"extras","title":"10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"# 10. Deploying and operating policies\n\nA useful policy becomes part of a running system. Operators need to know\nwhether a load succeeded, what an edit replaces, which state survives, and\nwho is allowed to install code. These are application contracts as much as\nlanguage features.","ref":"10-operation.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Give deployed sources stable identities - 10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"```elixir\nsource = File.read!(\"policies/garage.rule\")\nAnemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source, policy: \"garage.rule\")\n```\n\nA named load replaces the previous contents of that policy identity.\nDeclarations removed from the source are removed from the runtime. Unchanged\nrules and conditions keep their state; changed declarations start fresh.\nSource fingerprints ignore line positions, so moving unchanged text down\nthe file does not itself reset the declaration. Changing a declaration's\nactual parsed content can reset it even if the change seems operationally\nminor.\n\nNames are also protected across policies. A rule, condition, or capability\nowned by one named policy cannot be silently taken by another named load.\nAn anonymous load cannot replace a name owned by a named policy either.\nResolve ownership conflicts before deploying.\n\nWithout `policy:`, loading is anonymous: declarations accumulate, and rules\nare replaced by name. Anonymous loads are useful for exploration, but they\ndo not express a complete file replacement or give you a policy ID to unload.\nUse named policies for a managed deployment.","ref":"10-operation.html#give-deployed-sources-stable-identities"},{"type":"extras","title":"Handle all three load outcomes - 10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"```elixir\ncase Anemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source, policy: \"garage.rule\") do\n  :ok ->\n    :ready\n\n  {:partial, failures} ->\n    {:needs_attention, failures}\n\n  {:error, reason} ->\n    {:not_deployed, reason}\nend\n```\n\n`:ok` means the policy installed and its declarations loaded successfully.\n`{:error, reason}` means nothing from this attempted load was installed; an\nexisting deployment remains active. Parse failure, compile failure, rejected\nauthority, excessive source size, and ownership conflicts can produce errors.\n\n`{:partial, failures}` means the policy **did install**, but some declarations\nare inert. Schedules may not have armed or datasets may not have initialized.\nOther rules and capabilities in that source are present. This is not a\nrollback and must not be reported to operators as one. A successful load is\nalso not an exhaustive application validation: unknown command modules and\nquery engines may only be discovered when called.\n\nList or remove deployments with:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.policies(:anemos)\nAnemos.Runtime.unload(:anemos, \"garage.rule\")\n```\n\nUnload removes owned declarations, rule state, global data, subscriptions,\nand schedules no other policy needs. The direct host API is not authority\ngated; restrict who can call it. The runtime's `POLICY::unload` verb requests\nan unload from an already admitted policy; the request is completed after\nthe active dispatch. Its returned acknowledgement is not proof the target\nhas already disappeared.","ref":"10-operation.html#handle-all-three-load-outcomes"},{"type":"extras","title":"Deploy a directory - 10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"```elixir\n{Anemos.Runtime,\n name: :anemos,\n modules: %{\"DEVICE\" => MyApp.Device},\n watch: \"/etc/my_app/policies\"}\n```\n\nThe watcher recursively polls for `*.rule` files. A path relative to that\ndirectory becomes the policy ID. Saving deploys; deleting unloads. A missing\ndirectory is not treated as an empty one, so it does not erase the deployment.\nA file that fails a load leaves its last good version running and is retried\non later scans. A partial load is still a deployed policy with failures to\ninspect.\n\n```elixir\nwatcher = Anemos.Runtime.watcher_name(:anemos)\nAnemos.Runtime.Watcher.status(watcher)\nAnemos.Runtime.Watcher.scan(watcher)\n```\n\nThe default polling interval is 1,000 ms. To configure it or scan manually,\nstart `Anemos.Runtime.Watcher` separately under your supervisor with\n`runtime:`, `dir:`, and `interval_ms:` (`:manual` disables automatic polling).\nRegister required modules and data/feed handlers before dependent policies\nare loaded. A module map can be supplied at runtime startup; custom data/feed\nregistration may require startup sequencing before starting the watcher.","ref":"10-operation.html#deploy-a-directory"},{"type":"extras","title":"Authorize policy loads - 10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"A policy can invoke registered host verbs. Treat loading it as installing\ncode with access to those verbs. The public loading paths, including\n`Anemos.run/2` and the CLI, check a source-size limit and consult the configured\nload authority. The default authority is permissive.\n\nA custom authority implements `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.authorize/1` and\nreceives an `Anemos.Runtime.LoadRequest` with source, byte size, runtime name,\npolicy ID, declared signers, and the caller's `authorization` term. Return\n`:ok` to admit or `{:error, reason}` to refuse. Configure the module:\n\n```elixir\nconfig :anemos, :load_authority, MyApp.PolicyAuthority\n```\n\nPass its credentials explicitly:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source,\n  policy: \"garage.rule\",\n  authorization: %{tenant: \"acme\", token: token})\n```\n\nOne-shot execution presents runtime `:standalone` to the authority. A host\ncan refuse that path independently of named runtimes. The authority boundary\ndoes not isolate hostile host Elixir code already running inside the VM.","ref":"10-operation.html#authorize-policy-loads"},{"type":"extras","title":"Verify signed source - 10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"The bundled signed authority verifies detached Ed25519 signatures. Select it\nin application configuration:\n\n```elixir\nconfig :anemos, :load_authority, Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority.Signed\n```\n\nStart a runtime with public keys and an explicit unsigned policy:\n\n```elixir\n{Anemos.Runtime,\n name: :anemos,\n keyring: %{\"operator\" => operator_public_key},\n unsigned_policy: :strict}\n```\n\n`operator_public_key` is a `Comn.Secrets.Key` containing the public Ed25519\nkey and no private key. The signing system supplies it; Anemos does not\ngenerate or hold signing private keys. `:strict` refuses unsigned sources,\n`:warn` admits and warns, and `:permit` admits without requiring a signer.\nThe default unsigned setting is `:permit`.\n\nDeclare the signer at source level:\n\n```anemos\nsigned_by :operator\n\nrule greet {\n  when HELLO { log \"Hello\" }\n}\n```\n\nThen supply a detached signature over the **exact source bytes**, including\nthe declaration and whitespace:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.load(:anemos, source,\n  policy: \"greeting.rule\",\n  authorization: %{signatures: %{\"operator\" => signature}})\n```\n\nA signature is raw 64-byte Ed25519 data or its Base64 encoding. With one\ndeclared signer, `%{signature: signature}` is also accepted. Every declared\nsigner must verify. Unknown signers, absent signatures, unsupported key types,\nand bad signatures are refused; an unsigned-permit setting does not waive a\ndeclared signer's check. `signed_by` is an identity claim, not a signature\nby itself, and only an enforcing authority gives that claim meaning.\n\nThe watcher reads a Base64 signature from ` .sig`. It supports one\nsignature per source file; sources requiring several signers must use\n`Runtime.load/3`. Replacing the sidecar triggers a new deployment attempt.\n\nRule metadata also accepts `signed_by`; it still means a signer for the\nwhole source. Source-level placement makes that scope clearer and also works\nfor a source containing only conditions or data.","ref":"10-operation.html#verify-signed-source"},{"type":"extras","title":"Understand recovery boundaries - 10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"The runtime owns an in-memory state table that outlives its worker processes.\nSupported dispatcher/scheduler/condition restarts preserve rule `set`\nbindings, condition values and reports, module registrations, deployment\ndefinitions, and timer/firing state held there. The supervision tree rebuilds\ndependent workers from that retained information.\n\nThis is crash recovery inside a running supervisor, not disk persistence.\nStopping the entire runtime or restarting the VM loses in-memory state.\nExternal side effects already performed are not rolled back; replaying an\nevent can perform them again. Keep durable business records in host services,\nand give side-effecting operations application-level idempotency keys when\nreplay is possible.","ref":"10-operation.html#understand-recovery-boundaries"},{"type":"extras","title":"Resource limits and observability - 10. Deploying and operating policies","doc":"| Setting or limit | Default | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `:max_policy_bytes` | 1,048,576 | Bound source before parsing |\n| `:min_schedule_interval_ms` | 1,000 | Reject excessive interval frequency |\n| `:max_schedules` | 1,000 | Bound armed timers per scheduler |\n| Dispatch `timeout:` | 5,000 ms | Bound how long a caller waits |\n| `while` iterations | 10,000 | Bound one loop |\n| Event dispatch depth | 10,000 | Bound handoff/emit recursion |\n| Retained dispatch trace | 200 entries | Recent execution history |\n\nThe first three are application settings under `:anemos`. Set them in\napplication configuration before starting/loading. Dispatch timeouts are call\noptions, for example `Runtime.dispatch(:anemos, \"GO\", %{}, timeout: 10_000)`.\nA caller timeout is not cancellation or rollback of host work. The dispatcher\nis synchronous; slow host verbs delay other work. Put long-running operations\nbehind host services that return an acknowledgement and later emit completion\nevents.\n\nUse `Runtime.describe/1`, `Runtime.explain/3`, and `Runtime.trace/2` as\ndescribed next to distinguish a healthy interpreter from a policy that is\nloaded but unable to do useful work.\n\n[Next: Finding and fixing problems](11-troubleshooting.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"10-operation.html#resource-limits-and-observability"},{"type":"extras","title":"11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"# 11. Finding and fixing problems\n\nWhen a policy does nothing, start by asking which stage had enough information\nto decide. Did the source load? Was there a subscriber? Was it reachable?\nDid a guard suppress evaluation? Did an operation fail? These are different\nproblems and need different fixes.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Diagnose a one-shot run - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"`Anemos.run_report/2` adds reachability and error information to one-shot\nexecution:\n\n```elixir\n{:ok, report} = Anemos.run_report(source,\n  event: \"MOTION\",\n  context_path: \"garage.sensor\",\n  context_data: %{\"maintenance\" => false})\n\nMap.take(report, [:event, :subscribed, :reachable, :errors])\n```\n\n`subscribed` counts matching compiled handlers. `reachable` counts those\nadmitted by the arriving context. `operations` reports work produced;\n`results` and `errors` distinguish successful results from operation failures.\nA load/parse/compile failure returns `{:error, reason}` instead of a report.\n\nInterpret an empty operation list in this order:\n\n1. `subscribed == 0`: compare the exact event name to `when` clauses.\n2. `reachable == 0`: compare the caller's context path to the declared boundary.\n3. Otherwise, inspect guards, branch inputs, and intentionally empty bodies.\n\nA handler containing only `let`/`set`, or an expression call whose answer is\nunused, can execute without producing a visible operation. “No operations”\ndoes not necessarily mean “no code ran.”\n\nThe CLI reports these broad cases and exits `1` for usage, read, load, parse,\ncompile, or operation errors. It exits `0` when dispatch completes without\nreported operation errors, even if nothing subscribed or every branch was\nskipped. An assertion with `held: false` is not an operation error either.\nUse explicit result assertions in a host test when successful behavior\nrequires particular work to have happened.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#diagnose-a-one-shot-run"},{"type":"extras","title":"Ask what the runtime holds - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"```elixir\nsnapshot = Anemos.Runtime.describe(:anemos)\nsnapshot.rules\nsnapshot.conditions\nsnapshot.schedules\n```\n\nThe snapshot reports policies, rules, current persistent bindings and dwell\nstate, conditions, capabilities, datasets, schedules, and module names. It is\nan observation of a live system, not an atomic transaction over all workers.\nBindings are returned as values; wrapped secrets remain wrapped.\n\nTo ask what an event would reach without executing it:\n\n```elixir\nAnemos.Runtime.explain(:anemos, \"MOTION\",\n  %{context_path: \"garage.sensor\"})\n```\n\nRule outcomes include `:fires`, `:context_mismatch`, `:not_true` for a false\ncompound state, and `{:dwell, milliseconds}`. `:fires` means the dispatch\npath is eligible. Explain does not evaluate guards, branches, or host calls,\nso it cannot promise particular side effects.\n\n`Anemos.Runtime.trace(:anemos, 20)` returns recent dispatches newest first.\nEntries contain time, event, context path, rule steps, condition transitions,\nresult kinds, and duration. Handoffs and labeled emits appear in the steps.\nThe trace retains the last 200 dispatches. It summarizes most operation\nvalues; log messages are deliberately retained as log text.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#ask-what-the-runtime-holds"},{"type":"extras","title":"Inspect from a policy - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"```anemos\nrule inspect-system {\n  when DEBUG {\n    list rules\n    list conditions\n    list modules\n    list events\n    list policies\n    list schedules\n    show DEVICE\n    explain DEVICE\n    list DEVICE\n    explain MOTION\n    show ?payload\n    infer ?payload\n    trace ?payload\n    exists ?optional_value\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`show` asks what something is. `explain` asks what it can do or who listens.\n`list` asks for choices. A registered module answers through its description\nor Comn callbacks; an unregistered event name can be inspected for listeners.\nPolicy-level catalogues and listener answers are limited to the asking rule's\nown named policy. Anonymous loads share one inspection scope. Modules remain\nvisible across policies, and fact inspection uses the shared triple store.\nUse the host's `Runtime.describe/1` for the runtime-wide inventory.\nFor a value expression, the operations report its value or inferred kind.\nAnswers are in dispatch results, with a `value` field, rather than necessarily\nprinted to the terminal as prose.\n\n`trace ?payload` is a DSL value-inspection operation. It differs from the\nhost API `Runtime.trace/2`, which retrieves dispatch history. Treat explicit\nvalue inspection as intentional disclosure, just as you treat `log`. Wrap\ncredentials in the host before they enter policy values.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#inspect-from-a-policy"},{"type":"extras","title":"Common symptoms - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"| Symptom | Likely cause and next action |\n| --- | --- |\n| `mix: command not found` | Activate the toolchain in `.tool-versions`; check the terminal's `PATH` |\n| No subscriber | Exact-name mismatch, no loaded policy, or statements placed outside a trigger |\n| Subscriber but no reachable rule | Missing/wrong `context_path`; with map shorthand use outer `context` |\n| `?value` is missing | Wrong payload shape/key, wrong sigil, or absent field; use string payload keys |\n| `@name`/`$name` compile error | No matching `let`/`set` in the declaration; inspect the suggested sigil |\n| Numeric comparison behaves oddly | CLI supplied a string; use typed host data and inspect with `infer` |\n| A boolean guard always runs/skips | `\"false\"` is truthy; compare explicit values or fix producer types |\n| `unknown_module` | Register the module before calling it; a persistent runtime does not inherit one-shot defaults |\n| `unknown_verb` | Inspect module choices and check spelling and registration |\n| `invalid_argument` | Match arity/types/schema; a path argument may have failed confinement |\n| `unknown_dataset` | Dataset name/scope mismatch, failed initialization, or missing handler |\n| `query_missing_binding` | An expected input is absent from `let`, `set`, and string-keyed context data |\n| `unknown_data_model` | Register the query engine under its declared keyword |\n| `query_failed` | Engine parse/evaluation failed or omitted the named return binding |\n| Load says `partial` | Inspect every failure; some declarations installed but will not work |\n| Timer armed but scoped body never runs | Built-in scheduled events have no context; use a scoped host event |\n| Demand failed but later work happened | Current demand dispatch does not gate later operations |\n| Fact query yields a map instead of rows | Value-position triple query is a descriptor in this version |\n| Caller times out | Slow synchronous verb or excessive work; a timeout does not cancel it |\n\nStructured errors are `Comn.Errors.ErrorStruct` values. Their stable `code`\nidentifies the category; `field` often names the declaration or argument.\nThe message explains the immediate problem. Request/trace IDs support\noperator correlation. For readable author-facing output, use\n`Mix.Tasks.Anemos.Run.describe(error)` rather than dumping the full struct.\nDo not copy arbitrary raw host errors into user messages if they can contain\ncredentials or sensitive payloads.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#common-symptoms"},{"type":"extras","title":"Recipe: ignore incomplete messages - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"```anemos\nrule process-complete-reading {\n  when READING {\n    if not exists ?temperature { return }\n    if ?enabled != true { return }\n    log ?temperature\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis explicitly requires a present temperature and a boolean true flag.\nIt does not validate the temperature's numeric type; put that validation in\nthe event ingress or a host schema/verb if arithmetic follows.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#recipe-ignore-incomplete-messages"},{"type":"extras","title":"Recipe: one event updates state, another consumes it - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"```anemos\nrule remember-mode {\n  when MODE_CHANGED {\n    set mode = ?mode\n  }\n  when REQUEST {\n    if $mode == \"active\" {\n      log \"Processing is enabled\"\n    } else {\n      log \"Processing is disabled\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBefore the first mode event, `$mode` is `nil`, so the disabled branch runs.\nThe state is shared by both handlers because they belong to one named rule.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#recipe-one-event-updates-state-another-consumes-it"},{"type":"extras","title":"Recipe: two-stage processing with explicit payload - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"```anemos\nrule receive-job {\n  when SUBMITTED {\n    if not exists ?job_id { return }\n    nextevent VALIDATED with { job_id: ?job_id, checked: true }\n  }\n}\n\nrule record-job {\n  when VALIDATED {\n    if ?checked == true {\n      log ?job_id\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe second rule receives the overlay and any remaining original fields.\nFor a real trust boundary, do not treat an arbitrary `checked` payload field\nas proof of authorization: the host controls which producers can send each\nevent and context. This recipe demonstrates data handoff, not authentication.","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#recipe-two-stage-processing-with-explicit-payload"},{"type":"extras","title":"Test policies as programs - 11. Finding and fixing problems","doc":"Start an isolated runtime, load named source, dispatch typed events, and\nassert the results that matter. Check operation errors even when the outer\ndispatch tuple is `{:ok, ...}`. For stateful logic, test a sequence including\nreset/false reports rather than one happy event. For timers, observe delivery\nwith bounded waits instead of assuming millisecond-exact scheduling.\n\nRun the manual's checks from the repository root:\n\n```sh\nmix test test/anemos/manual_test.exs\nmix run docs/manual/examples/walkthrough.exs\nmix run docs/manual/examples/data_models.exs\nmix run docs/manual/examples/workshop.exs\n```\n\nUse `mix check` for the repository's formatting, strict Credo, and full test\nsuite. `mix docs` builds the manual alongside module API documentation.\n\n[Next: A complete workshop application](12-workshop.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"11-troubleshooting.html#test-policies-as-programs"},{"type":"extras","title":"12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts","doc":"# 12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts\n\nWe have learned the pieces of Anemos separately. Now we will combine them in\none application: a workshop temperature monitor with a sensor allow-list,\na mute switch, a count of alerts, and a host service that receives alerts.\nThe example runs entirely in memory so you can inspect every decision.\n\nThe downloadable files are [workshop.rule](examples/workshop.rule) and\n[workshop.exs](examples/workshop.exs). From the repository root:\n\n```sh\nmix run docs/manual/examples/workshop.exs\n```\n\nThe script checks the scenario described below and prints\n`Workshop scenario passed.` after every expected result has been observed.","ref":"12-workshop.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Write the input and output contracts first - 12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts","doc":"The host promises to deliver these events:\n\n| Event | Payload | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `TEMPERATURE` | `sensor`: string; `celsius`: number | A sampled reading |\n| `MUTE` | Empty map | Suppress temperature handling |\n| `UNMUTE` | Empty map | Resume temperature handling |\n| `REPORT` | Empty map | Report the number of alerts sent |\n\nThey arrive under `workshop.bench` or a descendant path. The accepted sensors\nare `bench_a` and `bench_b`. An accepted reading at least 30 degrees requests\nan alert unless muted. An unknown sensor logs a diagnostic. A missing\ntemperature logs a diagnostic and stops that handler.\n\nThe host supplies `ALERT::send(sensor, celsius)`. Its schema requires a string\nand a number; it returns an acknowledgement map. The example verb appends\nrequests to an in-memory list. A deployed implementation could instead submit\nrequests to a service with its own delivery guarantees.\n\nThese contracts prevent a policy from guessing units, payload types, or what\n“send” means. The host must also authenticate sensor identities and paths at\ningress. Membership in this sample dataset classifies a supplied sensor name;\nit does not establish who produced a message.","ref":"12-workshop.html#write-the-input-and-output-contracts-first"},{"type":"extras","title":"The policy - 12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts","doc":"```anemos\ndata accepted_sensors {\n  bench_a\n  bench_b\n}\n\nrule workshop-alerts {\n  context workshop.bench\n\n  when MUTE {\n    set muted = true\n  }\n\n  when UNMUTE {\n    set muted = false\n  }\n\n  when TEMPERATURE unless $muted {\n    if not exists ?celsius {\n      log \"Missing temperature\"\n      return\n    }\n    if ?sensor memberof(accepted_sensors) {\n      if ?celsius >= 30 {\n        let receipt = [ALERT::send ?sensor ?celsius]\n        set sent = ($sent OR 0) + 1\n        log @receipt\n      }\n    } else {\n      log \"Unknown sensor\"\n    }\n  }\n\n  when REPORT {\n    log $sent OR 0\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`muted` and `sent` belong to the named rule and are shared by its handlers.\nBefore initialization they are `nil`. A missing mute flag lets the guard\npass. A missing sent count falls back to zero. Zero itself is truthy, so\nsubsequent `$sent OR 0` expressions preserve it correctly.\n\nThe alert verb is deliberately in value position. Its acknowledgement must\nexist before the count increments. If the verb returns an error, evaluation\nstops before the `set`, and the failed request is not counted. This does not\ncreate a transaction with an external service: work may happen even if the\nacknowledgement is lost. Real delivery needs an application request ID and\nidempotency policy when retries are possible.\n\nAn accepted reading below 30 produces no operation. The rule still ran and\ntested the reading. An empty result alone does not mean dispatch failed.","ref":"12-workshop.html#the-policy"},{"type":"extras","title":"The host service - 12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts","doc":"The script starts an Agent to hold requests and registers this module:\n\n```elixir\ndefmodule Tutorial.Alerts do\n  use Anemos.Module, name: \"ALERT\"\n\n  @impl Anemos.Runtime.Module\n  def arg_schema(\"send\"), do: [[type: :string], [type: :number]]\n  def arg_schema(_verb), do: nil\n\n  verb :send, fn [sensor, celsius], _context ->\n    receipt = %{sensor: sensor, celsius: celsius}\n    Agent.update(Tutorial.AlertSink, &(&1 ++ [receipt]))\n    {:ok, receipt}\n  end\nend\n```\n\n`Tutorial.AlertSink` is a host process name. The interpreter knows only the\nDSL name `ALERT`. The script supplies that registration at runtime startup,\nloads the source as named policy `workshop.rule`, and checks the load returned\n`:ok` before sending events.","ref":"12-workshop.html#the-host-service"},{"type":"extras","title":"Follow a sequence - 12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts","doc":"The scenario drives one persistent runtime through these observations:\n\n| Input | Expected observation |\n| --- | --- |\n| `REPORT` | Log `0` |\n| Accepted sensor at 22 | No alert |\n| Accepted sensor at 31 | First alert; acknowledgement logged |\n| Unknown sensor at 35 | Diagnostic; no additional alert |\n| `MUTE`, then accepted sensor at 40 | Suppressed; no additional alert |\n| `UNMUTE`, then accepted sensor at 32 | Second alert |\n| `REPORT` | Log `2` |\n| Accepted sensor at 50 from `garden.bench` | Context denied; no additional alert |\n\nThe host asserts both dispatch results and the sink's stored values. Checking\nonly log text would miss an accidentally sent alert. Checking only the sink\nwould miss incorrect count state or diagnostics.\n\nThe script also checks replacement: reloading identical named source retains\nthe count; changing the rule resets it. It then unloads `workshop.rule` and\nchecks that subsequent reports produce nothing. Unload removes declarations\nand interpreter state, but does not erase the host's previously stored alerts.","ref":"12-workshop.html#follow-a-sequence"},{"type":"extras","title":"Change requirements deliberately - 12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts","doc":"Suppose the workshop adds a second room. If both rooms reach this rule, they\nshare a mute switch and count. For independent room behavior, deploy separate\nrule identities with separate contexts, or let a host service own keyed room\nstate. Adding a payload field alone does not partition `$` bindings.\n\nSuppose an alert should occur only when temperature enters the hot state.\nRepeated hot readings currently generate repeated alerts by design. Use a\ncondition with a threshold predicate for rising-edge behavior, and define the\nreports that reset it. If several sensors feed one condition, they share its\nstate; decide whether each sensor needs an independent condition identity.\n\nSuppose the threshold should be configurable. Add a numeric host payload\nfield and a documented default merged at ingress, or provide a settings query.\nThe producer contract is the right place to make units and types explicit.\n\nFinally, suppose the count must survive VM restarts. Move it into a durable\nhost service and include it in acknowledgements. `set` is interpreter state\nwithin a running runtime; records with retention requirements need durable\nownership.","ref":"12-workshop.html#change-requirements-deliberately"},{"type":"extras","title":"Exercises - 12. A complete application: workshop temperature alerts","doc":"1. Add an event that clears the count without changing mute state.\n2. Add `bench_c` to the dataset and send a hot reading from it.\n3. Supply string `\"35\"` as the temperature. What protects the host verb?\n4. Reload the identical named policy and send `REPORT`. Then change its\n   diagnostic text and reload. Which count survives?\n\nAnswers: (1) Add `when CLEAR_COUNT { set sent = 0 }` to the same rule.\n(2) Membership admits it after deployment; context must still match.\n(3) The numeric schema refuses the string. A mixed-type threshold comparison\nis not sufficient validation; the failed value call aborts before incrementing.\n(4) An unchanged rule keeps the count; a changed rule starts fresh and reports\nzero through the fallback.\n\n[Next: Language and API reference](reference.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"12-workshop.html#exercises"},{"type":"extras","title":"Language and API reference","doc":"# Language and API reference\n\nThis reference summarizes Anemos 0.1.0 as implemented. Read the chapters for\nmotivation, worked examples, and behavioral qualifications. Templates below\nuse angle brackets for placeholders and square-bracketed English descriptions\nfor optional material; those markers are not literal DSL syntax.","ref":"reference.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Source structure - Language and API reference","doc":"A source contains zero or more declarations. Supported top-level declarations\nare `rule`, `condition`, `capability`, `data`, `query`, and `signed_by`.\nStatements do not run at source level. Declaration names should be lowercase\nidentifiers. Use one context boundary per rule/condition.\n\n```text\nsigned_by : \n\nrule   {\n  [version \" \"]\n  [signed_by : ]\n  [scope local | global | mesh]\n  [context  ]\n   \n}\n\ncondition   {\n  [context  ]\n   \n  [if   {}]\n}\n\ncapability   {\n  [context  ]\n  on demand {   }\n}\n```\n\n`version` and rule `scope` are metadata. They do not enforce package version\nselection, distribute rules across a mesh, or replace context access checks.\n`signed_by` identifies source signers to the configured load authority; it\ndoes not contain a signature. Metadata belongs before a rule's body statements.\n\nBraced contexts and bare dotted contexts are supported:\n\n```text\ncontext \"building.garage\" {   }\ncontext building.garage\n```\n\nA quoted context currently expects a braced body. Use the bare dotted form\nfor a context line without a body.","ref":"reference.html#source-structure"},{"type":"extras","title":"Lexical conventions - Language and API reference","doc":"| Construct | Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| Comment to end of line | `; comment` or `# comment` |\n| String | `\"text\"`; escapes `\\\"`, `\\\\`, `\\n`, `\\t`. Or `~s(text)` with any of `() [] {} <> // \\|\\| \"\"`, no escapes |\n| Integer / float | `42`, `3.5`; negative through subtraction or host data |\n| Size | `10MB`; units `B KB MB GB TB`, binary multiples; an integer at runtime |\n| Regex | `~r/pattern/flags`, same delimiters as `~s`; compiled at load |\n| Boolean | `true`, `false` |\n| Symbol | `:name`, `:dotted.name`; runtime string value |\n| Identifier | Lowercase initial; letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.` afterward |\n| Module / event name | `DEVICE`, `FILE_CREATED`, `CONDITION_ready` |\n| Variable | `?name`, `@name`, `$name`; letters/digits/underscores |\n| Command qualification | `MODULE::verb` |\n| Block | `{ ... }` |\n| Command / triple | `[ ... ]` |\n\nNewlines and spaces do not delimit statements by themselves; keywords and\nexpression boundaries do. Semicolons start comments. For readability, put\none statement per line and spaces around infix operators. Only lowercase\nbooleans are literals. There is no `nil` literal, single-quoted string form,\ngeneral map literal, or general square-bracket list literal.\n\nRecognized keyword tokens are:\n\n```text\nrule when if at on it let set emit demand condition capability flow context\nelse elseif while for each every foreach do return done nextevent nextrule\nlog show list graph trace explain exists infer assert unless until as with\nby that import export version signed_by scope state effect stream to channel\nservice systemd self interval then filter map reduce data query source expect\ntransclude dynamic subscribe memberof global\n```\n\nRecognition as a keyword does not mean a construct is implemented. See feature\nstatus below, particularly `flow`, `import`, `export`, `it`, and destinations.","ref":"reference.html#lexical-conventions"},{"type":"extras","title":"Bindings and lookup - Language and API reference","doc":"| Write | Read | Scope/lifetime |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Host payload/context field | `?name` | Current dispatch's `context_data` |\n| `let name = expression` | `@name` | Current handler evaluation |\n| `set name = expression` | `$name` | Named rule across firings |\n| `for name in collection` | `@name` | Current iteration; removed afterward |\n| Pipeline `filter ?name` / `map ?name` | `?name` | Current pipeline element |\n| Pipeline `reduce acc initial ?name` | `$acc`, `?name` | Reduction accumulator and element |\n\n`?name` resolution: context data, then a visible named query, then a visible\ndataset, then `nil`. Local data/query scope is the named rule, and local\ndefinitions shadow global definitions. Query expected-input resolution:\n`let`, then `set`, then string-keyed context data. A missing expected input\nis an error.\n\nOnly boolean `false` and `nil` are falsey. `exists expression` is true for\nany non-`nil` value, including false. The compiler catches references to an\n`@`/`$` name with no possible binder anywhere in the declaration; it does\nnot prove execution-path initialization.","ref":"reference.html#bindings-and-lookup"},{"type":"extras","title":"Triggers - Language and API reference","doc":"```text\nwhen   [for  ] [unless   | until  ] {\n   \n}\nwhen (  AND   ...) {   }\nwhen (  OR   ...) {   }\n\nat   [unless   | until  ] {   }\n```\n\nUse homogeneous compound event groups. Compound reports start false and are\nreplaced by incoming reports. `state: false`/`nil` makes a report false;\nabsent `state` makes it true. A compound rule can fire repeatedly while the\ncombination remains true; a condition acts only on a false-to-true transition.\n\nSupported time specifications:\n\n| Form | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `13:00`, `TIME_1300` | Next occurrence, once |\n| `interval(5min)` | Repeating elapsed interval |\n| `09:00 every day` | Daily |\n| `09:00 every weekday` | Monday–Friday |\n| `09:00 every monday` | Weekly, named day |\n| `DATE_2099.04.15` | One calendar date |\n| `DAY_friday` | Next named day, once |\n| `NLD_tomorrow` | Resolved relative date, once |\n\nCompact duration suffixes: `ms`, `s`, `min`, `h`, `d`. Use nonnegative whole\namounts. Zero dwell is immediate; intervals must meet the configured minimum.\nNLD expressions: `today`, `tomorrow`, `yesterday`, `next. `, `next.week`,\n`next.month`, `next.year`, `end.of.month`, `end.of.week`.\nSee [time semantics and limitations](07-time.md), including local time,\npast-date refusal, captured dwell context, and unscoped scheduler events.","ref":"reference.html#triggers"},{"type":"extras","title":"Handler statements - Language and API reference","doc":"```text\nlet   =  \nset   =  \nlog  \n[MODULE::verb  ]\n\nif   [unless  ] {   }\n  elseif   {   }\n  else {   }\n\nwhile   {   }\nfor   in   {   }\neach   in   {   }\nevery   in   {   }\nforeach MODULE::verb {   }\n\nreturn\ndone\nnextevent   [with { key:  , other:   }]\nnextrule   [with { key:  , other:   }]\n\ndemand   [--requisite | --required | --optional | --sufficient]\nemit [state | effect | stream] [MODULE::verb  ]\n  [to channel( )] [as : ]\n\nshow  \nexplain  \nlist  \ngraph  \ntrace  \ninfer  \nexists  \nassert  \n\nlet [:subject -> :predicate -> :object]\n```\n\n`else if` is an alternative to `elseif`. Commands accept whitespace-separated\narguments and optional commas/parenthesized argument syntax. For computed\narguments, bind intermediate expressions with `let` for clarity. For example:\n\n```anemos\nrule calculated-command {\n  when NUMBER {\n    let next_value = ?value + 1\n    [MATH::double @next_value]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`return`/`done` stop this handler, not all event listeners. `return value`\ndoes not provide a return value in the current evaluator. `nextevent`\nbroadcasts by event; `nextrule` selects by named rule. Both preserve context,\noverlay `with` data on the original payload, and enforce receiving context.\n\nInspection subjects may be module/event names, expressions, or catalogue\nnames `rules`, `conditions`, `modules`, `events`, `policies`, `schedules`.\n`assert` requires an expression; use `assert exists DEVICE` to test a module\nregistration rather than asserting a bare name.","ref":"reference.html#handler-statements"},{"type":"extras","title":"Expressions and pipelines - Language and API reference","doc":"```text\n \n[MODULE::verb  ]\n( )\n(    ...)\n   \n     \n  memberof( ,  , ...)\n\n  |> filter ?item ( )\n  |> map ?item ( )\n  |> reduce accumulator   ?item ( )\n  |> [MODULE::verb  ]\n```\n\nThe final form prepends the collection as one command argument and returns\nthe host command's value. Pipeline collection functions wrap a scalar as one\nelement and treat `nil` as empty. A bare pipeline is an expression, not a\nstandalone handler statement: bind it or use it as another statement's value.","ref":"reference.html#expressions-and-pipelines"},{"type":"extras","title":"Operator precedence - Language and API reference","doc":"Lowest binding power first. Binary operators associate to the left. Unary\noperators bind above binary operators, and grouping parentheses override\nprecedence.\n\n| Level | Operators |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0 | `\\|>` |\n| 1 | `in`, `notin`, `contains`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`, `matches_glob`, `matches_regex`, `memberof` |\n| 2 | `OR`, `or`, `\\|\\|` |\n| 3 | `AND`, `and`, `&&` |\n| 4 | Bitwise `\\|` |\n| 5 | Bitwise `^` |\n| 6 | Bitwise `&` |\n| 7 | `==`, `!=`, `===`, `!==`, `eq`, `ne` |\n| 8 | `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `lt`, `le`, `gt`, `ge` |\n| 9 | `<<`, `>>` |\n| 10 | `+`, `-` |\n| 11 | `*`, `/`, `%` |\n| Above binary | Unary `not`, `!`, `~` |\n\nUppercase `IN` is accepted in expression membership position as well. Other\nword comparisons use the lowercase spellings listed. `matches_glob` is in the\nprecedence table but not implemented at evaluation time.\n\n`AND` compounds non-boolean values, dropping `true` and absorbing the first\nfalsey operand. `OR`/`||` chooses the first truthy value, or the first operand\nwhen all are falsey. `&&` returns the falsey left value or the right value.\nAll are eager, including host calls in operands. See [Chapter 3](03-values-and-expressions.md#logical-operators-also-carry-values)\nfor shape examples and mixed-value rules.","ref":"reference.html#operator-precedence"},{"type":"extras","title":"Data and queries - Language and API reference","doc":"```text\ndata   {\n   \n}\n\ndata   {\n  [source  ]\n    {   }\n  [transclude to shape( ) with   as  ]\n}\n\ndata   {\n  dynamic { subscribe   \" \" }\n}\n\nquery   {\n  [expect :input, :other_input]\n    {   }\n  return ?output\n}\n```\n\nTop-level data/query declarations are global. Inline declarations are local\nto the named rule unless written `data global  ` or `query global  `.\nOnly simple sets work without a host data handler. Format/engine bodies are\ntoken-reconstructed text, not arbitrary raw source. `source` is metadata;\ndynamic protocol handlers implement fetching, subscription and any polling.\nQuery access through `?name` executes the query;\nit does not cache it.\n\nTriple assertions work as facts; query pattern evaluation is partial. See\n[Chapter 8](08-data.md#facts-and-triple-patterns) before using patterns as values.","ref":"reference.html#data-and-queries"},{"type":"extras","title":"Command-line reference - Language and API reference","doc":"```sh\nmix anemos.run path/to/policy.rule --event EVENT_NAME [options]\n```\n\n| Option | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--event NAME`, `-e NAME` | Required event to dispatch |\n| `--context PATH`, `-c PATH` | Caller path for listener boundaries |\n| `--context-data KEY=VALUE` | Repeatable string-valued payload fields |\n| `--stub-modules` | Opt into bundled demonstration module doubles |\n| `--help`, `-h` | Usage |\n\nThe bundled example doubles cover `EVENT`, example `FILE`, `INFRA`, `PDF`,\n`SECRET`, and `STRING`. They do no external work. The one-shot path normally\nregisters only development `DEVICE` and `FILE` stubs. A persistent runtime\nstarts with `POLICY` and host registrations, not the one-shot stub defaults.\n\nThe CLI dispatches once and exits. Exit `1` covers usage/read/load/parse/\ncompile/operation errors. Exit `0` means no reported operation errors, not\nthat a required action occurred. Context-data parsing splits only the first\n`=`; a key without `=` receives an empty string.","ref":"reference.html#command-line-reference"},{"type":"extras","title":"Host API quick reference - Language and API reference","doc":"`rt` below is the host-controlled runtime atom, usually `:anemos`.\n\n| Call | Result / purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Anemos.run(source, event: name, ...)` | `{:ok, results}` or `{:error, reason}`; one-shot |\n| `Anemos.run_report(source, event: name, ...)` | Detailed one-shot report or load error |\n| `Runtime.start_link(name: rt, ...)` | Start supervised runtime |\n| `Runtime.register(rt, module)` | Register a macro-defined module by its embedded name |\n| `Runtime.register(rt, \"NAME\", module)` | Register explicit command name |\n| `Runtime.register_data_model(rt, handler)` | Register format/query handler by `keyword/0` |\n| `Runtime.put_context(rt, \"key\", value)` | Write context store; currently not merged into dispatch lookup |\n| `Runtime.connect(rt, connector)` | Replace current channel connector |\n| `Runtime.load(rt, source, policy: id, ...)` | `:ok`, `{:partial, failures}`, or `{:error, reason}` |\n| `Runtime.unload(rt, id)` | Remove a named policy |\n| `Runtime.policies(rt)` | Loaded policy ownership information |\n| `Runtime.dispatch(rt, name, context, opts)` | `{:ok, results}`; inspect individual errors |\n| `Runtime.dispatch(rt, event_struct_or_map)` | Dispatch through `Anemos.Event` |\n| `Runtime.condition_state(rt, \"name\")` | `true`, `false`, or absent `nil` |\n| `Runtime.describe(rt)` | Runtime inventory, state, and schedules |\n| `Runtime.explain(rt, name, context)` | Reachability without guard/body evaluation |\n| `Runtime.trace(rt, limit)` | Newest-first recent dispatch history |\n| `Runtime.Watcher.status(watcher)` | Last deployment status by file |\n| `Runtime.Watcher.scan(watcher)` | Force one scan |\n\n`Runtime` abbreviates `Anemos.Runtime`. All calls are Elixir, not DSL syntax.\nPublic API module documentation gives specs and additional options.\n\nOne-shot options: `event:`, `context:`, `context_path:`, `context_data:`,\n`modules:`, `authorization:`. Runtime startup options: `name:`, `modules:`,\n`watch:`, `keyring:`, `unsigned_policy:`. Load options: `policy:`,\n`authorization:`. Dispatch accepts `timeout:` in its fourth argument.\n\nString dispatch context uses `%{context_path: path, context_data: payload}`.\nMap-protocol events use `%{name: name, context: path, payload: payload}`.\nUse string keys within payloads.","ref":"reference.html#host-api-quick-reference"},{"type":"extras","title":"Extension contracts - Language and API reference","doc":"| Interface | Required operations | Optional / notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `Anemos.Runtime.Module` | `handle_verb/3` | `arg_schema/1`, `describe/1`; macro `use Anemos.Module` |\n| `Anemos.Event` | `name/1`, `context/1`, `payload/1` | Protocol for application event values |\n| `Anemos.Channel` | `publish/2` | Receives envelope; host owns delivery guarantees |\n| `Anemos.DataModel` | `keyword/0`, `parse/1`, `eval/2`, `transduce/3` | Return `{:error, :not_supported}` for unsupported transformations |\n| `Anemos.Subscribable` | `subscribe/3`, `unsubscribe/1` | Adapter owns actual feed/polling |\n| `Anemos.Runtime.LoadAuthority` | `authorize/1` | Receives `LoadRequest`; default permissive |","ref":"reference.html#extension-contracts"},{"type":"extras","title":"Feature status - Language and API reference","doc":"These distinctions are part of the documentation contract, not implied\npromises that unfinished syntax will acquire a particular future meaning.\n\n| Feature | Current behavior | Practical guidance |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rules, context boundaries | Implemented; one extracted context per declaration | Use one explicit scope |\n| `let`, `set`, expressions, branches | Implemented | Initialize persistent state inside a handler |\n| `put_context/3` | Stores values separately; ordinary dispatch does not resolve them | Merge defaults into event `context_data` in the host |\n| Command statements / values | Implemented with different execution timing | Read Chapter 2 before depending on side-effect order |\n| Explicit collection iteration / pipelines | Implemented | Supply lists; use explicit loop sources |\n| Bare `each item` / `every item` | Parses but has no iterable and does no work | Use `each item in ?items` or `for` |\n| Pipeline `emit` | Descriptors discarded; no reliable per-item dispatch | Use a `for` with command statements |\n| `until` | Latch can be lost; ignored on `if`; fresh guard on conditions | Use explicit persistent state with `unless` |\n| Conditions | Trigger/predicate state and rising-edge actions implemented | One transition body per condition; no condition dwell |\n| Capability demand | Each demand is its own stack; later statements continue | Do not use as an authorization gate |\n| Capability body operations | Returned as results, not recursively executed | Expression host calls execute; do not infer queued execution |\n| `return value` | Parses; value ignored | Use bare return or explicit handoff |\n| `nextevent` | Broadcasts to all eligible event subscribers | Choose event names accordingly |\n| `nextrule` | Calls every compiled handler with target name | Prefer one-action target rules |\n| `at` in scoped rules | Timer events have no context, so boundary denies | Schedule scoped events in host |\n| `at` persistent bindings | Generated time handlers have separate names/state | Use host-scheduled ordinary events to share `when` state |\n| Local calendar schedules | Implemented with local naive time | Externalize zone/DST/durable scheduling requirements |\n| Simple datasets / `memberof` | Exact membership implemented | CIDRs are strings, not subnet matchers |\n| Format data / queries / transclusion | Host handlers required | Register before loading; inspect failures |\n| Embedded engine text | Reconstructed tokens, not original bytes | Keep handler grammar compatible |\n| Dynamic feeds | Subscription/update/cancellation implemented | Host supplies adapters and polling |\n| Triple assertions / `graph` | Implemented | Shared runtime fact store |\n| Triple queries in expressions | Descriptor values, not bound rows | Use host matching/query services |\n| `matches_glob` | Parsed; evaluator falls back to false | Use `matches_regex` or a host matcher |\n| `++`, `~&`, `~\\|` | Tokenized, not working binary expression operators | Use implemented operators or host verbs |\n| Unary minus | Not parsed | Use subtraction, such as `0 - 5` |\n| Interpolated symbols / labels | Parsed text is not dynamically resolved | Use fixed labels and explicit payloads |\n| `to service(...)`, `to systemd(...)`, `to self` | Carried on the emit operation for the host to route; no delivery inside Anemos | Use `to channel(...)` for in-runtime delivery |\n| General `emit value` | Not implemented; command form required | Emit a host command's result |\n| Rule `version`, `scope` | Descriptive metadata | Do not infer version enforcement/distribution |\n| `signed_by` | Enforced only through an appropriate load authority | Configure signed verification explicitly |\n| `flow`, `import`, `export`, modal `it` | Reserved/design vocabulary, no working feature | Compose implemented rules and host services |\n\n[Glossary and index](glossary.md) · [Contents](README.md)","ref":"reference.html#feature-status"},{"type":"extras","title":"Glossary and subject index","doc":"# Glossary and subject index","ref":"glossary.html"},{"type":"extras","title":"Glossary - Glossary and subject index","doc":"**Binding.** A name associated with a value. A `let` creates an `@` binding;\na `set` creates a `$` binding. See [values](03-values-and-expressions.md#binding-and-reading).\n\n**Capability.** A passive named `on demand` body. Its context restricts which\ndeclaration may demand it. Current dispatch has important stack/execution\nlimits. See [capabilities](06-conditions-and-capabilities.md#define-work-to-perform-on-demand).\n\n**Channel.** A named output destination handled by the current host connector.\nIt is distinct from an internal event subscription. See [channels](09-embedding.md#emit-values-and-connect-a-channel).\n\n**Command.** A bracketed module/verb invocation. In value position it executes\nduring evaluation; in statement position it becomes a queued operation.\nSee [operation order](02-execution-model.md#bodies-evaluate-then-operations-execute).\n\n**Compound.** The collection formed by value-level `AND`, preserving operand\nboundaries. See [compounding](03-values-and-expressions.md#logical-operators-also-carry-values).\n\n**Compound trigger.** A remembered-state `AND` or `OR` over event reports.\nIt differs from value-level compounding. See [reports](06-conditions-and-capabilities.md#combine-reports-in-a-rule).\n\n**Condition.** A named boolean state reevaluated on subscribed events. Its\nfalse-to-true transition generates `CONDITION_ ` and evaluates transition\nactions. See [conditions](06-conditions-and-capabilities.md#name-a-continuing-condition).\n\n**Context data.** Values available to `?` lookup from the current dispatch's\npayload map. The host can merge defaults into that map. See [event input](09-embedding.md#supply-event-data).\n\n**Context path.** A dotted location used for listener and capability boundary\nchecks. Whole segments determine containment. See [boundaries](02-execution-model.md#a-context-belongs-to-a-listener).\n\n**Dataset.** Named reference data used for exact membership or read through\n`?name`. Simple datasets are sets. See [datasets](08-data.md#a-named-dataset).\n\n**Declaration.** A top-level named language definition, such as a rule or\nquery. See [source structure](reference.md#source-structure).\n\n**Dispatch.** Delivering an event through subscription selection, context\nfiltering, handler evaluation, and operation execution. See [execution model](02-execution-model.md).\n\n**Dwell.** A duration for which a reported trigger state must remain true\nbefore a handler runs. See [dwell timers](07-time.md#wait-for-a-reported-state-to-persist).\n\n**Envelope.** A published value plus type, source, time, label, and channel.\nSee [emission](09-embedding.md#emit-values-and-connect-a-channel).\n\n**Event.** An exact name, optional context path, and payload supplied by a\nproducer. A name such as `SENSOR::motion` is still an event until bracketed as\na command. See [names](02-execution-model.md#the-four-kinds-of-name).\n\n**Falsey.** Boolean `false` or host `nil`. All other values, including zero\nand empty collections, are truthy. See [truth](03-values-and-expressions.md#truth-and-comparison).\n\n**Guard.** A trigger/block modifier such as `unless`, evaluated before its\nbody. See [guards](04-control-flow.md#skip-a-trigger-with-unless).\n\n**Handler.** The executable body selected for a trigger. Several handlers may\nbelong to one named rule and share its persistent binding state.\n\n**Host.** The Elixir application embedding Anemos, supplying command modules,\nevent producers, adapters, and operational guarantees. See [embedding](09-embedding.md).\n\n**Load authority.** Host policy that admits/refuses source before compilation\nand installation. See [authorization](10-operation.md#authorize-policy-loads).\n\n**Module.** A registered collection of host verbs, addressed by a name such as\n`MATH`. It is not defined in policy source. See [extension](09-embedding.md#define-a-real-command-contract).\n\n**Operation.** A descriptor produced during body evaluation for the dispatcher\nto execute or report. Not every evaluated expression produces an operation.\n\n**Partial load.** A source installed with some inert declarations, reported as\n`{:partial, failures}`. See [load outcomes](10-operation.md#handle-all-three-load-outcomes).\n\n**Payload.** The data accompanying an event; normally a map with string keys.\n\n**Persistent binding.** A `$` value retained across firings of one named rule,\nwithin the runtime's lifetime. See [state](02-execution-model.md#state-has-a-lifetime-and-an-owner).\n\n**Pipeline.** An expression passing a collection/value through `|>` stages.\nSee [pipelines](05-collections.md#filter-map-reduce).\n\n**Policy.** A source unit containing declarations. Named deployments give it\nan ID used for replacement and unload. See [deployment](10-operation.md).\n\n**Query.** A named host-engine computation invoked through `?name`, with\nexpected inputs and a named output. See [queries](08-data.md#queries-name-a-computation).\n\n**Report.** An event's last true/false state remembered by a compound listener.\nIt remains until replaced by a new report of the same event.\n\n**Rule.** A named collection of event/time handlers, context, state, and\noptional local data/query declarations. See [first policy](01-first-policy.md).\n\n**Runtime.** A supervised interpreter with installed policies, registered host\nmodules, in-memory state, dispatcher, and scheduler.\n\n**Sigil.** A prefix (`?`, `@`, `$`) selecting how a value name is read.\n\n**Transclusion.** A data declaration's request to derive another representation;\nthe handler performs it with `transduce/3`. See [structured data](08-data.md#structured-data-belongs-to-a-handler).\n\n**Triple.** A subject–predicate–object fact stored in the runtime's shared\ntriple store. See [facts](08-data.md#facts-and-triple-patterns).\n\n**Verb.** An operation name implemented by a host module, such as `double` in\n`MATH::double`.","ref":"glossary.html#glossary"},{"type":"extras","title":"Subject index - Glossary and subject index","doc":"| Find | Read |\n| --- | --- |\n| `?`, `@`, `$` | [Binding rules](03-values-and-expressions.md#binding-and-reading) |\n| `AND`, `OR`, `&&`, `\\|\\|` | [Value algebra](03-values-and-expressions.md#logical-operators-also-carry-values), [event algebra](06-conditions-and-capabilities.md#combine-reports-in-a-rule) |\n| Argument validation and filesystem roots | [Module boundary](09-embedding.md#validate-inputs-at-the-module-boundary) |\n| Application walkthrough | [Workshop alerts](12-workshop.md) |\n| Assertions and early exit | [Stop a handler](04-control-flow.md#stop-a-handler-early) |\n| Boolean/string mismatches | [Truth and comparison](03-values-and-expressions.md#truth-and-comparison) |\n| CLI flags and exit codes | [CLI reference](reference.md#command-line-reference) |\n| Context denial | [Boundaries](02-execution-model.md#a-context-belongs-to-a-listener), [diagnosis](11-troubleshooting.md#common-symptoms) |\n| Counter initialization | [State ownership](02-execution-model.md#state-has-a-lifetime-and-an-owner) |\n| Data handler implementation | [Complete handlers](09-embedding.md#supply-a-complete-data-handler) |\n| Demand flags | [Integration limits](06-conditions-and-capabilities.md#demand-flags-and-the-current-integration-limit) |\n| Emit labels and connectors | [Emission](09-embedding.md#emit-values-and-connect-a-channel) |\n| Empty results | [One-shot diagnosis](11-troubleshooting.md#diagnose-a-one-shot-run) |\n| Event protocol and map shapes | [Event input](09-embedding.md#supply-event-data) |\n| Examples to run | [First policy](01-first-policy.md), [scripts](README.md#conventions-and-example-files) |\n| `exists`, `infer`, `show`, `explain`, `list` | [Policy inspection](11-troubleshooting.md#inspect-from-a-policy) |\n| Feature gaps and reserved syntax | [Feature status](reference.md#feature-status) |\n| `for`, `each`, `every`, `foreach`, `while` | [Collections](05-collections.md) |\n| `if`, `elseif`, `else` | [Branches](04-control-flow.md#branch-on-a-value) |\n| Loading/reloading/unloading | [Operation](10-operation.md#give-deployed-sources-stable-identities) |\n| Membership / CIDR caveat | [Datasets](08-data.md#a-named-dataset) |\n| `nextevent`, `nextrule`, `with` | [Handoffs](04-control-flow.md#hand-work-to-another-event) |\n| Operator precedence | [Full table](reference.md#operator-precedence) |\n| Persistent state, reset, and recovery | [State ownership](02-execution-model.md#state-has-a-lifetime-and-an-owner), [recovery](10-operation.md#understand-recovery-boundaries) |\n| Query inputs / output | [Query contract](08-data.md#queries-name-a-computation) |\n| Schedules, NLD, time zones | [Time](07-time.md) |\n| Secrets and redaction | [Wrapped credentials](09-embedding.md#keep-credentials-wrapped) |\n| Signing and `signed_by` | [Signed source](10-operation.md#verify-signed-source) |\n| Tracing and explain API | [Runtime observation](11-troubleshooting.md#ask-what-the-runtime-holds) |\n| `unless`, `until`, run once | [Guards](04-control-flow.md#until-and-stopping-permanently) |\n| Watcher and signature sidecars | [Directory deployment](10-operation.md#deploy-a-directory) |\n\n[Contents](README.md)","ref":"glossary.html#subject-index"}],"proglang":"elixir","content_type":"text/markdown","producer":{"name":"ex_doc","version":"0.40.4"}}